/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"Overall equipment effectiveness","d":"The company, productivity and standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad#oee","x":"04 Overall equipment effectiveness Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE), born with total productive maintenance in Japan, measures what fraction of the planned time is converted into good parts at the ideal speed. It breaks down into three factors: \\[ OEE = D \\cdot R \\cdot C \\] Availability \\( D \\) discounts unplanned stops —failures, tool changes—; performance \\( R \\) compares what was produced with what would have been produced at the ideal cycle during the operating time, and discounts minor stops and reduced speed; quality \\( C \\) is the fraction of good parts. They are the six big losses grouped in pairs. Lab · overall effectiveness of a line A 480-minute shift with 30 minutes of planned stops and a machine with an ideal cycle of 30 s. The bars show how minutes are lost from the planned time down to the effective time of good parts. Unplanned stops = Parts produced = Defective parts ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The company, productivity and standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Organization chart Draw the organization chart of a well-known electronics company from its public information and classify its structure. Activity 2 · Productivity Measure the labor productivity of a board assembly in the laboratory before and after improving the method. Activity 3 · Overall effectiveness During a practice session, record the stops, parts and defects of a machine or a process, and calculate its overall effectiveness."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The company, productivity and standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing output with productivity. Producing more is not better if proportionally more is used. Looking at only one factor of overall effectiveness. A machine that is always available but slow, or that produces defects, yields little. Using the actual cycle as the ideal cycle. It hides the speed losses. Setting standards without a defined method. The standard time is valid for one method; if the method changes, it has to be measured again."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The company, productivity and standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment With the initial lab values, what are D, R and C? D = 405/450 = 0.90; R = 700 · 30 s / 405 min = 0.864; C = 680/700 = 0.971. And the overall effectiveness? 0.756: equivalent to 680 good parts at 30 s each, 340 minutes of the 450 planned. What advantage and what limit does a matrix structure have? It combines specialties in each project; in exchange, each person reports to two bosses. What is a standard time? The time a qualified worker needs, at a normal pace and with a defined method, plus allowances for rest and personal needs."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The company, productivity and standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading International Labour Organization (George Kanawaty, ed.). Introduction to Work Study. 4th ed., ILO, 1992. (Spanish edition: “Introducción al estudio del trabajo”, 4th ed., ILO, 1996.) Productivity and the study of methods and times, freely available. Seiichi Nakajima. Introduction to TPM: Total Productive Maintenance. Productivity Press, 1988. The origin of overall equipment effectiveness and its six big losses. Idalberto Chiavenato. Introducción a la teoría general de la administración. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) Organizational structures and their characteristics."},{"t":"A test anyone can repeat","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#que-es","x":"01 A test anyone can repeat A standards-based test is a measurement with a procedure fixed in advance: what is measured, with which instruments, under which environmental conditions, with which setup and by which criterion it is accepted. The goal is that two different laboratories, testing the same product, reach the same result. This is how a product is certified as safe, as not interfering with other equipment, and as withstanding the environment where it is going to operate. International standards are issued by the IEC (electrotechnology) and ISO (everything else), and in Argentina IRAM (the Argentine standards institute) adopts them and issues its own standards. Some are mandatory by regulation: low-voltage electrical products sold in the country must certify their safety under the regime of the Secretariat of Commerce, and radio equipment must obtain homologation from ENACOM (the national communications regulator)."},{"t":"The families of tests","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#familias","x":"02 The families of tests Family Reference standards What is verified Electrical safety IEC 61010 (measuring equipment), IEC 62368-1 (audio, video and IT), IEC 60335 (household appliances) Dielectric strength, insulation resistance, leakage current, ground continuity, temperatures Electromagnetic emissions CISPR 32, IEC 61000-3-2 (harmonics) That the equipment does not disturb others, by conduction and by radiation Electromagnetic immunity IEC 61000-4-2 (electrostatic discharge), -4-3 (radiated fields), -4-4 (bursts), -4-5 (surges) That the equipment keeps working under standardized disturbances Environmental IEC 60068-2 (temperature, humidity, vibration, shock) That it withstands transport and operating conditions Ingress protection IEC 60529 (IP code) Protection against solids and water In immunity tests the result is not a number but a behavior, classified into criteria: A, it operates normally during the test; B, it degrades temporarily and recovers on its own; C, it recovers with operator intervention. Each product standard sets which criterion it requires."},{"t":"How it is carried out and reported","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#ejecucion","x":"03 How it is carried out and reported A valid test requires calibrated instruments with traceability, the environmental conditions set by the standard, the exact setup it describes (distances, cables, ground plane) and a record of everything. The report, according to ISO/IEC 17025, identifies the item tested, the standard and its version, the method, the equipment, the conditions, the results with their uncertainty and, if applicable, the statement of conformity with the decision rule that was used. That rule is what connects the measurement uncertainty, seen in errors and uncertainty, with the decision to accept or reject."},{"t":"Deciding with uncertainty","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#decision","x":"04 Deciding with uncertainty If a power supply must give 5 V ± 1% and the laboratory measures 5.045 V with an expanded uncertainty of 10 mV, the true value may be between 5.035 and 5.055 V: outside the tolerance. Does it comply or not? Decision rules (ILAC-G8 guide, JCGM 106 document) resolve it in two ways: Simple acceptance. It is accepted if the measured value is within the tolerance. The risk of accepting something that does not comply is shared between the laboratory and the customer, and is acceptable only if the uncertainty is small compared with the tolerance. Guard band. The acceptance limits are shrunk by \\( U \\) on each side. Conformity is declared only if the measured value falls within them; between the guard band and the limit, neither one thing nor the other can be stated. \\[ TUR = \\frac{T}{U} \\] The ratio between the half-width of the tolerance \\( T \\) and the expanded uncertainty. Traditional practice asks for at least 4:1; with 10:1 the guard band hardly reduces the acceptance zone. Lab · does it meet the specification? A power supply specified at 5.00 V ± 1% (from 4.95 to 5.05 V). You choose the measured value and the expanded uncertainty (k = 2). The graph shows the tolerance, the acceptance zone with guard band and the probable distribution of the true value. U = 2 mV U = 5 mV U = 10 mV U = 20 mV Measured value ="},{"t":"Common safety tests","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#ejemplos","x":"05 Common safety tests Test How it is done What it protects Dielectric strength A high AC or DC voltage is applied between the mains and the accessible parts for a fixed time, without breakdown occurring That the insulation withstands overvoltages Insulation resistance Measured with 500 V DC The condition of the insulation Leakage current The current that would flow through a person touching the equipment is measured, with a network that imitates the human body Against electric shock Ground continuity A high current is passed through the protective conductor and its resistance is measured That the ground acts in the event of a fault The voltage, time and limit values are set by each product standard according to the equipment class and the overvoltage category."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Test plan Write the test plan for a laboratory power supply: variables, instruments with their uncertainty, conditions, acceptance criteria and decision rule. Activity 2 · Electrostatic discharge With an ESD simulator, apply 4 kV contact discharges to a device with a microcontroller according to IEC 61000-4-2 and classify its behavior under criteria A, B or C. Activity 3 · Report according to 17025 Write the report of a measurement made in another activity with all the elements the standard requires."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Declaring conformity without considering the uncertainty. Near the limit, the decision may be wrong. Citing a standard without its version. Requirements change between editions. Improvising the setup. In electromagnetic compatibility, a cable moved changes the result. Testing with instruments without a valid calibration. The result loses traceability and validity."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What TUR does a tolerance of ±0.5% on 10 V measured with U = 10 mV have? \\( 50\\ \\text{mV} / 10\\ \\text{mV} = 5 \\). With a guard band, up to what value is an upper tolerance of 5.05 V accepted with U = 10 mV? Up to 5.04 V. What does criterion B mean in an immunity test? That the equipment temporarily loses some function during the test and recovers on its own, without intervention. Which document defines the competence requirements for a testing laboratory? The ISO/IEC 17025 standard."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Standards-based testing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading JCGM. Evaluation of Measurement Data: The Role of Measurement Uncertainty in Conformity Assessment (JCGM 106:2012). BIPM, 2012. Decision rules with uncertainty and the calculation of consumer’s and producer’s risks. ILAC. ILAC-G8: Guidelines on Decision Rules and Statements of Conformity. ILAC, 2019. The guide used by accredited laboratories to declare conformity. Tim Williams. EMC for Product Designers. 5th ed., Newnes, 2017. The electromagnetic compatibility standards and how to test and design to meet them."},{"t":"A radio signal is a carrier with two pieces of data","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#pasabanda","x":"01 A radio signal is a carrier with two pieces of data Almost all communication signals are bandpass: they occupy a narrow band around a carrier of frequency \\( f_c \\) much greater than their bandwidth. An FM radio occupies 200 kHz around 100 MHz; a Wi-Fi channel, 20 MHz around 2.4 GHz. Any such signal can be written as a carrier whose amplitude and phase change slowly: \\[ x(t) = a(t)\\cos\\big(2\\pi f_c t + \\varphi(t)\\big) \\] \\[ x(t) = x_I(t)\\cos 2\\pi f_c t - x_Q(t)\\sin 2\\pi f_c t \\] \\( a(t) \\) is the envelope and \\( \\varphi(t) \\) the phase; \\( x_I \\) and \\( x_Q \\), the in-phase and quadrature components. All the information is in these slow functions; the carrier only shifts it in frequency."},{"t":"The complex envelope","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#envolvente","x":"02 The complex envelope The two components are combined into a single complex number that varies in time, the complex envelope: \\[ g(t) = x_I(t) + j\\,x_Q(t) = a(t)\\,e^{j\\varphi(t)} \\] \\[ x(t) = \\operatorname{Re}\\left\\{ g(t)\\, e^{j2\\pi f_c t} \\right\\} \\] The bandpass signal is the real part of the envelope multiplied by the complex carrier. In frequency, the spectrum of \\( x \\) is that of \\( g \\) shifted to \\( \\pm f_c \\). The practical consequence is enormous: everything that happens to a radio signal —modulation, filtering, noise, demodulation— can be analyzed and simulated with its complex envelope, at low frequencies, without dealing with the carrier. This is how simulators and software-defined receivers work."},{"t":"Every modulation is an envelope","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#modulaciones","x":"03 Every modulation is an envelope Modulation Complex envelope \\( g(t) \\) In the I/Q plane AM \\( A_c\\,[1 + m(t)] \\) Moves along the real axis DSB-SC \\( A_c\\, m(t) \\) Along the real axis, crossing the origin SSB \\( A_c\\,[m(t) + j\\,\\hat m(t)] \\) Rotates in one direction only PM \\( A_c\\, e^{j k_p m(t)} \\) On a circle FM \\( A_c\\, e^{j k_f \\int m\\, dt} \\) On a circle QAM and PSK \\( \\sum_k (a_k + j b_k)\\, p(t - kT) \\) Jumps between points of a constellation \\( \\hat m(t) \\) is the Hilbert transform of the message. Angle modulations have a constant envelope: \\( |g| \\) does not change, and the information is all in the phase. Lab · the complex envelope in the I/Q plane On the left, the trajectory of \\( g(t) \\) in the I/Q plane; on the right, a few cycles of the bandpass signal \\( x(t) \\) with its envelope. The message is a sinusoid (or random symbols, in QPSK). AM DSB-SC FM QPSK Index ="},{"t":"The I/Q receiver","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#receptor","x":"04 The I/Q receiver To recover the complex envelope, the receiver multiplies the signal by \\( \\cos 2\\pi f_c t \\) and by \\( -\\sin 2\\pi f_c t \\) and filters the two products with lowpass filters: it obtains \\( x_I/2 \\) and \\( x_Q/2 \\). This is the quadrature demodulator, and with two converters behind it, it is the front end of every modern digital receiver: once \\( g(t) \\) is in samples, the demodulation of any modulation is software. The converters at that input are those of AD and DA converters."},{"t":"Envelope and amplifiers","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#papr","x":"05 Envelope and amplifiers The ratio of peak power to average power (PAPR) of the envelope decides what amplifier is needed. With a constant envelope, like FM, a saturated class C amplifier will do, with an efficiency of 70 % or more. With a variable envelope, like AM or QAM, a linear amplifier operating below its maximum is needed: otherwise it clips the peaks, distorts and widens the spectrum. A 4G channel, with hundreds of subcarriers, has a PAPR of 10 dB or more, and its amplifier is one of the big energy consumers of a base station."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · A software-defined receiver With a low-cost SDR receiver, capture I/Q samples of an FM radio and plot their trajectory in the complex plane. Activity 2 · Quadrature demodulator Build an I/Q demodulator for a 455 kHz AM signal with two mixers and a 90° phase shifter, and view the two outputs on the oscilloscope in X-Y mode. Activity 3 · PAPR With an oscilloscope, measure the peak and average power of a 100 % AM signal and compare them with 4.26 dB."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting that the envelope is complex. A single component loses the phase. Confusing the real envelope with the complex one. The real one is the magnitude \\( |g| \\). Using a saturated amplifier with a variable-envelope modulation. It distorts and pollutes the neighboring channels. Forgetting the imbalance between I and Q. A gain or phase error between the two branches creates an image of the spectrum."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the complex envelope of \\( x(t) = 3\\cos(2\\pi f_c t + \\pi/4) \\)? \\( g = 3e^{j\\pi/4} \\), constant. What PAPR does an AM signal with index 1 and a sinusoidal message have? \\( 4/1.5 \\): 4.26 dB. Why can FM be amplified with a saturated amplifier? Because its envelope is constant: the information is in the phase, which saturation does not alter. What two multiplications does a quadrature demodulator perform? By \\( \\cos 2\\pi f_c t \\) and by \\( -\\sin 2\\pi f_c t \\), followed by lowpass filters."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The complex envelope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Leon W. Couch. Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 8th ed., Pearson, 2013. Uses the complex envelope as the central tool for all modulations. Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. The quadrature signal explained intuitively, with the I/Q receiver. Simon Haykin and Michael Moher. Communication Systems. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. The representation of bandpass signals and their spectrum."},{"t":"Water ionizes itself","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#agua","x":"01 Water ionizes itself Two water molecules exchange a proton, and everything else follows from that equilibrium. Its constant is small but not zero, and that is enough to fix the scale on which acidity is measured: \\[ \\mathrm{2H_2O \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+ + OH^-} \\qquad K_w = [\\mathrm{H^+}][\\mathrm{OH^-}] = 10^{-14} \\] At 25 °C. From this it follows that in pure water \\( [\\mathrm{H^+}] = 10^{-7} \\) and the pH is 7, and that \\( \\mathrm{pH} + \\mathrm{pOH} = 14 \\) in any aqueous solution. And from this also follows the resistivity limit of ultrapure water that appeared in the unit on solutions: those 10⁻⁷ moles per liter of each ion are what set the floor of 18.2 MΩ·cm. No purification can remove them, because the water itself makes them. 7 is not neutral at every temperature \\( K_w \\) grows with temperature: at 60 °C it is \\( 9.6\\times10^{-14} \\) and pure water has a pH of 6.5. It is still neutral —there are as many protons as hydroxide ions— but the number is no longer 7. Neutral means \\( [\\mathrm{H^+}] = [\\mathrm{OH^-}] \\), not “pH equal to seven.”"},{"t":"Strong, weak and the constant that separates them","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#fuerza","x":"02 Strong, weak and the constant that separates them A strong acid dissociates completely; a weak one reaches an equilibrium, and how much it dissociates is given by its \\( K_a \\). The scale is handled better in logarithms: \\[ \\mathrm{HA \\rightleftharpoons H^+ + A^-} \\qquad K_a = \\frac{[\\mathrm{H^+}][\\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\\mathrm{HA}]} \\qquad \\mathrm{p}K_a = -\\log K_a \\] Acid \\( K_a \\) \\( \\mathrm{p}K_a \\) In electronics Hydrochloric, HCl Strong — Cleaning of metal oxides, pickling Hydrofluoric, HF \\( 6.8\\times10^{-4} \\) 3.17 The only one that attacks SiO₂; weak, and yet extremely dangerous Acetic \\( 1.8\\times10^{-5} \\) 4.74 Aluminum etchant mixtures Ammonium ion, NH₄⁺ \\( 5.6\\times10^{-10} \\) 9.25 The ammonia pair in BOE Weak does not mean harmless Hydrofluoric acid is a weak acid and the most dangerous reagent in an electronics laboratory. Precisely because it is weak it circulates undissociated, passes through the skin without warning —at first it hardly hurts— and once inside the fluoride binds to the calcium of the bones. A splash on a small area can be fatal. The strength of an acid measures its dissociation equilibrium, not its toxicity."},{"t":"Titration and buffer solutions","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#titulacion","x":"03 Titration and buffer solutions Adding base to an acid a little at a time and following the pH draws a curve with three regions: a plateau at the beginning —the buffer region, where the pH hardly moves—, a sharp jump at the equivalence point and another plateau at the end. In a buffer solution the pH is set by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation: \\[ \\mathrm{pH} = \\mathrm{p}K_a + \\log\\frac{[\\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\\mathrm{HA}]} \\] When the two forms are present in equal amounts the logarithm vanishes and the pH is exactly \\( \\mathrm{p}K_a \\). That point is the center of the buffer region and the point of maximum buffering capacity. Lab · titration curve 25 mL of 0.1 M acid titrated with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide. The pH does not come from an approximate formula: the exact charge balance is solved for each point. Compare the jump of hydrochloric acid with that of ammonium. hydrochloric acid · strong hydrofluoric acid · pKₐ 3.17 acetic acid · pKₐ 4.74 ammonium ion · pKₐ 9.25 Hydroxide added = 0 mL BOE is a true buffer solution The buffered oxide etch —hydrofluoric acid with ammonium fluoride— is the standard etch of silicon oxide. Ammonium fluoride is the conjugate base of HF, so the mixture is exactly a buffer pair: it keeps constant the concentration of the etching species as it is consumed, and that is why the etch rate does not keep falling during the process. Unbuffered HF etches more and more slowly and the thickness removed stops being predictable."},{"t":"Solubility product","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#solubilidad","x":"04 Solubility product A sparingly soluble salt in contact with its saturated solution is also in equilibrium, and its constant is called the solubility product: \\[ \\mathrm{AgCl(s) \\rightleftharpoons Ag^+ + Cl^-} \\qquad K_{sp} = [\\mathrm{Ag^+}][\\mathrm{Cl^-}] = 1.8\\times10^{-10} \\] Comparing the product of the concentrations with \\( K_{sp} \\) tells you whether it will precipitate or not, just like \\( Q \\) against \\( K \\). And from this follows the common-ion effect: adding one of the two ions reduces the solubility of the other, which is the principle by which salts are purified by recrystallization. Salt \\( K_{sp} \\) Where it appears AgCl \\( 1.8\\times10^{-10} \\) Ag/AgCl reference electrodes CaF₂ \\( 3.9\\times10^{-11} \\) What forms when HF waste is neutralized with lime Cu(OH)₂ \\( 2.2\\times10^{-20} \\) Treatment of electroplating effluents PbSO₄ \\( 1.8\\times10^{-8} \\) What is deposited on the plates of a discharged battery The second row is the safety procedure for a hydrofluoric acid spill: it is neutralized with calcium carbonate or hydroxide, and the fluoride precipitates as calcium fluoride, which is insoluble and harmless. The last one explains why a lead battery that was left discharged for a long time does not recover: the precipitated sulfate crystallizes and stops responding."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Recording a titration curve Titrate 25 mL of 0.1 M acetic acid with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide, measuring the pH every milliliter. Plot it, locate the equivalence point by the derivative and read the \\( \\mathrm{p}K_a \\) at the half-equivalence point. Compare with the tabulated value. Activity 2 · Testing a buffer solution Prepare an acetate/acetic acid buffer and measure how much its pH changes when a drop of strong acid is added. Repeat on distilled water and compare the two shifts. Activity 3 · Precipitating and the common ion Form silver chloride by mixing dilute silver nitrate with sodium chloride. Then add excess chloride and observe. Calculate the solubility before and after with \\( K_{sp} \\), and compare with what you see."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that pH 7 is always neutral. It is at 25 °C; neutral means \\( [\\mathrm{H^+}] = [\\mathrm{OH^-}] \\). Confusing a weak acid with a dilute acid. Strength is the constant; concentration is how much there is. Assuming that the pH is 7 at the equivalence point. Only if the acid and the base are both strong; with a weak acid the salt hydrolyzes and the solution ends up basic. Believing that a weak acid is less dangerous. Hydrofluoric acid disproves that in the worst way. Using Henderson-Hasselbalch outside the buffer region. It is an approximation valid near \\( \\mathrm{p}K_a \\). Comparing \\( K_{sp} \\) between salts of different stoichiometry. The exponents are not the same and the numbers are not directly comparable."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the pH at the half-equivalence point? Exactly \\( \\mathrm{p}K_a \\): there is as much acid form as basic form and the Henderson-Hasselbalch logarithm vanishes. Why does the equivalence point of acetic acid with sodium hydroxide give a basic pH? Because what is left is sodium acetate, and acetate is a weak base that hydrolyzes. What is a buffer solution? A mixture of a weak acid with its conjugate base, which resists changes in pH when acid or base is added. What is ammonium fluoride for in BOE? It is the conjugate base of HF: it buffers the pH and keeps the oxide etch rate constant as the acid is consumed. How is a hydrofluoric acid spill neutralized? With a calcium compound: the fluoride precipitates as CaF₂, which is insoluble —\\( K_{sp} = 3.9\\times10^{-11} \\)— and harmless. What is the common-ion effect? That adding one of the ions of a sparingly soluble salt shifts the equilibrium and reduces its solubility."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Equilibrium in solutions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 15 and 16: acids and bases, pH, hydrolysis, buffers and solubility product. Daniel C. Harris. Quantitative Chemical Analysis. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Análisis químico cuantitativo”), Reverté, 2007. Titrations worked out with the exact charge balance, which is how the lab on this page calculates them. Werner Kern (ed.). Handbook of Semiconductor Wafer Cleaning Technology. Noyes Publications, 1993. BOE, etchant mixtures and the safe handling of hydrofluoric acid in industry."},{"t":"Reactions that come back","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#reversible","x":"01 Reactions that come back Many reactions do not go to completion: they reach a point at which the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate and the concentrations stop changing. That is chemical equilibrium, and it is dynamic: underneath everything keeps happening, but the net balance is zero. \\[ a\\mathrm{A} + b\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons c\\mathrm{C} + d\\mathrm{D} \\qquad K = \\frac{[\\mathrm{C}]^c[\\mathrm{D}]^d}{[\\mathrm{A}]^a[\\mathrm{B}]^b} \\] The equilibrium constant depends only on temperature. It is not changed by pressure, by the initial amounts, or by a catalyst. Pure solids and pure liquids do not appear in the expression: their activity is 1. The reaction quotient \\( Q \\) has the same form but is calculated with the concentrations at that moment. Comparing \\( Q \\) with \\( K \\) tells you which way the system will move: if \\( Q \\lt K \\), toward the products; if \\( Q \\gt K \\), toward the reactants; and if they are equal, it is already at equilibrium. A catalyst does not shift the equilibrium It speeds up the forward and reverse reactions in the same proportion, so the end point does not move: what changes is how long it takes to get there. That is why the iron catalyst of the Haber-Bosch process does not increase the theoretical yield; it makes it possible to reach it in a reasonable time at a temperature at which, without it, the reaction would be unworkable."},{"t":"Le Chatelier's principle","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#chatelier","x":"02 Le Chatelier's principle If a system at equilibrium is disturbed, the system shifts in the direction that partially counteracts the disturbance. There are three possible disturbances and each has its own rule: Disturbance The system responds Does \\( K \\) change? Adding a reactant By consuming it: it shifts toward products No Increasing the pressure Toward the side with fewer moles of gas No Increasing the temperature Toward the endothermic side Yes The third row is the only one that really changes \\( K \\); the other two move the concentrations within a \\( K \\) that keeps the same value. The dependence on temperature has its own equation: \\[ \\ln\\frac{K_2}{K_1} = -\\frac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}}{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{T_2} - \\frac{1}{T_1}\\right) \\] The van 't Hoff equation. If the reaction is exothermic, \\( \\Delta H \\lt 0 \\) and heating lowers \\( K \\): the yield gets worse. That conflict between kinetics and equilibrium is the heart of almost every industrial process. Lab · shifting the equilibrium Four gas-phase equilibria, chosen to cover the four possible cases. Move temperature and pressure and the composition is recalculated. Pay attention to the last one: it has the same number of moles on both sides. N₂ + 3 H₂ ⇌ 2 NH₃ · exothermic, fewer moles 2 SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2 SO₃ · exothermic, fewer moles N₂O₄ ⇌ 2 NO₂ · endothermic, more moles H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2 HI · equal number of moles Temperature = 700 K Total pressure = 1 atm"},{"t":"The trade-off in every industrial process","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#compromiso","x":"03 The trade-off in every industrial process Ammonia synthesis is exothermic and goes from four moles of gas to two. Le Chatelier then says that high pressure and low temperature are favorable. But at low temperature the reaction, although favorable, is so slow that it does not occur: kinetics rules over equilibrium. Haber and Bosch resolved the conflict from both sides: an iron catalyst so that the kinetics suffices at moderate temperature, about 200 atmospheres to push the equilibrium, and recycling of what did not react. Neither the thermodynamic optimum nor the kinetic one is chosen: what is chosen is the point where the product of the two yields the most per hour. The same tension, in electronics The growth of the gate oxide is exothermic, so thermodynamics would prefer to do it cold. But at room temperature the oxide grows two nanometers and stops. That is why thermal oxidation is done at 1000 °C, where the equilibrium is less favorable but the kinetics allows finishing in hours instead of centuries. It is exactly the same trade-off."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · The tube that changes color With a sealed tube of NO₂ in equilibrium with N₂O₄, immerse it alternately in hot water and in ice. Record the color and deduce the sign of \\( \\Delta H \\) from the direction in which the equilibrium shifts. Activity 2 · Le Chatelier by concentration On the iron(III) thiocyanate equilibrium, separately add iron(III) ion, thiocyanate and an agent that complexes iron. Predict with \\( Q \\) and \\( K \\) the direction of the shift before each addition, and compare with the color. Activity 3 · Measuring a constant Determine \\( K \\) of the thiocyanate equilibrium by colorimetry at two temperatures and obtain \\( \\Delta H^{\\circ} \\) with van 't Hoff. Discuss the uncertainty of calculating a slope with only two points."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that everything stops at equilibrium. It is dynamic: both reactions continue, at the same rate. Including pure solids and liquids in \\( K \\). Their activity is 1 and they do not appear. Thinking that pressure changes \\( K \\). It changes the concentrations, not the constant. Applying the pressure effect when \\( \\Delta n = 0 \\). If there is the same number of moles of gas on both sides, compressing shifts nothing. Expecting a catalyst to improve the yield. It improves the time, not the end point. Confusing a large \\( K \\) with a fast reaction. They are different things: one is equilibrium, the other kinetics."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does the equilibrium constant depend on? Only on temperature. Neither pressure, nor the initial amounts, nor a catalyst modifies it. Which way does an equilibrium shift when compressed? Toward the side with fewer moles of gas. If there is the same number on both sides, it does not shift. What happens to \\( K \\) of an exothermic reaction when heated? It decreases: the system shifts toward the endothermic side, which is the reactant side. What is the use of comparing \\( Q \\) with \\( K \\)? To know in which direction the system will evolve. If \\( Q \\lt K \\), toward products. Why does the Haber-Bosch process use 200 atmospheres? Because the reaction goes from four moles of gas to two, and pressure shifts the equilibrium toward ammonia. Why is it not carried out at low temperature, since it is exothermic? Because there the kinetics is impractically slow. The chosen point is a compromise between equilibrium and rate."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Chemical equilibrium","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapter 14: equilibrium constant, reaction quotient and Le Chatelier, with the Haber process worked out. Peter Atkins and Julio de Paula. Physical Chemistry. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química física”), Editorial Médica Panamericana, 2018. Equilibrium from the Gibbs free energy, which is where van 't Hoff comes from. Ralph H. Petrucci et al. General Chemistry. 11th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química general”), Pearson, 2017. Worked gas-phase equilibrium problems, with the ICE-table method."},{"t":"No measurement is exact","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#medir","x":"01 No measurement is exact To measure is to compare a quantity with a reference, and no comparison is perfect. The value an instrument gives differs from the true one because of the instrument, the method, the environment and the person measuring. What distinguishes an engineering measurement is not that it has no error, but that it says how much it may be wrong: a result without its uncertainty does not allow any decision. “5.00 V” does not say whether the supply meets a specification of 5 V ± 1%; “5.003 V ± 0.007 V” does. Metrology has a precise vocabulary, fixed in the International Vocabulary of Metrology (VIM): Term What it means Measurand The quantity to be measured, defined with all its conditions Trueness Closeness of the average of many measurements to the true value: it is affected by systematic error Precision Closeness of the measurements to each other: it is affected by random error Accuracy Closeness of a measurement to the true value: it combines the two previous ones Resolution The smallest change the instrument can display; it is not accuracy How to use the instruments is covered in the technician program, in digital instruments and analog instruments."},{"t":"Systematic and random errors","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#errores","x":"02 Systematic and random errors A systematic error repeats identically in every measurement: a shifted calibration, the loading effect of the voltmeter, the resistance of the leads. Averaging does not reduce it; it has to be known and corrected. A random error changes from one measurement to the next: noise, fluctuations, the reading of the last digit. Averaging \\( n \\) measurements reduces its effect by \\( \\sqrt{n} \\). Gross errors, such as writing down a wrong value or using the wrong range, are not handled with statistics: they are avoided. When the result is calculated from several measured quantities, the errors propagate. For a product or a quotient, such as \\( P = V \\cdot I \\) or \\( R = V / I \\), in the worst case the relative errors add: if \\( V \\) has ±0.5% and \\( I \\) ±1%, the power has ±1.5%."},{"t":"Uncertainty according to the GUM","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#gum","x":"03 Uncertainty according to the GUM The Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement (GUM) is the method accepted worldwide. Each source of uncertainty is expressed as a standard deviation, the standard uncertainty \\( u \\), and is evaluated in one of two ways: \\[ u_A = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\] \\[ u_B = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}} \\] Type A: by statistics of \\( n \\) repeated readings, with \\( s \\) their standard deviation. Type B: from any other information, such as the manufacturer’s specification. If all that is known is that the error lies between \\( -a \\) and \\( +a \\), with any value equally likely, the distribution is rectangular and it is divided by \\( \\sqrt{3} \\). \\[ u_c = \\sqrt{\\sum_i \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i}\\right)^2 u_i^2} \\] \\[ U = k \\cdot u_c \\] Independent uncertainties are combined in quadrature, weighted by the sensitivity of the result to each input. The expanded uncertainty \\( U \\) multiplies by a coverage factor, usually \\( k = 2 \\), which gives an interval with about 95% probability. The result is reported as \\( y \\pm U \\), with the unit and the value of \\( k \\), and with the uncertainty rounded to one or two significant figures: “5.0003 V ± 0.0066 V (k = 2)”."},{"t":"How to read an instrument’s accuracy","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#especificaciones","x":"04 How to read an instrument’s accuracy Digital multimeters specify accuracy as \\( \\pm(\\% \\text{ of reading} + \\text{counts}) \\) or \\( \\pm(\\% \\text{ of reading} + \\% \\text{ of range}) \\). The fixed term weighs more the smaller the reading is compared with the range: that is why the lowest range that does not overflow is always preferable. Analog instruments specify a class: the maximum error as a percentage of full scale, so a reading at one third of the scale has three times the relative error. The specification holds under certain conditions: a temperature range (typically 23 °C ± 5 °C), a time since the last calibration (one or two years) and, in AC, a frequency range. Outside them, additional coefficients are added. Lab · the uncertainty budget of a reading A 4½-digit multimeter (19,999 counts) with accuracy ±(0.05% of reading + 3 counts) measures a DC voltage with noise of about 2 counts. \\( n \\) readings are taken; the dots are the readings, the line is the average and the band is the interval \\( \\pm U \\). 2 V range (resolution 0.1 mV) 20 V range (resolution 1 mV) 200 V range (resolution 10 mV) 5 readings 10 readings 30 readings Measured voltage ="},{"t":"Traceability and standards","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#normativa","x":"05 Traceability and standards A measurement is traceable when it can be linked to the International System of Units by an unbroken chain of calibrations, each with its uncertainty. The laboratory multimeter is calibrated against a calibrator, which is calibrated against a standard from an accredited laboratory, which is calibrated against the national standard. In Argentina, the national standards are maintained by INTI (the National Institute of Industrial Technology), and Law 19,511 on Metrology establishes the legal system of units, the SIMELA. Document What it establishes JCGM 200 (VIM) The vocabulary of metrology JCGM 100 (GUM) The method for evaluating and expressing uncertainty ISO/IEC 17025 The requirements for a testing or calibration laboratory to be competent IEC 61010 The safety of measuring instruments, with their overvoltage categories"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Budget of a resistor Measure a 1 kΩ resistor ten times with a multimeter, calculate the Type A uncertainty and the Type B one from the specification, and express the result with its expanded uncertainty. Activity 2 · Two instruments, one value Measure the same voltage with a 3½-digit multimeter and a 6½-digit one, calculate the two uncertainties and check whether the results are compatible. Activity 3 · Propagation Measure voltage and current in a resistor, calculate the power and its combined uncertainty with the propagation law."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#errores-frecuentes","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing resolution with accuracy. Six digits on the display do not guarantee six correct digits. Measuring on a range that is too high. The counts or range term grows in proportion. Reporting the uncertainty with many digits. One or two are enough; the value is rounded to the same decimal position. Believing that averaging fixes everything. It only reduces the random part; the calibration error remains."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What Type B uncertainty does a specification of ±0.1 V with a rectangular distribution give? \\( 0.1 / \\sqrt{3} \\approx 0.058 \\) V. Ten readings have a standard deviation of 3 mV. What is \\( u_A \\)? \\( 3 / \\sqrt{10} \\approx 0.95 \\) mV. What is the maximum error of a class 1 voltmeter with a full scale of 10 V when reading 3 V? ±0.1 V, which is 3.3% of the reading. What does it mean for a measurement to be traceable? That it can be related to the SI standards by a documented chain of calibrations, each with its uncertainty."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading JCGM. Evaluation of Measurement Data: Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement (JCGM 100:2008). BIPM, 2008. The GUM itself, freely available on the BIPM website. Les Kirkup and Robert B. Frenkel. An Introduction to Uncertainty in Measurement. Cambridge University Press, 2006. The GUM explained with examples, for those applying it for the first time. John P. Bentley. Principles of Measurement Systems. 4th ed., Pearson, 2005. Measurement systems from the sensor to the display, with their errors."},{"t":"The same structure in very different things","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#estructura","x":"01 The same structure in very different things Arrows in the plane can be added and multiplied by numbers. So can 2 × 3 matrices. And polynomials of degree at most 3. And voltage signals as functions of time. In all four cases the same rules hold, so it makes sense to study them once and for all: that is a vector space. Formally, a set V with an addition and a multiplication by scalars is a vector space if the addition is commutative and associative, has an identity element (the zero vector) and additive inverses, and the multiplication by scalars is associative, has 1 as its identity and distributes over both additions. That makes eight axioms, and all of them hold in the examples above. Why abstraction pays off Everything that is proved from the eight axioms automatically holds for arrows, matrices, polynomials and signals. When it is later said that a periodic signal is a linear combination of sines and cosines —the Fourier series—, exactly this language is being used: a basis of a function space. Space Its “vectors” Dimension \\( \\mathbb{R}^2 \\), \\( \\mathbb{R}^3 \\), \\( \\mathbb{R}^n \\) Pairs, triples, n-tuples of numbers 2 · 3 · n \\( M_{2 \\times 3} \\) Matrices with 2 rows and 3 columns 6 P 3 Polynomials of degree \\( \\le 3 \\) 4 C[a, b] Continuous functions on an interval Infinite Solutions of \\( A \\cdot \\vec{x} = \\vec{0} \\) The vectors that A sends to zero n − rank(A)"},{"t":"Subspaces","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#subespacios","x":"02 Subspaces A subspace is a subset that, with the same operations, is again a vector space. You do not need to verify the eight axioms: three conditions are enough. Subspace criterion S ⊆ V is a subspace if it contains the zero vector, if the sum of two elements of S stays in S and if the scalar multiple of an element of S stays in S. That is why a line through the origin is a subspace of R², and one that does not pass through the origin is not: it does not contain the zero vector. The same goes for planes in R³. The most important example for engineering: the solution set of a homogeneous system A·x = 0 is always a subspace, called the kernel or null space of A. Its dimension is n − rank(A), and that is exactly the number of free parameters that appeared in systems of linear equations."},{"t":"Linear combinations and spanning sets","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#generadores","x":"03 Linear combinations and spanning sets A linear combination of v₁, …, v k is any expression a₁v₁ + a₂v₂ + … + a k v k. The set of all possible linear combinations is called the span and is written span{v₁, …, v k }: it is always a subspace, the smallest one that contains those vectors. In R², a nonzero vector spans a line; two non-parallel vectors span the whole plane; three vectors span the plane just the same, but one of them is superfluous."},{"t":"Linear dependence and independence","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#independencia","x":"04 Linear dependence and independence \\[ a_1 v_1 + a_2 v_2 + \\cdots + a_k v_k = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad a_1 = a_2 = \\cdots = a_k = 0 \\] If the only linear combination that gives the zero vector is the one with all coefficients zero, the vectors are linearly independent. If there is another, they are dependent. Said without formulas: they are dependent when one of them can be written in terms of the others, that is, when there is surplus information. To decide, build the matrix with the vectors as rows and compute the rank: if it equals the number of vectors, they are independent. With n vectors of R n the determinant is enough: independent if and only if it is nonzero."},{"t":"Basis and dimension","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#base","x":"05 Basis and dimension A basis is a set of vectors that is both spanning and independent: just what is needed and no more. All the bases of a given space have the same number of elements, and that number is the dimension. The standard basis of R³ is {(1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1)}, that is {î, ĵ, k̂}. That of P₃ is {1, x, x², x³}: four elements, dimension 4. In a space of dimension n, any set of n independent vectors is a basis, and any set of more than n vectors is necessarily dependent. The existence of a basis is what makes it possible to work with coordinates: once a basis is fixed, each vector is written in exactly one way as a linear combination of it, and that list of coefficients identifies it. Lab · basis, coordinates and dependence Drag the two basis vectors and the target point. The oblique grid is the one generated by that basis. Standard basis Make them dependent Basis rotated 30°"},{"t":"Change of basis","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#cambio-base","x":"06 Change of basis The coordinates of a vector depend on the chosen basis: the vector is the same, what changes is how it is measured. If B = {b₁, b₂} is a basis of R² and P is the matrix that has b₁ and b₂ as columns, then: \\[ X_{\\text{standard}} = P \\cdot X_B \\quad X_B = P^{-1} \\cdot X_{\\text{standard}} \\] P is always invertible, because its columns are a basis: they are independent and its determinant is not zero. Changing basis is the key step of many methods: choosing the “right” basis turns a tangled problem into a simple one. The diagonalization in eigenvalues and eigenvectors is precisely that: looking for the basis in which a transformation reduces to stretching each axis."},{"t":"Orthogonal bases and signals","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#producto-interno","x":"07 Orthogonal bases and signals When the space has an inner product defined —the generalization of the dot product— you can talk about magnitudes, angles and perpendicularity. An orthogonal basis is one in which all the vectors are perpendicular to each other, and it is far more convenient: each coordinate is computed separately, by projecting, without solving any system. Fourier is a change of basis In the space of periodic signals, the inner product of two signals is the integral of their product over one period. With that definition, the sines and cosines of the different harmonics turn out to be mutually orthogonal and form a basis. The Fourier coefficients of a signal are its coordinates in that basis, and that is why they are computed one by one with an integral: it is exactly the projection. The spectrum shown by an analyzer is nothing other than that list of coordinates. It is covered in signals and Fourier and in the instrument simulator."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Exercise 1 · Deciding whether it is a subspace For each set, decide whether it is a subspace of R³ and justify it with the criterion: the vectors with x + y + z = 0; those satisfying x + y + z = 1; those with a positive first component; the solutions of a homogeneous system of your own choosing. Exercise 2 · Basis of the solution space Solve a 3 × 4 homogeneous system, write the general solution with parameters and extract from it a basis of the solution space. Verify that its dimension is 4 − rank(A). Exercise 3 · Coordinates in another basis With B = {(1, 1), (1, −1)}, find the coordinates of (5, 1) by solving the system and verify with P −1. Check the result with the lab in section 5."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Calling a line that does not pass through the origin a subspace. Without the zero vector, it is not one. Confusing a spanning set with a basis: a spanning set can have surplus vectors. Believing that four vectors of R³ can be independent. In dimension 3, four are always dependent. Forgetting that coordinates depend on the basis and comparing coordinates from different bases. Building the change-of-basis matrix with the vectors as rows instead of columns."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Is the set of (x, y) with y = 2x a subspace of R²? Yes: it contains (0,0), the sum of two of its elements satisfies the condition again and so does the scalar multiple. It is the line through the origin with slope 2. Are (1, 2), (2, 4) and (0, 1) independent? No: the second is twice the first. Besides, three vectors of R² can never be independent. What is the dimension of the space of symmetric 3 × 3 matrices? 6: the three on the diagonal plus the three above it, because those below are determined by symmetry. If A is 5 × 7 with rank 4, what is the dimension of the solution space of A·x = 0? 7 − 4 = 3. Coordinates of (5, 1) in the basis {(1, 1), (1, −1)}. a(1,1) + b(1,−1) = (5,1) gives a + b = 5 and a − b = 1: a = 3 and b = 2. The coordinates are (3, 2). Why is Fourier said to be a change of basis? Because it writes a signal as a linear combination of an orthogonal basis of sines and cosines; its coefficients are the coordinates of the signal in that basis."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Vector spaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Stanley I. Grossman. Álgebra lineal. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). Spaces and subspaces, with the examples of matrices, polynomials and functions well developed. David C. Lay. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Pearson, 2016. Very clear on basis, dimension, coordinates and change of basis. Gilbert Strang. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Cengage Learning, 2007. The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix, and the connection with Fourier."},{"t":"What is plotted when you plot a spectrum","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#que","x":"01 What is plotted when you plot a spectrum The transform returns one complex number for each frequency, so two plots are needed to show it in full: the magnitude plot and the phase plot. \\[ X(j\\omega) = |X(j\\omega)|\\,e^{\\,j\\angle X(j\\omega)} \\] In practice you almost always look at the magnitude plot, and for one reason: the ear and most detectors are insensitive to phase. But the phase is there, and it is what holds the information about when: two signals with identical magnitude spectra can be completely different in time if their phases differ. Type of signal Its spectrum Units Periodic Lines at \\( k\\omega_0 \\) Volts: it is an amplitude Isolated, finite energy Continuous V/Hz: it is a density Noise, random signal Power spectral density V²/Hz The difference between amplitude and density matters more than it seems. The spectrum of an isolated signal is not measured in volts but in volts per hertz, and its value at a point means nothing by itself: what makes sense is the integral over a band. That is why the noise of an amplifier is specified in nV/√Hz, and you have to multiply it by the square root of the bandwidth to obtain volts."},{"t":"Two-sided and one-sided","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#unilateral","x":"02 Two-sided and one-sided The transform gives positive and negative frequencies. For a real signal, the Hermitian symmetry means that the negative half is determined by the positive half, so instruments show only one half. This calls for an adjustment: \\[ A_{\\text{unilateral}}(n\\omega_0) = 2\\,|a_n| \\quad\\text{for } n \\ge 1, \\qquad A(0) = |a_0| \\] Each positive line also takes on what its negative twin contributed. The DC component, which has no twin, stays the same. This is the origin of a classic mistake: computing the complex coefficients, keeping those with \\( n \\ge 1 \\) and forgetting the factor 2. The result is half of what the analyzer shows."},{"t":"What the analyzer does with a finite window","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#lab","x":"03 What the analyzer does with a finite window A real spectrum analyzer cannot observe the signal from \\( -\\infty \\) to \\( +\\infty \\): it takes a piece of finite duration. And taking a piece is multiplying by a rectangular pulse, which by the multiplication property is equivalent to convolving the spectrum with a sinc. The consequence is called spectral leakage: a pure tone, which should give a single line, appears smeared over the neighboring frequencies. Unless the window contains an integer number of cycles, in which case the zeros of the sinc fall exactly on the other lines and nothing odd shows up. Lab · spectral leakage and windows A tone observed over a finite window. Move the frequency and watch what happens when an integer number of cycles no longer fits; then try the windows and compare them. rectangular window · none Hann Hamming Blackman Flat-top Decibel scale Cycles in the window = 16.0"},{"t":"Choosing the window is choosing what you lose","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#ventanas","x":"04 Choosing the window is choosing what you lose All windows do the same thing: instead of cutting the signal off abruptly, they fade it out smoothly at the edges. That reduces leakage, but it widens the main lobe, which is the same as saying that it worsens the frequency resolution. There is no good window: there are windows suited to each purpose. Window Sidelobes Main lobe width What it is for Rectangular −13 dB The narrowest Separating two similar, strong tones Hann −31 dB Double General use, it is the default Hamming −43 dB Double When the first sidelobes are a nuisance Blackman −58 dB Triple Seeing a weak signal next to a strong one Flat-top −90 dB Very wide Measuring amplitudes: its level error is minimal The last row deserves an explanation. When a frequency falls between two lines of the analysis, the measured amplitude ends up below the real one: with a rectangular window the error reaches 3.9 dB, and with Hann 1.4 dB. The flat-top is designed so that this error is a few hundredths of a dB, at the cost of terrible resolution. That is why, on an analyzer, you use flat-top to measure the level of a tone, and another window to separate nearby tones. Resolution is set by the observation time Two tones can be told apart only if they are separated by more than \\( 1/T \\), where \\( T \\) is the duration of the window. To resolve 1 Hz you have to observe for 1 second, and no window or algorithm can avoid that: it is the same trade-off between duration and bandwidth as always."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Causing leakage Using the FFT of an oscilloscope, capture a sinusoid whose frequency is an exact multiple of the resolution and then shift it by half a line. Compare the two spectra and measure how many decibels appear at the sides. Activity 2 · Window versus resolution Generate two tones 50 Hz apart with equal amplitudes, and then with one 40 dB lower than the other. Find which window lets you see both in each case and explain why it is not the same one. Activity 3 · Measuring a level properly Measure the amplitude of a tone with a rectangular window, Hann and flat-top, for a frequency that falls between two lines. Compare the three values with the one shown by the true-RMS voltmeter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the factor 2 when going to one-sided. It gives half of the real amplitude. Reading a spectral density as if it were an amplitude. V/Hz are not volts; you have to integrate over the band. Blaming the instrument for leakage. It is a consequence of observing for a finite time, not a defect. Using the default window to measure amplitudes. That is what the flat-top is for. Expecting more resolution than the observation time allows. The limit is \\( 1/T \\)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Why are two plots needed for a spectrum? Because the transform is complex: one for the magnitude and one for the phase. What causes spectral leakage? Observing for a finite time, which is equivalent to multiplying by a pulse and convolving the spectrum with a sinc. When is there no leakage with a rectangular window? When an integer number of cycles fits in the window: the zeros of the sinc fall on the other lines. What do you gain and what do you lose with a window? You gain low sidelobes and lose resolution: the main lobe widens. Which window is best for measuring the level of a tone? The flat-top: its amplitude error is minimal, even though its resolution is poor. How long do you have to observe to resolve 1 Hz? One second. The resolution is \\( 1/T \\), and there is no way around it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Ronald W. Schafer. Discrete-Time Signal Processing. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento de señales en tiempo discreto”), Prentice Hall, 2010. The chapter on spectral analysis with windows, with the figures for each one. Fredric J. Harris. On the Use of Windows for Harmonic Analysis with the DFT. Proceedings of the IEEE, vol. 66, no. 1, 1978. The reference paper on windows. All the tables that are still in use come from here. Keysight Technologies. Spectrum Analysis Basics · Application Note 150. Keysight, current edition. How a real analyzer works: resolution bandwidth, sweep time and detectors."},{"t":"When a feedback amplifier oscillates","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#criterio","x":"01 When a feedback amplifier oscillates The denominator \\( 1 + T(s) \\) vanishes if at some frequency the loop gain is exactly −1: magnitude 1 and phase −180°. A signal of that frequency goes around the loop and returns unchanged, with no excitation at all. The Nyquist criterion gives the complete condition; for everyday amplifiers, with a loop gain that decreases with frequency, the margins seen in the previous unit are enough: \\[ \\mathrm{PM} = 180° + \\angle T(j\\omega_c), \\quad |T(j\\omega_c)| = 1 \\] \\[ \\mathrm{GM} = -20 \\log_{10} |T(j\\omega_{180})| \\] The phase margin PM is measured at the crossover frequency; the gain margin GM, at the frequency where the phase reaches −180°. Both have to be positive; in practice PM ≥ 45° and GM ≥ 10 dB are required."},{"t":"Compensation","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#compensacion","x":"02 Compensation To compensate is to modify the gain without feedback so that \\( |T| \\) crosses 1 before the phase approaches −180°. There are two ways with a single capacitor: Method What it does Cost Added dominant pole A new RC network adds a very low pole The bandwidth falls to almost nothing Shifting the first pole A large capacitor at the node of the first pole lowers it Much smaller: the original pole stops adding phase Miller (pole splitting) A capacitor between the input and output of the gain stage It lowers the first pole and also raises the second Miller compensation is that of almost all integrated op amps: the Miller effect that in frequency response was a problem is used to advantage. A capacitor of a few picofarads, multiplied by the gain of the second stage, creates the dominant pole, and the local feedback it introduces pushes the second pole out to higher frequencies. Lab · compensating a three-pole amplifier The amplifier of the previous unit: gain \\( 10^5 \\) and poles at 1 kHz, 1 MHz and 10 MHz. It is compensated by adding a pole at \\( f_d \\) or by lowering the 1 kHz one down to \\( f_d \\). The gray curve is the original gain; the blue one, the compensated one; the green one, the feedback one, and the dotted yellow one, \\( 1/\\beta \\). Lower the first pole to f d Add a new pole at f d Follower (gain 1) Gain 10 Gain 100 Dominant pole f d ="},{"t":"Slew rate","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#slew","x":"03 Slew rate Compensation has another consequence. In an op amp with Miller compensation, the maximum current that the input stage can deliver to the capacitor \\( C_c \\) limits the speed at which the output can change: \\[ SR = \\frac{I_{\\max}}{C_c} \\] \\[ f_{\\max} = \\frac{SR}{2\\pi V_p} \\] \\( f_{\\max} \\) is the full-power bandwidth: the highest frequency at which a sine wave of amplitude \\( V_p \\) is obtained without deforming it. With 0.5 V/μs, typical of a 741, a 10 V peak output is deformed above 8 kHz, even though the small-signal bandwidth is 1 MHz. Above \\( f_{\\max} \\) the sine wave becomes a triangle. It is a large-signal limitation, which does not appear in the small-signal analysis: an amplifier can have bandwidth to spare and distort all the same."},{"t":"Low-frequency oscillators","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#osciladores","x":"04 Low-frequency oscillators An oscillator is an amplifier that purposely meets the oscillation condition at a single frequency: the Barkhausen criterion, \\( T(j\\omega_0) = 1 \\). In the Wien bridge, a series–parallel RC network has zero phase and a transfer of 1/3 at one frequency, and the amplifier provides a gain of 3: \\[ f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi RC} \\] \\[ A = 1 + \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = 3 \\] With a gain of exactly 3 the amplitude is indeterminate; with a little more, it grows until it saturates and distorts. Quality oscillators control the gain with an element that depends on amplitude: a lamp, a thermistor or a JFET with a detector. The phase-shift oscillator uses three RC sections that add up to 180° and an inverter with a gain of 29. The audio-frequency oscillators in laboratories are Wien-bridge types: the first company of Hewlett and Packard was born with one, stabilized with a lamp. The introduction with the equipment is in oscillators, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Slew rate Apply a large square wave to a follower and measure the slope of the output. With a 10 V peak sine wave, raise the frequency until the triangle appears and compare with \\( f_{\\max} \\). Activity 2 · Wien bridge Build a Wien-bridge oscillator with diode stabilization and measure the frequency and the distortion. Change the gain and observe the start-up. Activity 3 · External compensation With an uncompensated op amp, such as those that have pins for the compensation capacitor, measure the overshoot as a follower with different capacitors."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Compensating for high gain and using the amplifier as a follower. Compensation is designed for the lowest gain of use. Confusing small-signal bandwidth with full-power bandwidth. The latter is set by the slew rate and depends on amplitude. Setting the Wien gain at exactly 3. The oscillator does not start or dies out; a margin and an amplitude control are needed. Forgetting parasitic capacitances. A long cable at the output adds a pole and eats into the phase margin."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What full-power bandwidth does a 13 V/μs op amp have at 10 V peak? \\( 13/(2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\) MHz ≈ 207 kHz. A Wien bridge uses R = 10 kΩ and C = 10 nF. At what frequency does it oscillate? \\( 1/(2\\pi \\cdot 10^{-4}) \\approx 1.59 \\) kHz. Why does shifting the first pole give more bandwidth than adding a new pole? Because the original 1 kHz pole stops adding phase: the crossover can be near the second pole instead of below the first. If I max = 20 μA and C c = 30 pF, what is the slew rate? 0.67 V/μs."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. Miller compensation, pole splitting and the slew rate of the two-stage op amp. Sergio Franco. Design with Operational Amplifiers and Analog Integrated Circuits. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2015. Stability with capacitive loads and oscillators with op amps. Jerald G. Graeme. Optimizing Op Amp Performance. McGraw-Hill, 1997. Stability and compensation of op amp circuits, analyzed with the loop gain."},{"t":"What a stable system is","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#definicion","x":"01 What a stable system is A linear system is stable if every bounded input produces a bounded output. For a system described by a rational transfer function, that happens if and only if all its poles have negative real part: each pole contributes to the response an exponential \\( e^{p t} \\), which decays only if \\( \\mathrm{Re}(p) stability, in Signals and Systems. In control, the problem is that feedback moves the poles: a stable plant can become unstable when the loop is closed with too much gain, and an unstable one can be stabilized. \\[ T(s) = \\frac{G(s)}{1 + G(s)} \\] \\[ 1 + G(s) = 0 \\] The closed-loop poles are the roots of the characteristic equation \\( 1 + G(s) = 0 \\). With \\( G = N/D \\), they are the roots of \\( D(s) + N(s) \\)."},{"t":"The Routh–Hurwitz criterion","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#routh","x":"02 The Routh–Hurwitz criterion Computing the roots of a high-degree polynomial was impractical by hand. In 1877, Edward Routh found a procedure to tell how many of them have positive real part without computing them. With the polynomial \\( a_n s^n + a_{n-1} s^{n-1} + \\dots + a_0 \\), a table is built: the first two rows with the alternating coefficients, and each following row from the two before it: \\[ b_1 = \\frac{a_{n-1} a_{n-2} - a_n a_{n-3}}{a_{n-1}} \\] The number of sign changes in the first column is the number of roots with positive real part. The system is stable if all the elements of the first column have the same sign. A necessary, but not sufficient, condition is that all the coefficients exist and have the same sign. For a loop with \\( G(s) = K/(s(s+a)(s+b)) \\), the characteristic equation is \\( s^3 + (a+b)s^2 + ab\\,s + K = 0 \\), and the table gives the gain limit: \\[ K_{crit} = ab\\,(a + b) \\] \\[ \\omega_{osc} = \\sqrt{ab} \\] With \\( K = K_{crit} \\) the third row vanishes: there is a pair of roots on the imaginary axis, and the system oscillates at \\( \\omega_{osc} \\), which comes from the previous row. It is the starting point of the Ziegler–Nichols tuning method. Lab · Routh table and roots The loop \\( K/(s(s+a)(s+b)) \\) with unity feedback. On the left, the first column of the Routh table; on the right, the roots of the characteristic equation in the s-plane, computed numerically. a = b = Gain K = There are two special cases: if an element of the first column vani"},{"t":"Relative stability","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#relativa","x":"03 Relative stability Knowing that a system is stable is not enough: a loop with poles barely to the left of the imaginary axis oscillates heavily and any change in parameters makes it unstable. Relative stability measures how far away it is: with the damping of the dominant poles, with the gain margin (how much K can be multiplied before reaching K crit ) or with the margins of the frequency response. Routh's criterion can be used for this by shifting the axis: replacing \\( s \\) with \\( s - \\sigma \\) tells whether all the roots are to the left of \\( -\\sigma \\)."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Critical gain Build a third-order loop with op amps and adjustable gain, raise the gain until it oscillates and compare the gain and the oscillation frequency with those from Routh. Activity 2 · Routh in Python Program the Routh table for any polynomial, with the special cases, and check it against the roots computed numerically. Activity 3 · Gain margin With the lab loop, measure the gain margin as the ratio between the critical gain and the working gain."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Looking at the stability of the plant instead of the closed loop. Feedback moves the poles. Settling for the positive-coefficients condition. It is necessary but not sufficient from third order up. Forgetting the special cases of the table. A zero in the first column does not mean stability. Designing just below K crit. The system is stable, but it oscillates heavily and does not tolerate changes."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the critical gain of K/(s(s+2)(s+3))? \\( 6 \\cdot 5 = 30 \\), with oscillation at \\( \\sqrt 6 \\approx 2.45 \\) rad/s. How many roots with positive real part are there if the first column is 1, 4, −2, 5? Two: there are two sign changes. Is s³ + s² + 2s + 8 stable? No: the third row gives \\( (2 - 8)/1 = -6 \\), two sign changes. What gain margin does the loop of the first question have with K = 10? 3, that is, 9.5 dB."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Stability of control systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). Routh's criterion with its special cases and relative stability. Richard C. Dorf and Robert H. Bishop. Modern Control Systems. 10th ed., Pearson, 2005 (Spanish edition: “Sistemas de control moderno”). The stability of feedback systems with design examples. Edward J. Routh. A Treatise on the Stability of a Given State of Motion. Macmillan, 1877. The original essay, awarded a prize by the University of Cambridge."},{"t":"Bounded input, bounded output","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#bibo","x":"01 Bounded input, bounded output The stability criterion used in signals has a name of its own: BIBO, for bounded input, bounded output. A system is stable if every bounded input produces a bounded output: \\[ |x(t)| \\le M \\lt \\infty \\;\\;\\text{for all } t \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; |y(t)| \\le K \\lt \\infty \\;\\;\\text{for all } t \\] The key word is every. Finding a single bounded input that makes the output grow is enough for the system to be unstable. That last point is what makes verification delicate: a system behaving well with the usual test signals proves nothing. The condition has to hold for any input, and there is always one that is worse than the others."},{"t":"The condition on the impulse response","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#condicion","x":"02 The condition on the impulse response For an LTI system, everything boils down to an integral. The output is \\( y(t) = \\int h(\\tau)x(t-\\tau)d\\tau \\), and bounding in absolute value: \\[ |y(t)| \\le \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |h(\\tau)|\\,|x(t-\\tau)|\\,d\\tau \\le M \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |h(\\tau)|\\,d\\tau \\] So if that integral is finite, the output is bounded. And the converse also holds, because the input that forces equality can always be constructed. The conclusion is clean: \\[ \\text{BIBO stable} \\iff \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |h(t)|\\,dt \\lt \\infty \\qquad\\text{or, in discrete time,}\\qquad \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} |h[n]| \\lt \\infty \\] It is said that \\( h \\) has to be absolutely integrable, or absolutely summable. The worst input for it What is the bounded signal that maximizes the output at an instant \\( T \\)? The one that at every moment has the same sign as the \\( h \\) that will multiply it: \\( x(t) = \\operatorname{sgn} \\big(h(T-t)\\big) \\). With that input, all the contributions to the integral add up instead of canceling, and the result is exactly \\( y(T) = \\int |h| \\). If that integral diverges, the output goes to infinity with an input that never exceeded 1 in absolute value. The lab constructs that input and applies it."},{"t":"Watching the integral converge, or not","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#lab","x":"03 Watching the integral converge, or not Lab · the integral of |h| decides Above, the impulse response and the worst-case input that corresponds to it. Below, the cumulative integral of \\( |h| \\), which is exactly the output that input produces. If the curve flattens out, the system is stable; if it keeps climbing, it is not. h(t) = e^(−t)·u(t) · an RC h(t) = e^(−0.3t)·cos(5t)·u(t) · an RLC h(t) = u(t) · an integrator h(t) = e^(0.3t)·u(t) · pole on the right h(t) = cos(3t)·u(t) · poles on the axis h(t) = sinc · an ideal low-pass filter Up to T = 10.0 s"},{"t":"Read in the poles","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#polos","x":"04 Read in the poles For a rational system, the integral condition translates into the location of the poles, which is how it is used in practice: Poles \\( \\int |h| \\) Stability Example All with \\( \\operatorname{Re} \\lt 0 \\) Converges Stable RC, damped RLC Any with \\( \\operatorname{Re} \\gt 0 \\) Diverges Unstable Positive feedback Simple, on the \\( j\\omega \\) axis Diverges Not BIBO stable Integrator, ideal oscillator Double, on the axis Diverges Unstable Double integrator The third row deserves attention because it contradicts a habit. A system with simple poles on the imaginary axis is usually called marginally stable, and that is not wrong as a description —its impulse response does not grow— but it is not BIBO stable. An integrator with a constant input, which is a perfectly bounded signal, gives a ramp that grows without limit. And an ideal oscillator driven exactly at its frequency goes into resonance and also runs away. Why a real integrator does not blow up Because it is not ideal. An op amp integrator has a resistor in parallel with the capacitor —sometimes just the leakage resistance— that moves the pole slightly to the left of the axis. That makes it BIBO stable and bounds the output to the DC value set by that resistor. When that path does not exist, the offset voltage is integrated and the output ends up against the power supply: the classic integrator that goes into saturation."},{"t":"In discrete time","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#discreto","x":"05 In discrete time The condition is \\( \\sum |h[n]| \\lt \\infty \\), and in terms of poles, all inside the unit circle. An FIR filter is always stable, regardless of its coefficients: since its impulse response has a finite number of terms, the sum cannot diverge. An IIR, on the other hand, can be unstable, and that is why the position of its poles has to be checked. \\[ y[n] = a\\,y[n-1] + x[n] \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; h[n] = a^{n}u[n], \\qquad \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}|a|^{n} = \\frac{1}{1 - |a|} \\;\\text{ if } |a| \\lt 1 \\] The simplest first-order filter: stable if and only if \\( |a| \\lt 1 \\), which is the pole inside the circle. With \\( |a| = 1 \\) the sum diverges and with \\( |a| \\gt 1 \\) the system grows exponentially. In a fixed-point implementation this shows up right away: the accumulator overflows within a few samples."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · The integral, by hand Compute \\( \\int_0^{\\infty} |h| \\) for \\( h(t) = e^{-2t}u(t) \\) and for \\( h(t) = e^{-0.2t}\\cos(10t)u(t) \\). Check that both converge and compare the values with those given by the lab. Exercise 2 · The integrator that runs away Build an op amp integrator with no DC feedback resistor and observe how long the output takes to reach saturation with the input grounded. Then add a 1 MΩ resistor in parallel with the capacitor and measure the new quiescent value. Exercise 3 · The limit of an IIR Program \\( y[n] = a\\,y[n-1] + x[n] \\) with \\( a = 0.9 \\), \\( 0.99 \\), \\( 1.0 \\) and \\( 1.01 \\). Measure how many samples each one takes to overflow a 16-bit accumulator with a constant input of value 1. The first two never overflow: they settle at \\( 1/(1-a) \\). Write down that value, and for the other two, at which sample they overflow."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Testing with a single input and concluding it is stable. The condition holds for every bounded input. Calling a system with poles on the axis stable. It is marginally stable, and it is not BIBO stable. Believing that a bounded \\( h \\) is enough. The step is bounded and its integral diverges. Forgetting that an FIR is always stable. Its sum is finite by construction. Confusing stability with causality. They are independent."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does the BIBO criterion say? That every bounded input must produce a bounded output. A single exception is enough to rule out stability. What is the condition on \\( h \\)? That it be absolutely integrable: \\( \\int|h(t)|dt \\lt \\infty \\). In discrete time, absolutely summable. Is an integrator BIBO stable? No. With a constant —bounded— input the output is a ramp that grows without limit. What is the worst input for a system? \\( x(t) = \\operatorname{sgn}(h(T-t)) \\): it makes all the contributions add up instead of canceling. For which values of \\( a \\) is \\( y[n] = a\\,y[n-1] + x[n] \\) stable? For \\( |a| \\lt 1 \\). There the geometric sum converges to \\( 1/(1-|a|) \\). Why is an FIR always stable? Because its impulse response has a finite number of terms, and a finite sum never diverges."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Stability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The stability condition proved in both directions, with the worst-case input. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Pearson, 2010. Stability in terms of poles, with Routh-Hurwitz and the phase and gain margins. John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Pearson, 2007. Stability of IIR filters and the effects of finite-precision arithmetic."},{"t":"Why a single number is not enough","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#medir","x":"01 Why a single number is not enough Measure the same resistance ten times with the same instrument and you do not get the same number ten times. That scatter is not a flaw in the procedure: it is information, and throwing it away amounts to inventing a precision you do not have. Descriptive statistics summarizes a data set with two questions: around what value the data lie and how spread out they are. Everything else is detail. Measure What it tells you When to use it Mean \\( \\bar{x} \\) The arithmetic average Data without outliers Median The middle value when the data are sorted With outliers: it does not get dragged along Mode The most frequent value Discrete or categorical data Standard deviation \\( s \\) The typical spread The standard measure of uncertainty Interquartile range The width of the central 50 % With outliers, like the median \\[ \\bar{x} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum x_i \\qquad s^2 = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum (x_i - \\bar{x})^2 \\qquad u(\\bar{x}) = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\] The \\( n-1 \\) in the denominator —the degrees of freedom— appears because the mean has already been computed from the same data. The last expression is the Type A uncertainty: the size of the “plus or minus” of the average, which falls with the square root of the number of measurements. That \\( \\sqrt{n} \\) has a hard practical consequence: to improve the uncertainty tenfold you have to measure a hundred times more. That is why averaging has diminishing returns, and at some point it pays to improve the instrument inste"},{"t":"See the shape, not just the numbers","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#dispersion","x":"02 See the shape, not just the numbers Two data sets can have the same mean and the same standard deviation and still be completely different. That is why a histogram is worth more than any summary: it shows the shape. Lab · a batch of components Five batches with different histories. Watch how the mean and the median drift apart when there are outliers, and what happens with a batch that was screened before it reached you. normal batch · 10 kΩ ± 1 % uniform batch · even tolerance screened batch · the center parts were removed batch with wrong parts mixed in skewed batch · process drift Number of parts = 200 Show the box plot The batch with its center removed A manufacturer that produces resistors and sorts them by tolerance sells the ones that fell within 1 % as 1 % components, and the rest as 5 %. The result: the “5 %” batch has a hole in the middle, and its histogram is bimodal. The mean still gives the nominal value and the standard deviation looks reasonable, but the shape looks nothing like a bell curve. It is the classic example of why looking at the histogram is not optional."},{"t":"Quartiles and outliers","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#caja","x":"03 Quartiles and outliers When the data are sorted, the quartiles split them into four equal groups. The first leaves 25 % below it, the second is the median and the third leaves 75 % below it. The distance between the first and the third —the interquartile range — measures the spread without a single absurd data point ruining it. \\[ \\mathrm{IQR} = Q_3 - Q_1 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{outlier if } x Q_3 + 1.5\\,\\mathrm{IQR} \\] The 1.5 criterion is a practical convention, not a theorem. In a normal distribution it leaves out about 0.7 % of the data, which is a reasonable threshold for manually reviewing whatever got flagged. An outlier is not deleted: it is investigated Discarding a data point just because it is inconvenient is the worst sin in the lab. An outlier can be a typo, a badly connected instrument or a different part that slipped into the batch —all legitimate reasons to exclude it, as long as you document it — but it can also be the only data point that shows the real problem. The rule is to look at what produced it before deciding."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · The batch of resistors Measure fifty resistors of the same nominal value and tolerance. Build the histogram, compute the mean, standard deviation and quartiles, and check whether the measured spread is consistent with the stated tolerance. Activity 2 · Type A uncertainty Measure the same resistance thirty times without touching anything. Compute the standard deviation of the measurements and the uncertainty of the average. Repeat with ten measurements and verify the \\( \\sqrt{n} \\) relationship between the two uncertainties. Activity 3 · Finding the screened batch Measure a batch of 5 % tolerance resistors and build the histogram with narrow classes. Check whether it has a bell shape or whether the center is missing, and explain what each result would reveal."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Reporting an average without its spread. A value without its “plus or minus” is not a measurement. Using the mean with outliers. A single one shifts it; the median does not budge. Dividing by \\( n \\) instead of \\( n-1 \\). It underestimates the spread, and with few data points by quite a bit. Confusing the standard deviation of the data with that of the average. The latter is \\( s/\\sqrt{n} \\) and is much smaller. Looking only at the numbers and not at the histogram. Two very different data sets can have the same summaries. Deleting a data point because it is inconvenient. First find out what produced it."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment Why does the sample standard deviation divide by \\( n-1 \\)? Because the mean was computed from the same data, so one degree of freedom is lost. Dividing by \\( n \\) underestimates the spread. How much does the uncertainty improve if you measure a hundred times more? Tenfold: it falls with the square root of \\( n \\). That is why averaging has diminishing returns. When is the median preferable to the mean? When there are outliers: the median does not get dragged along by them. What is the interquartile range? The distance between the first and the third quartile: the width of the central 50 % of the data. Why can a 5 % batch be bimodal? Because the parts from the center were removed to be sold as 1 % parts. The histogram ends up with a hole in the middle. What do you do with an outlier? Investigate what produced it. It can be excluded if there is an identified cause, and documenting it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Descriptive statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery and George C. Runger. Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Probabilidad y estadística aplicadas a la ingeniería”), Wiley, 2018. The standard reference for the course. Chapters 1 and 6 cover this unit with engineering data. John W. Tukey. Exploratory Data Analysis. Addison-Wesley, 1977. The source of the box plot and the 1.5 · IQR criterion, with the argument for why you have to look at the data before summarizing it. JCGM. Evaluation of measurement data — Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement. JCGM 100:2008 (Spanish edition published by the BIPM). The standard that defines how a measurement is reported. Type A and Type B uncertainties come from here."},{"t":"The error once everything has settled","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#error","x":"01 The error once everything has settled The transient response tells how the system gets there; the steady state, where it ends up. In a loop with unity feedback and loop gain \\( G(s) \\), the error between the reference and the output is \\( E(s) = R(s)/(1 + G(s)) \\), and its final value is obtained with the final value theorem of the Laplace transform, if the closed loop is stable: \\[ e_{ss} = \\lim_{s \\to 0} \\frac{s\\, R(s)}{1 + G(s)} \\] The result depends on the input: a step (a fixed position), a ramp (a constant velocity) or a parabola (a constant acceleration), and on how many integrators the loop has."},{"t":"System type and the error constants","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#tipo","x":"02 System type and the error constants The type of a system is the number of poles at the origin of its loop gain, that is, of integrators. Each integrator makes it possible to follow, without error, an input one degree harder: \\[ K_p = \\lim_{s\\to 0} G(s) \\] \\[ K_v = \\lim_{s\\to 0} s\\,G(s) \\] \\[ K_a = \\lim_{s\\to 0} s^2 G(s) \\] Position, velocity and acceleration error constants. Type Step Ramp Parabola 0 \\( 1/(1 + K_p) \\) Infinite Infinite 1 0 \\( 1/K_v \\) Infinite 2 0 0 \\( 1/K_a \\) A motor position control, which already integrates the velocity, is type 1: it follows a fixed position without error and a ramp with a constant error. The oven with proportional control in the introduction is type 0: that is why it left an error. Adding an integrator in the controller raises the type by one, at the cost of phase and stability. Lab · system type and steady-state error Three loops with unity feedback: type 0, \\( K/((s+1)(0.2s+1)) \\); type 1, \\( K/(s(0.2s+1)) \\); type 2, \\( K(s+0.5)/(s^2(0.2s+1)) \\). They are simulated for 20 s with Runge-Kutta; the gray is the reference and the blue, the output. Type 0 system Type 1 system Type 2 system Step input Ramp input Parabola input t²/2 Gain K ="},{"t":"Classification of control systems","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#clasificacion","x":"03 Classification of control systems Besides the type, control systems are classified by what is asked of them. A regulator keeps the output at a fixed value despite disturbances, like an oven or a power supply; a servomechanism makes the output follow a changing reference, like the axis of a robot. A distinction is also made between linear and nonlinear, continuous and discrete, single-input single-output and multivariable, and fixed-parameter and adaptive systems. The same question, how much error remains, is also asked in the face of a constant disturbance, and the answer depends on where the integrators are with respect to it."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Position error Close a proportional speed loop around a motor and measure the steady-state error for several gains. Compare with \\( 1/(1+K_p) \\). Activity 2 · Ramp tracking With a position loop, apply a ramp reference and measure the tracking error. Verify that it goes down as the gain goes up. Activity 3 · Integral action Add integral action to the speed loop and check that the position error disappears."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Applying the final value theorem to an unstable system. The result makes no sense; stability has to be checked first. Counting the plant's integrators without looking at the controller. The type is that of the complete loop gain. Raising the gain only to lower the error. The error goes down, but the overshoot and the risk of instability grow. Confusing error with respect to the reference with error with respect to disturbances. They depend on where each signal enters."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment A type 0 system with K p = 19, what error does it have for a unit step? \\( 1/20 = 0.05 \\). What error does a type 1 system with K v = 50 s⁻¹ have for a ramp of 2 units per second? \\( 2/50 = 0.04 \\). What type is \\( G(s) = 10/(s(s+2)(s+5)) \\) and what is K v ? Type 1; \\( K_v = 10/10 = 1 \\) s⁻¹. Why does a motor position control follow a step without error? Because the motor integrates the velocity: the loop has a pole at the origin and is type 1."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Steady state and system type","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). Steady-state errors and the error constants. Richard C. Dorf and Robert H. Bishop. Modern Control Systems. 10th ed., Pearson, 2005 (Spanish edition: “Sistemas de control moderno”). Steady-state error and the sensitivity of feedback systems. Norman S. Nise. Control Systems Engineering. 8th ed., Wiley, 2019. Steady-state error with respect to references and disturbances."},{"t":"Gases, and why they are the easy ones","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#gases","x":"01 Gases, and why they are the easy ones In a gas the molecules are so far apart that they hardly notice one another. That mutual indifference is what makes it possible to describe the gas with a single equation, the same for all gases: \\[ PV = nRT \\qquad R = 0.082\\ \\frac{\\text{atm}\\cdot\\text{L}}{\\text{mol}\\cdot\\text{K}} = 8.314\\ \\frac{\\text{J}}{\\text{mol}\\cdot\\text{K}} \\] One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 liters at 0 °C and 1 atm. It does not matter which gas it is: that is the remarkable part. The equation fails when the gas is compressed or cooled enough for the molecules to start noticing each other. Van der Waals corrects the two things the ideal model ignores —the volume of the molecules themselves and the attraction between them—: \\[ \\left(P + \\frac{an^2}{V^2}\\right)(V - nb) = nRT \\] Where this appears in electronics In diffusion furnaces and deposition chambers, where the gas flow is measured in sccm —standard cubic centimeters per minute—, which is a unit of amount of substance disguised as a volume. Converting sccm to moles per second is applying \\( PV = nRT \\) with \\( P \\) and \\( T \\) fixed by convention."},{"t":"Liquids and solids: when the forces win","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#condensados","x":"02 Liquids and solids: when the forces win As the temperature drops, kinetic energy is no longer enough to overcome the intermolecular forces and matter condenses. In a liquid the molecules touch but slide past each other; in a solid they are fixed in definite positions. State Volume and shape Order What dominates Gas Neither of its own None Thermal energy Liquid Volume yes, shape no Short-range A tie Crystalline solid Both Long-range, periodic The bonding forces Amorphous solid Both Short-range, frozen The viscosity on cooling The last row matters more than it seems. Glass and thermal silicon dioxide are amorphous: they have the local order of the crystal —each silicon surrounded by four oxygens— but no periodicity. And the silicon of wafers is the opposite, a single crystal in which the lattice repeats without interruption from one end to the other, because any grain boundary would be a leakage path for the carriers. The four solids, according to what holds them together Ionic —NaCl, ceramic oxides—: hard, brittle, insulating as solids. Covalent network —Si, diamond, SiC—: the hardest, with a very high melting point, semiconductors or insulators. Metallic —Cu, Al—: ductile and conductive. Molecular —ice, paraffin, most plastics—: soft and with a low melting point, because what has to be overcome is not the bond but the force between molecules."},{"t":"The phase diagram","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#fases","x":"03 The phase diagram The state of a substance is not decided by temperature alone: temperature and pressure decide it together. The map of that decision is the phase diagram, and on it there are two points with names of their own: the triple point, where the three states coexist, and the critical point, beyond which the distinction between liquid and gas no longer makes sense. Lab · phase diagram Move the temperature and the pressure, and the point is located on the map. Look at silicon and water: their melting curves slope to the left, unlike all the others. water carbon dioxide nitrogen silicon Temperature = 300 K Pressure = 1 atm Silicon behaves like water Almost all substances contract on solidifying, and that is why their melting curve slopes to the right: more pressure, harder to melt. Water and silicon expand on freezing —both form a tetrahedral network more open than the liquid—, so their curve slopes to the left. It is the same reason why ice floats and why a quartz crucible in which the silicon solidifies cracks."},{"t":"Vacuum, which is where chips are made","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#vacio","x":"04 Vacuum, which is where chips are made A good part of electronics manufacturing takes place in vacuum chambers: metal evaporation, sputtering, ion implantation. The reason is not cleanliness but geometry. For an evaporated atom to reach the wafer in a straight line, it must not collide with anything along the way, and how far it travels without colliding is the mean free path: \\[ \\lambda = \\frac{kT}{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi d^2 P} \\] With \\( d \\approx 0.37 \\) nm for the nitrogen in air. At atmospheric pressure \\( \\lambda \\) is 65 nm; at \\( 10^{-6} \\) torr, about 50 meters. Those 50 meters are the number that justifies all of vacuum engineering: if the chamber measures half a meter, a mean free path of 50 m means that practically no atom collides before arriving. The lab above calculates \\( \\lambda \\) for the chosen pressure. Regime Pressure \\( \\lambda \\) What for Atmosphere 760 torr 65 nm — Medium vacuum 10⁻³ torr 5 cm Sputtering, etching plasmas High vacuum 10⁻⁶ torr 50 m Metal evaporation Ultra-high vacuum 10⁻⁹ torr 50 km Molecular beam epitaxy"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Verify the gas law With a capped 60 mL syringe and a pressure gauge, compress the air in steps and record \\( P \\) and \\( V \\). Plot \\( P \\) against \\( 1/V \\) and verify the linearity. Estimate the error introduced by the dead volume of the connection. Activity 2 · Dry ice and the triple point Observe solid CO₂ at atmospheric pressure: it sublimes without passing through a liquid. Explain with the phase diagram why, and calculate at what pressure it would have to be held to see it melt. Activity 3 · The cooling curve of tin Melt solder tin in a crucible with a thermocouple inside and record the temperature as it cools. Identify the solidification plateau and compare it with the melting point of the alloy used."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using \\( PV = nRT \\) with the temperature in degrees Celsius. \\( T \\) always goes in kelvin. Believing that every solid is crystalline. Glass and thermal SiO₂ are amorphous, and that changes their properties. Thinking that more pressure always solidifies. In water and in silicon it is the other way around. Confusing evaporation with boiling. One occurs at the surface at any temperature; the other, throughout the volume, when the vapor pressure equals the external pressure. Assuming that vacuum means “having nothing.” At 10⁻⁶ torr about 10¹⁰ molecules remain per cubic centimeter; what changes is how far they travel without colliding."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What volume does one mole of gas occupy at 0 °C and 1 atm? 22.4 liters, and it does not depend on which gas it is. What is the triple point? The only combination of pressure and temperature at which solid, liquid and vapor coexist. For water, 0.006 atm and 0.01 °C. Why does ice float? Because water expands on solidifying: its tetrahedral network is more open than the liquid. Silicon does the same. What distinguishes a crystalline solid from an amorphous one? Long-range order. The amorphous one keeps the local order but not the periodicity. What is high vacuum for in metal evaporation? So that the mean free path greatly exceeds the size of the chamber and the atoms reach the wafer in a straight line. What does the van der Waals equation correct? The volume of the molecules themselves, with \\( b \\), and the attraction between them, with \\( a \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"States of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 5 and 11: ideal and real gases, intermolecular forces and phase diagrams. William D. Callister and David G. Rethwisch. Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la ciencia e ingeniería de los materiales”), Reverté, 2019. Crystal structures, amorphous solids and equilibrium diagrams of alloys. Stephen A. Campbell. Fabrication Engineering at the Micro- and Nanoscale. 4th ed., Oxford University Press, 2013. The vacuum chapter: mean free path, pumps and the regimes in the table of this unit."},{"t":"The two conditions of equilibrium","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#condiciones","x":"01 The two conditions of equilibrium A body not moving does not mean there are no forces: it means they cancel. And for an extended body it is not enough for the forces to cancel, because a pair of equal and opposite forces applied at different points makes it rotate. Two conditions are needed: \\[ \\sum_{} \\vec{F} = 0 \\quad \\sum_{} \\vec{M} = 0 \\] Translational equilibrium and rotational equilibrium. In the plane they are three scalar equations: ΣF x, ΣF y and ΣM. The moment of a force M = F·d, where d is the perpendicular distance from the pivot point to the line of action of the force —the lever arm—. That is why a long wrench loosens what a short one cannot: same force, longer arm. If ΣF = 0, the total moment has the same value about any point. That freedom is the central tool: you choose as the center the point where the unknowns you do not care about intersect, and they disappear from the equation."},{"t":"Constraints and reactions","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#vinculos","x":"02 Constraints and reactions Each support prevents certain motions, and for every motion prevented an unknown reaction appears: Support Prevents Unknowns Roller support Displacement perpendicular to the surface 1 Pin support (hinge) Both displacements 2 Fixed support (clamped end) Both displacements and rotation 3 If there are as many unknowns as equations, the problem is statically determinate and is solved with statics alone. If there are more unknowns than equations it is statically indeterminate, and the deformation of the material has to be considered: that is already strength of materials. Lab · reactions on a supported bar Move the supports and the load. The reactions come from the two equilibrium conditions, and if one comes out negative the bar tips over. Left support at 1.0 m Right support at 5.0 m Point load at 4.0 m Value of the load 400 N Self-weight of the bar 200 N"},{"t":"Center of gravity","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#gravedad","x":"03 Center of gravity It is the point where the whole weight can be considered to act. For a homogeneous body it coincides with the geometric center; for a composite one it is calculated as a weighted average: \\[ x_G = \\frac{\\sum_i m_i x_i}{\\sum_i m_i} \\] Stability A body resting on a surface tips over when the vertical through its center of gravity falls outside the base of support. That is why a tall, narrow cabinet is unstable, why a rack is anchored to the wall and why a bench instrument has its transformer at the bottom: lowering the center of gravity and widening the base are the only two ways to improve stability."},{"t":"Beams and trusses","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#reticulados","x":"04 Beams and trusses A truss is a structure of bars pinned at their ends and loaded only at the joints. Under those assumptions, each bar works only in tension or compression, never in bending, and this makes it possible to build light and very stiff structures: bridges, cranes and guyed antenna towers. Method of joints: set ΣF x = 0 and ΣF y = 0 at each joint. Method of sections: cut the structure and balance one of the parts. It is convenient when only a few bars matter. In a beam, on the other hand, transverse loads produce shear force and bending moment, which vary along its length. Sizing it means making sure the material holds up at the most heavily stressed section."},{"t":"Statics for an electronics engineer","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#electronica","x":"05 Statics for an electronics engineer Antenna support. Calculating the tensions in the guy wires and the compression of the mast under wind load is a statics problem. A mistake there takes the whole link down with it. Mounting cabinets and racks. The wall anchorage is sized from the moment produced by the weight at a distance. Securing boards and heavy components. A large transformer on a circuit board generates moments that fatigue the solder joints if it is not fastened mechanically. Load cells. An electronic scale measures deformation and, through it, force: its design starts from the equilibrium of moments in the beam that supports it. Cable trays. They are calculated as continuous beams, with their distributed load and their supports."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Reactions with two scales Rest a board on two kitchen scales and place a weight at different positions. Verify that the sum of the two readings is constant and that the distribution follows the law of moments. Activity 2 · Tipping angle Tilt an empty cabinet and then the same one with a mass at the base. Measure the angle at which it tips and relate it to the position of the center of gravity. Activity 3 · Load cell with strain gauges Instrument a cantilever beam with strain gauges in a Wheatstone bridge. Verify that the output is proportional to the bending moment, that is, to the weight times the distance."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Using the distance to the force instead of the perpendicular distance to its line of action. Forgetting the self-weight of the bar or the structure. Taking moments about a poorly chosen point and ending up with two unknowns in one equation, when choosing a support would have eliminated one. Confusing mass with weight when entering the data: scales give kilograms, the equations require newtons. Accepting a negative reaction as valid at a support that can only push. It means that the structure tips over or that an anchor is needed."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A weightless 4 m bar, supported at its ends, carries 600 N at 1 m from the left end. What is each reaction? Moments about A: R B ·4 = 600·1, so R B = 150 N and R A = 450 N. The load is shared in inverse proportion to the distances. Why is it convenient to take moments about a support? Because the reaction at that support has zero lever arm and disappears from the equation, which is left with a single unknown. When does a body resting on a surface tip over? When the vertical through its center of gravity falls outside the base of support. How many unknowns does a fixed support contribute in the plane? Three: two force components and a moment. Why do the bars of an ideal truss not work in bending? Because they are pinned at their ends and loaded only at the joints: each bar receives forces only at its two ends, and to be in equilibrium they must be aligned with its axis. A 30 kg cabinet hangs with its center of gravity 25 cm from the wall. What moment does the anchor carry? M = 30·9.8·0.25 ≈ 73.5 N·m, shared among the screws according to their vertical spacing."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Statics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ferdinand P. Beer and E. Russell Johnston. Vector Mechanics for Engineers: Statics. 10th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2013 (Spanish edition, “Mecánica vectorial para ingenieros: estática”). The reference text: constraints, trusses and centers of gravity, with problems on real structures. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapter 12 treats the equilibrium of the rigid body with a basic-physics approach. Russell C. Hibbeler. Engineering Mechanics: Statics. 12th ed., Pearson, 2010 (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería mecánica: estática”). Very graphic, with step-by-step free-body diagrams."},{"t":"The balanced equation is a recipe","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#receta","x":"01 The balanced equation is a recipe A balanced chemical equation does not say what happens: it says in what proportion it happens. The coefficients are the recipe, and they are read in moles, not in grams or in volumes. \\[ \\mathrm{Si} + \\mathrm{O_2} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{SiO_2} \\] One mole of silicon with one mole of oxygen gives one mole of dioxide. By mass: 28.09 g of silicon with 31.99 g of oxygen give 60.08 g of oxide, because mass is conserved even though the numbers look nothing alike. This particular reaction is the one that creates the gate oxide of every MOS transistor: the wafer goes into a furnace at 1000 °C with oxygen, and the silicon at the surface turns into SiO₂. Since the oxide occupies 2.27 times the volume of the silicon consumed, for each micron of oxide grown, 0.44 microns came out of the wafer. That number is pure stoichiometry, and it has to be taken into account when designing the final thickness. The order for solving any problem 1. Balance the equation. 2. Convert the data to moles, dividing by the molar mass. 3. Compare with the coefficients and find the limiting reactant. 4. Calculate products and leftovers in moles. 5. Go back to grams if needed. Skipping step 2 is the source of almost all errors."},{"t":"The limiting reactant","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#limitante","x":"02 The limiting reactant When the reactants are not in the exact proportion of the recipe, one runs out first. That is the limiting reactant, and it is the only one that decides how much product is obtained: there is some of the other left over, and what is left over does not react no matter how much of it is there. Lab · limiting reactant and energy Pick one of the reactions that appear in electronics manufacturing and enter the moles of each reactant. The calculation shows which one limits, how much product comes out, how much is left over and how much energy is released. silicon oxidation · Si + O₂ → SiO₂ oxide etching · SiO₂ + 6 HF → H₂SiF₆ + 2 H₂O silicon deposition · SiHCl₃ + H₂ → Si + 3 HCl alkaline battery · Zn + 2 MnO₂ → ZnO + Mn₂O₃ Moles of the first reactant = 1 Moles of the second reactant = 1 In industry, one almost never works in exact proportion. In oxidation a huge excess of oxygen is used so that the limiting reactant is always the silicon at the surface and the rate does not depend on the flow; in etching with hydrofluoric acid, the excess acid guarantees that the oxide is removed entirely. The excess is chosen, not suffered. Theoretical yield and actual yield What stoichiometry calculates is the theoretical yield: the maximum possible. The actual yield is always lower, because of side reactions, equilibrium or handling losses. The ratio between the two is the percent yield, and in manufacturing processes it is also called yield: the fraction of the chips on"},{"t":"The energy of the reaction","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#energia","x":"03 The energy of the reaction Every reaction exchanges energy with its surroundings, because breaking bonds costs energy and forming them gives it back. If it gives back more than it costs, the reaction is exothermic and \\( \\Delta H \\lt 0 \\); if it is the other way around, endothermic. \\[ \\Delta H_{\\text{reaction}} = \\sum \\Delta H_f^{\\circ}(\\text{products}) - \\sum \\Delta H_f^{\\circ}(\\text{reactants}) \\] With the coefficients of the balanced equation as factors. The enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is zero by definition. That enthalpy is a state function —it depends on where you start and where you end, not on the path— is what makes it possible to add enthalpies. That is Hess's law, and it is the reason why a single table of enthalpies of formation is enough to calculate any reaction, even one that nobody has ever measured. Reaction \\( \\Delta H \\) What it implies in practice Si + O₂ → SiO₂ −911 kJ/mol Very exothermic: silicon oxidizes on its own in air, and that 2 nm native layer has to be removed before any process SiHCl₃ + H₂ → Si + 3 HCl +219 kJ/mol Endothermic: that is why the Siemens reactor operates at 1100 °C and consumes the energy it does Zn + 2 MnO₂ → ZnO + Mn₂O₃ −270 kJ/mol The energy of an alkaline battery. Its voltage comes from the free energy (about −290 kJ/mol), divided by the charge transferred The thermite reaction is not a curiosity The reaction \\( \\mathrm{2Al + Fe_2O_3 \\rightarrow Al_2O_3 + 2Fe} \\) releases 852 kJ per mole and reac"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Finding the limiting reactant by measuring React measured amounts of baking soda with vinegar in a container on a balance and record the loss of mass due to the CO₂ released. Repeat, varying the proportion until you identify experimentally the point at which the limiting reactant changes. Activity 2 · The heat of a reaction Use a polystyrene-cup calorimeter to measure the temperature rise when dissolving sodium hydroxide in water and when neutralizing it with hydrochloric acid. Calculate \\( \\Delta H \\) per mole and compare with the tabulated value. Activity 3 · Hess with three paths Experimentally determine the enthalpy of three related reactions —solid NaOH in water, solid NaOH with HCl, and NaOH in solution with HCl— and verify that the first plus the third give the second."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Comparing grams with grams to find the limiting reactant. The comparison is done in moles divided by their coefficient. Balancing by changing the subscripts. That changes the substance; only the coefficients are touched. Calculating the product from the excess reactant. The limiting reactant decides it, always. Confusing theoretical yield with actual yield. One comes from the calculation; the other, from the balance. Forgetting the sign of \\( \\Delta H \\). Negative is exothermic: the system gives off energy. Reversing products and reactants in Hess's law. It is products minus reactants, and when a reaction is reversed the sign changes."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How is the limiting reactant identified? By dividing the moles of each reactant by its stoichiometric coefficient: the smallest quotient rules. What does it mean for \\( \\Delta H \\) to be negative? That the reaction is exothermic: it releases energy to the surroundings. What does Hess's law say? That the enthalpy of a reaction does not depend on the path, so steps can be added to obtain an overall reaction. What is the enthalpy of formation of gaseous O₂? Zero: it is an element in its standard state. Why does the silicon deposition reactor need heat? Because the reaction is endothermic, with \\( \\Delta H \\approx +219 \\) kJ per mole of silicon deposited. If one micron of silicon is oxidized, how much oxide grows? About 2.27 microns, because SiO₂ occupies more volume than the silicon it comes from."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 3 and 6: stoichiometry, limiting reactant, yield and thermochemistry. Ralph H. Petrucci et al. General Chemistry. 11th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química general”), Pearson, 2017. Many worked problems on limiting reactant and Hess's law, with the detail of the units. Stephen A. Campbell. Fabrication Engineering at the Micro- and Nanoscale. 4th ed., Oxford University Press, 2013. Thermal oxidation of silicon, with the Deal–Grove model and the volume ratio of this unit."},{"t":"Where the electrons are","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#orbitales","x":"01 Where the electrons are The Bohr model —electrons circling in orbits— is enough to draw a picture, but not to explain. The current model says that an electron has no trajectory but a probability distribution, and that its state is described by four numbers: Number What it says Values \\( n \\), principal The energy level, the “size” 1, 2, 3, … \\( \\ell \\), azimuthal The shape of the orbital 0 to \\( n-1 \\): s, p, d, f \\( m_\\ell \\), magnetic The orientation in space \\( -\\ell \\) to \\( +\\ell \\) \\( m_s \\), spin The electron's own spin \\( +\\tfrac12 \\) or \\( -\\tfrac12 \\) And acting on those four numbers is the Pauli exclusion principle: two electrons of the same atom cannot have all four equal. That prohibition is what forces electrons to fill ever higher levels instead of all piling up in the lowest one, and the whole periodic table follows from it. \\[ \\text{electrons per sublevel} = 2(2\\ell + 1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s: 2, \\quad p: 6, \\quad d: 10, \\quad f: 14 \\]"},{"t":"The filling order, and the table that results","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#configuracion","x":"02 The filling order, and the table that results Electrons first occupy the sublevels of lowest energy —the Aufbau rule— following the order \\( 1s\\,2s\\,2p\\,3s\\,3p\\,4s\\,3d\\,4p\\ldots \\), which does not coincide with the order of \\( n \\). In sublevels with several orbitals, Hund's rule says that electrons are distributed one at a time before pairing up. The periodic table is that filling order, drawn out. The columns group elements with the same valence configuration, and that is why they are chemically similar. Lab · electron configuration and valence Tap an element and its configuration, its valence electrons and the role it plays in electronics appear. Groups 13, 14 and 15 are highlighted: they are the groups of the semiconductors and their dopants. Highlight the semiconductor groups"},{"t":"Why silicon and not something else","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#valencia","x":"03 Why silicon and not something else Group 14 —carbon, silicon, germanium— has four valence electrons, which is exactly half of the eight that fit. That allows them to form four covalent bonds in a perfect three-dimensional lattice, in which all the electrons are committed. Group Valence In silicon it acts as Examples 13 3 Acceptor: one electron is missing, leaving a hole Boron, aluminum, gallium, indium 14 4 The crystal itself Carbon, silicon, germanium 15 5 Donor: one electron is left over, and it becomes free Phosphorus, arsenic, antimony That is all of solid-state electronics in three lines. Pure silicon does not conduct well because it has no free electrons; by adding one group 15 atom per million, one out of every million positions has an extra electron, and that electron can move. With group 13 the opposite happens: an electron is missing, and the hole moves as if it were a positive charge. And germanium, which came first The first transistors were made of germanium, from the same group 14. It lost to silicon for two chemical reasons: its band gap is narrower —0.67 eV against 1.12— so at 70 °C it already conducts on its own, and above all its oxide is soluble in water. SiO₂, on the other hand, is stable, insulating and grows on the material itself: that is what made planar technology possible and, with it, the integrated circuit. Silicon won because of its oxide, not because of the semiconductor."},{"t":"Periodic properties","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#periodicas","x":"04 Periodic properties As you move across the table, several properties vary regularly because they depend on the configuration: Property Moving to the right Moving down a group Atomic radius Decreases Increases Ionization energy Increases Decreases Electronegativity Increases Decreases Metallic character Decreases Increases The fourth row explains a band that runs diagonally across the table: the metalloids, between the metals on the left and the nonmetals on the right. Boron, silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony and tellurium are there, and it is no coincidence that solid-state electronics was built almost entirely from that diagonal: they are the ones that neither fully conduct nor fully insulate. And electronegativity is what anticipates the type of bond, which is the subject of the next unit: when two atoms have very different electronegativities, the bond is ionic; when they have similar ones, covalent."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Configurations by hand Write the complete electron configuration of Si, P, B, Ga, As and Cu, and mark the valence electrons in each one. Predict which ones can act as a donor or an acceptor in silicon and justify your answer. Activity 2 · The spectrum reveals the structure Use a diffraction-grating spectroscope to observe the light from a sodium lamp, a mercury lamp and a white LED. Explain why the first two show lines and the third a continuous band. Activity 3 · The diagonal of the metalloids Locate the six metalloids in the table and look up the resistivity of each one. Plot it on a logarithmic scale next to that of a metal and that of an insulator, and mark where doped silicon falls."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Filling in order of \\( n \\). The 4s fills before the 3d; you have to follow the order of energy. Pairing electrons too soon. Hund's rule requires distributing them one per orbital. Counting all the electrons of the outermost level as valence in the transition metals. The d electrons are also involved there. Believing that the Bohr model describes the atom. It works as a picture, not as a model. Confusing electronegativity with ionization energy. One is the tendency to attract electrons in a bond; the other, the energy needed to pull one off an isolated atom."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the configuration of silicon? \\( 1s^2\\,2s^2\\,2p^6\\,3s^2\\,3p^2 \\), with four valence electrons in the third level. Why is phosphorus a donor in silicon? Because it has five valence electrons: four form the bonds of the crystal and the fifth is left free to conduct. How many electrons fit in a d sublevel? Ten: there are five orbitals with two electrons each. What does the Pauli principle say? That two electrons of the same atom cannot have all four quantum numbers equal. Filling by levels follows from that. Why did silicon win out over germanium? Above all because of its oxide: SiO₂ is stable, insulating and grows on the material itself. Germanium's is soluble in water. What are the metalloids? The diagonal between metals and nonmetals: boron, silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony and tellurium. They neither fully conduct nor fully insulate."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Structure of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 7 and 8: atomic structure, electron configuration and periodic properties. Peter Atkins and Julio de Paula. Physical Chemistry. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química física”), Editorial Médica Panamericana, 2018. The quantum treatment of orbitals, for those who want to see where the numbers come from. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. The step from the isolated atom to the bands of the crystal, which is the natural continuation of this unit."},{"t":"When the array is not enough","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#porque","x":"01 When the array is not enough An array is fast and simple, but its size is fixed, decided before the program runs. If more data arrives than expected, it overflows; if less arrives, memory is wasted. A dynamic structure grows and shrinks during execution, allocating one node per item of data. Operation Array Linked list Access element i O(1): direct calculation O(n): you have to traverse Insert at the beginning O(n): shift everything O(1): change two pointers Insert at the end O(1) if there is room O(n), or O(1) with a pointer to the tail Delete from the middle O(n): shift everything O(1) if you already have the previous node Memory per item Only the data The data plus a pointer Cache locality Excellent: contiguous Poor: scattered nodes Which one to choose There is no winner: there is judgment. If you know the size and access by index, use an array. If there are many insertions and deletions at arbitrary positions, use a list. And in practice, for a few elements the array often wins anyway, because contiguous memory flies in the processor’s cache."},{"t":"The linked list","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#lista","x":"02 The linked list Each node holds the data and the address of the next one. The last one points to NULL, and an external variable —the head— holds the address of the first: typedef struct node { int data; struct node *next; } Node; Node *head = NULL; The structure refers to itself: that is why the name struct node is needed inside its own definition. Inserting at the beginning takes three lines: Node *n = malloc(sizeof(Node)); n->data = x; n->next = head; head = n; Order matters If you do head = n before n->next = head, the node points to itself and the whole list is lost. In linked structures, the order of pointer assignments is the most frequent error and the hardest to spot by reading. Lab · linked list Insert and delete nodes, and watch how the pointers rearrange and how many steps each operation costs. Value 25 Insert at head Insert at tail Insert in order Delete the first Delete that value Search Clear"},{"t":"Variants and derived structures","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#variantes","x":"03 Variants and derived structures Structure What it is like What it is for Doubly linked list Each node points to the next and the previous Traversing in both directions, deleting without searching for the previous node Circular list The last one points to the first Round-robin schedulers, buffers Stack (LIFO) The last item in is taken out Function calls, undo, evaluating expressions Queue (FIFO) The first item in is taken out Communication buffers, event queues Circular queue Fixed array with indices that wrap around UART buffers in microcontrollers Binary search tree Each node has two children, in order Search in O(log n) if balanced The stack you have already been using Each function call pushes its frame —local variables and return address— and pops it on return. That is why a recursion without a base case ends in a stack overflow: the data structure that overflows is, literally, a stack."},{"t":"Binary trees","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#arboles","x":"04 Binary trees In a binary search tree, everything smaller than a node is in its left subtree and everything larger is in the right one. Searching means walking down comparing, and if the tree is balanced each comparison discards half of the remaining data: 20 comparisons are enough for a million elements. In-order traversal —left, node, right— returns the data sorted from smallest to largest. Preorder —node, left, right— is used to copy the structure. Postorder —left, right, node— to free it, because it destroys the children before the parent. The problem is imbalance: if the data arrives already sorted, the tree degenerates into a list and search goes back to O(n). This is the origin of self-balancing trees, such as AVL and red-black trees."},{"t":"Where they are used in a device","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#electronica","x":"05 Where they are used in a device UART circular buffer. The receive interrupt writes to the queue and the main program reads: it is the most widely used structure in embedded systems. Event queue. A graphical interface, a real-time operating system and firmware with a state machine all use the same producer-consumer scheme. A scheduler’s task list. Ordered by priority or by deadline. Sample history. A sliding window for averaging or filtering signals is implemented with a circular queue. Device table. An operating system kernel maintains linked lists of open processes, files and drivers."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · A complete linked list Implement in C insertion at the head, at the tail and in order, deletion by value, search, counting and freeing the whole list. Check with valgrind that no memory is leaked. Exercise 2 · Circular buffer Write a fixed-size circular queue with read and write indices, and use it to receive data from a serial port without losing bytes while the program does something else. Exercise 3 · Comparing costs by measuring Insert a hundred thousand elements at the beginning of an array and of a list, and measure the time of each one. Repeat accessing by index. Contrast the numbers with the table in the first section."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Losing the head of the list by reassigning pointers in the wrong order. Freeing a node before saving its next, and being left with no way to reach the rest. Not handling the empty list or the deletion of the first node, which are the two edge cases. Forgetting to set the next of the last node to NULL. Using a list where an array was the better choice, only because it sounds more sophisticated. Traversing with the same pointer that is used to free, without an auxiliary variable."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How much does it cost to insert at the beginning of a linked list? O(1): allocate the node and change two pointers, no matter how many elements there are. Why is accessing element 500 of a list slow? Because you have to follow 500 pointers: the address cannot be calculated, you have to traverse. Which structure suits a UART’s receive buffer? A circular queue on a fixed array: it needs no malloc, its size is known and an interrupt can use it safely. Which traversal of a binary search tree returns the data in order? The in-order traversal: left, node, right. What happens if already sorted data is inserted into a simple binary tree? It degenerates into a list: each node has only one child and the search goes from O(log n) to O(n). Why does infinite recursion end in a “stack overflow”? Because each call pushes a frame with its local variables, and the program’s stack has a limited size."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Dynamic data structures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Thomas H. Cormen et al. Introduction to Algorithms. 4th ed., MIT Press, 2022. The reference on data structures and their cost analysis. Mark Allen Weiss. Estructuras de datos en C. Addison-Wesley, 1995 (in Spanish). Complete implementations in C, at exactly the level of the course. Brian W. Kernighan and Dennis M. Ritchie. The C Programming Language. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “El lenguaje de programación C”), Pearson, 1991. Chapter 6 builds a symbol table with linked structures, as a capstone example. Robert Sedgewick. Algorithms in C. 3rd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1998. Very graphic, with good explanations of trees and their balancing."},{"t":"Ethics, morality and deontology","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#conceptos","x":"01 Ethics, morality and deontology Morality is the set of convictions of a person or a community about what is right and what is wrong. Ethics is the reflection on those convictions: why something is right, how to decide when two duties collide. Professional deontology is ethics applied to a profession, written into concrete rules that a professional community gives itself and that a disciplinary tribunal can enforce. The Engineering and Society page on the profession and ethics introduced the concepts and the gender perspective. Here we work with the codes in force and with cases."},{"t":"Codes of ethics","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#codigos","x":"02 Codes of ethics In national jurisdiction, the code of ethics for surveying, architecture and engineering was approved by Decree 1099/84 and is applied by professional councils such as COPITEC. Each provincial association has its own, with very similar content. Internationally, the IEEE code is the reference for the field, and it begins with the same principle as all of them: putting the safety, health and welfare of the public first. Duties to… Examples Society Not signing work that puts people or the environment at risk; warning the authority when a danger is not corrected The profession Not lending your signature; not practicing outside your own competence; keeping up to date Colleagues Not displacing another professional by offering lower fees; not replacing them on a job without notifying them; not criticizing them without grounds The client or employer Loyalty, professional secrecy, candidly reporting the risks and the limits of the work, avoiding conflicts of interest Breaches are judged before the ethics or disciplinary tribunal of the council or association, with penalties ranging from a warning to suspension or revocation of the license."},{"t":"Conflict of interest","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#conflicto","x":"03 Conflict of interest A conflict of interest exists when a personal interest can influence, or appear to influence, the professional judgment that others expect to be impartial. The engineer who writes the specification for a tender and is a partner of one of the bidders; the one who recommends a supplier that pays them a commission; the expert witness who has a relationship with one of the parties. The general rule is simple: disclose it and, if it compromises impartiality, step aside. Hiding it turns an awkward situation into a breach."},{"t":"Cases where the legal and the ethical do not coincide","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#dilemas","x":"04 Cases where the legal and the ethical do not coincide Most of the serious ethical failures in the history of engineering were not illegal at the time they were decided. Some cases studied around the world: Case What happened The lesson Therac-25 (1985-1987) A radiotherapy machine with software errors delivered lethal overdoses; hardware safeguards had been removed in reliance on the software Safety software needs the same review as hardware, and failure reports must be investigated Challenger (1986) The engineers at the rocket manufacturer warned that the seals failed in cold weather; the decision to launch was made by management The engineer has to stand by their technical judgment under pressure from the organization Volkswagen emissions (2015) Software detected homologation tests and changed how the engine ran Cheating on a test is a design decision, and someone writes it Dilemmas · what would you do? Three situations of the kind that come up in the professional life of an electronic engineer. You choose a response and the analysis appears according to the codes of ethics: there is not always a perfect option, but there are options that a disciplinary tribunal would judge."},{"t":"Professional secrecy","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#secreto","x":"05 Professional secrecy What a professional learns through their work —a client’s design, their processes, their failures— is confidential. Revealing it without just cause is an ethical breach and can be a crime (Article 156 of the Código Penal, the Criminal Code). Secrecy yields to a greater duty: if keeping it puts people’s lives or safety at risk, the duty to the public prevails."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Situation The duty at stake A client asks you to skip an electromagnetic compatibility test to meet a deadline To the public and to the truth of what is being certified Recommending the equipment brand you are a distributor of Disclosing the conflict of interest Reusing in a new project the firmware developed for another client Secrecy and intellectual property Signing an installation done by someone else without having inspected it Not lending your signature"},{"t":"Activities","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Read your own code Obtain the code of ethics of the association or council of your jurisdiction and compare it with the IEEE’s: which duties do they share and which appear in only one. Activity 2 · Analyze a case Read a public report on the Therac-25 case and list the engineering decisions that, had they been made differently, would have prevented the accidents. Activity 3 · Write a dilemma Write a realistic ethical dilemma from the field of electronics with three courses of action and analyze them against the code of ethics."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that what is legal is sufficient. Many serious ethical breaches were legal. Staying silent about a conflict of interest hoping it goes unnoticed. Disclosing it almost always solves it; hiding it makes it worse. Lending your signature to an acquaintance. It is one of the breaches that disciplinary tribunals penalize most. Thinking that responsibility lies with the company. The ethical duty is personal."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is professional deontology? Ethics applied to a profession, written into concrete rules that a disciplinary tribunal can enforce. What principle heads the engineering codes of ethics? Putting the safety, health and welfare of the public first. What should you do in the face of a conflict of interest? Disclose it and, if it compromises impartiality, step aside. When does professional secrecy yield? When keeping it puts people’s lives or safety at risk."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Ethics in professional practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Charles E. Harris, Michael S. Pritchard, Michael J. Rabins and others. Engineering Ethics: Concepts and Cases. Cengage. The reference text on engineering ethics, with methods for analyzing cases. Nancy G. Leveson and Clark S. Turner. An Investigation of the Therac-25 Accidents. IEEE Computer, 1993. The classic analysis of the case, essential reading for anyone who designs equipment with software. IEEE. IEEE Code of Ethics. IEEE Policies, Section 7.8. The code of the field, brief and in force."},{"t":"The time value of money","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#tiempo","x":"01 The time value of money A peso today is worth more than a peso a year from now, because it can be invested and because the future is uncertain. To compare amounts from different moments they are brought to the same date with an interest rate \\( k \\): an amount \\( F_t \\) received \\( t \\) periods from now is equivalent today to its present value: \\[ PV = \\frac{F_t}{(1 + k)^t} \\] The rate \\( k \\) used to evaluate a project is the cutoff rate, or cost of capital: what the best alternative of similar risk yields, or what it costs to finance. In contexts with inflation one must be consistent: nominal flows with a nominal rate, or flows in constant currency with a real rate, as seen in macroeconomics."},{"t":"The cash flow","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#flujo","x":"02 The cash flow Evaluating a project —a production line, a test fixture, a new product— starts with its cash flow: year by year, what comes into and goes out of the cash box because of doing the project, compared with not doing it. Item What it includes Investment Equipment, installation, initial working capital Revenues Additional sales or cost savings Outflows Additional variable and fixed costs, as seen in costs Taxes On profit; depreciation is not a cash outflow, but it reduces the tax Residual value What the project is worth at the end, and the recovery of working capital"},{"t":"The decision criteria","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#criterios","x":"03 The decision criteria The net present value adds up all the flows brought to the present; if it is positive, the project yields more than the cutoff rate and adds value. The internal rate of return is the rate that makes it zero: the project’s own profitability, which is compared with the cutoff rate. The payback period is the time in which the flows return the investment; it is an indicator of risk and liquidity, not of profitability. \\[ NPV = -I_0 + \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{F_t}{(1 + k)^t} \\] \\[ NPV(IRR) = 0 \\] With conventional flows —one investment followed by positive flows— the two criteria agree on accepting or rejecting. To choose between mutually exclusive projects of different size, the NPV governs. The IRR is calculated by trial and error or, as in the lab, by bisection. Lab · net present value and internal rate of return A project with an initial investment and a constant annual flow for n years, with no residual value. The curve is the net present value as a function of the cutoff rate; it crosses zero at the internal rate of return. The yellow dot is the chosen rate. Initial investment = Annual flow = Project life n = Cutoff rate k = Since future flows are estimates, the analysis is completed with sensitivity —how much the NPV changes if sales, costs or investment depart from what was forecast— and with pessimistic, likely and optimistic scenarios."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Cash flow spreadsheet Build in a spreadsheet the cash flow for the purchase of a surface-mount assembly machine, with labor savings, maintenance, taxes and residual value, and calculate NPV and IRR with the spreadsheet functions. Activity 2 · Sensitivity Calculate how much the savings can fall before the NPV turns negative. Activity 3 · Final project Sketch the cash flow of the product of the degree’s final project."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Adding up flows from different years without discounting them. A peso five years from now is not worth a peso today. Mixing nominal flows with a real rate, or the other way around. The result makes no sense. Treating depreciation as a cash outflow. It matters only through its effect on the tax. Choosing between mutually exclusive projects by the IRR. A small project with a high IRR can add less value than a large one."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What NPV does investing one million to receive 300 thousand per year for five years have, at 12 %? About ARS 81,400. What is its internal rate of return? Around 15.2 %. And its simple and discounted payback period at 12 %? 3.3 years simple and about 4.5 years discounted. What does a negative NPV indicate? That the project yields less than the cutoff rate: the alternative is preferable."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Nassir Sapag Chain and Reinaldo Sapag Chain. Preparación y evaluación de proyectos. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) The most widely used text in the region for building and evaluating cash flows. Leland Blank and Anthony Tarquin. Engineering Economy. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería económica”.) The time value of money and the decision criteria, with exercises. Richard A. Brealey, Stewart C. Myers and Franklin Allen. Principles of Corporate Finance. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Principios de finanzas corporativas”.) The cost of capital and the foundations of net present value."},{"t":"The most important function in the subject","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#porque","x":"01 The most important function in the subject There is a precise reason why all of signal analysis revolves around the complex exponential, and it is not an aesthetic one. It is this: \\[ x(t) = e^{st} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; \\boxed{\\text{LTI system}} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; y(t) = H(s)\\,e^{st} \\] The waveform comes out the same as it went in. The only thing that changes is a complex factor, \\( H(s) \\), which depends on the system and on the value of \\( s \\). No other family of signals has that property. If a pulse goes in, something else comes out; if a triangle wave goes in, it comes out distorted. But if \\( e^{st} \\) goes in, \\( e^{st} \\) comes out, scaled and phase-shifted. Such signals are called eigenfunctions of the system, and they are the reason frequency analysis exists: if an arbitrary signal is written as a sum of complex exponentials, the output is built by adding up each one multiplied by its \\( H(s) \\). That is Fourier, and that is Laplace. Where it comes from Plugging \\( e^{st} \\) into the convolution integral: \\( y(t) = \\int h(\\tau)\\,e^{s(t-\\tau)}d\\tau = e^{st}\\int h(\\tau)e^{-s\\tau}d\\tau \\). The exponential comes out because \\( e^{s(t-\\tau)} = e^{st}e^{-s\\tau} \\), and what is left inside no longer depends on \\( t \\): it is \\( H(s) \\), the transform of the impulse response."},{"t":"The purely imaginary case","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#imaginaria","x":"02 The purely imaginary case With \\( s = j\\omega_0 \\), the exponential stays on the unit circle and rotates forever: \\[ e^{j\\omega_0 t} = \\cos(\\omega_0 t) + j\\operatorname{sin}(\\omega_0 t) \\] It is always periodic, with period \\( T_0 = 2\\pi/|\\omega_0| \\), and its magnitude is one at every instant: it is a phasor of constant amplitude. From this come the two Euler relations that are used constantly to go from sinusoids to exponentials and back: \\[ \\cos(\\omega_0 t) = \\frac{e^{j\\omega_0 t} + e^{-j\\omega_0 t}}{2}, \\qquad \\operatorname{sin}(\\omega_0 t) = \\frac{e^{j\\omega_0 t} - e^{-j\\omega_0 t}}{2j} \\] A real cosine is the sum of two phasors rotating in opposite directions. That is where “negative frequencies” come from: they are not an artifact, they are the phasor rotating the other way. The harmonically related exponentials \\( \\phi_k(t) = e^{jk\\omega_0 t} \\) share the same period \\( T_0 \\) for every integer \\( k \\), and they form the basis from which a Fourier series is built. In continuous time there are infinitely many distinct ones; in discrete time, as we saw, there are only \\( N \\)."},{"t":"The general case: the map of the s-plane","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#general","x":"03 The general case: the map of the s-plane With the full \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\), the exponential does two things at once: \\[ e^{st} = e^{\\sigma t}\\,e^{j\\omega t} = \\underbrace{e^{\\sigma t}}_{\\text{envelope}}\\big(\\cos\\omega t + j\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\big) \\] The real part of \\( s \\) sets the envelope and the imaginary part, the rotation speed. Each point of the \\( s \\)-plane corresponds to a different waveform, and that correspondence is the map you have to keep in your head for the rest of your degree. Lab · each point of the plane, a waveform Click the \\( s \\)-plane on the left and see which signal appears on the right. The marked points are the characteristic cases. Pick with the mouse Origin · constant On the jω axis · sustained oscillation Negative real axis · pure decay Left half-plane · damped sinusoid Right half-plane · grows without bound Also show the imaginary part"},{"t":"In discrete time","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#discreto","x":"04 In discrete time The discrete version is \\( x[n] = z^{n} \\), and with \\( z = e^{j\\Omega_0} \\) it becomes \\( e^{j\\Omega_0 n} \\). The differences from the continuous case were already anticipated and are worth repeating because they are the source of half the mistakes: Continuous time Discrete time Distinct frequencies Infinitely many: each \\( \\omega_0 \\) gives a different signal Only an interval of \\( 2\\pi \\) Periodicity Always, with \\( T_0 = 2\\pi/\\omega_0 \\) Only if \\( \\Omega_0/2\\pi \\) is rational Distinct harmonics Infinitely many Exactly \\( N \\) The “fastest” one Does not exist \\( \\Omega = \\pi \\): alternates +1 and −1 The third row is what gives rise to the DFT: since there are only \\( N \\) distinct harmonic exponentials, the representation of a periodic sequence of period \\( N \\) needs exactly \\( N \\) coefficients. Not an infinite sum: a finite sum, which a computer can calculate. The z-plane, which is the s-plane in disguise The mapping \\( z = e^{sT} \\) takes the imaginary axis of the \\( s \\)-plane to the unit circle of the \\( z \\)-plane, and the left half-plane to the interior of the circle. That is why the stability criterion changes in form but not in substance: where before it was “poles on the left”, now it is “poles inside the circle”."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Reading the s-plane on an oscilloscope Build a series RLC circuit and adjust \\( R \\) to obtain three cases: overdamped, critically damped and underdamped. For each one, measure the step response, extract \\( \\sigma \\) and \\( \\omega \\) from the envelope and the period, and locate the poles in the plane. Exercise 2 · The two phasors of a cosine With a two-channel quadrature generator, add two signals that rotate in opposite directions and verify that the result is a real cosine. It is the Euler relation, built with instruments. Exercise 3 · The eigenfunction, measured Inject a sinusoid into an RC filter and measure the amplitude and phase of the output. Check that the waveform is identical and that the input-output relationship matches \\( H(j\\omega) = 1/(1 + j\\omega RC) \\) in magnitude and phase, for five frequencies."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing negative frequencies do not exist. They are the phasor rotating the other way, and they are needed to build a real cosine. Confusing \\( |e^{st}| \\) with \\( e^{|s|t} \\). The magnitude depends only on \\( \\sigma \\): \\( |e^{st}| = e^{\\sigma t} \\). Assuming \\( e^{st} \\) with \\( \\sigma \\ne 0 \\) is periodic. If there is an envelope, it does not repeat. Forgetting the \\( 2j \\) of the sine. In the Euler relation the cosine carries a 2 and the sine a \\( 2j \\). Carrying the properties of the continuous case over to the discrete case. Periodicity and the number of harmonics work differently."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does it mean for \\( e^{st} \\) to be an eigenfunction of an LTI system? That the output is the same exponential multiplied by a complex number \\( H(s) \\). The shape does not change. What is \\( |e^{(-2+j5)t}| \\) at \\( t = 1 \\)? \\( e^{-2} \\approx 0.135 \\). The imaginary part only rotates; it does not affect the magnitude. Why does a real cosine need two exponentials? Because a single one rotates and has an imaginary part. Two rotating in opposite directions cancel their imaginary parts and leave a cosine. What signal corresponds to a point on the imaginary axis? An oscillation of constant amplitude: it neither grows nor dies out. It is the borderline case between stable and unstable. How many distinct harmonic exponentials are there in discrete time? Exactly \\( N \\). That is why the DFT has \\( N \\) coefficients and can be computed on a computer. Where does the left half-plane go under \\( z = e^{sT} \\)? To the interior of the unit circle. Hence the stability criterion for digital filters."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The complex exponential signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. Sections 1.3 and 3.2: the complex exponential and its role as an eigenfunction. John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Pearson, 2007. The discrete case, with the N harmonics and the road to the DFT. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Pearson, 2010. Reading the s-plane in terms of time response, with the damping maps."},{"t":"A square law instead of an exponential","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#cuadratica","x":"01 A square law instead of an exponential The field-effect transistor —the MOSFET and the JFET— amplifies with the same idea as the bipolar: an input voltage controls an output current. The difference lies in the shape of that relationship. In saturation, the drain current follows a parabola of the overdrive voltage \\( V_{ov} = V_{GS} - V_{th} \\) (in the JFET, \\( V_{GS} - V_P \\)): \\[ i_D = \\frac{k}{2}\\, (v_{GS} - V_{th})^2 \\] \\[ g_m = k\\, V_{ov} = \\frac{2\\, I_D}{V_{ov}} = \\sqrt{2\\, k\\, I_D} \\] The models of each device are in the units on the MOS transistor and the JFET. The comparison with the bipolar at equal current is the first design lesson: \\( g_m / I_D = 2 / V_{ov} \\) in the FET against \\( 1 / V_T \\) in the bipolar. With \\( V_{ov} = 0.2 \\) V, the FET gives 10 S/A and the bipolar 38.7: it amplifies almost four times less per milliampere. In exchange, its gate draws no current and, as you will see below, it is much more linear."},{"t":"The small-signal configurations","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#configuraciones","x":"02 The small-signal configurations Configuration Voltage gain Input resistance Output resistance Common source \\( -\\dfrac{g_m R_D}{1 + g_m R_S} \\) Practically infinite (that of the biasing circuit) \\( \\approx R_D \\) Common drain (source follower) \\( \\dfrac{g_m R_S}{1 + g_m R_S} Practically infinite \\( \\approx 1/g_m \\) Common gate \\( g_m R_D \\) \\( \\approx 1/g_m \\) \\( \\approx R_D \\) The source follower is less ideal than the emitter follower: since \\( g_m \\) is smaller, its gain is farther from 1 and its output resistance is higher. In an integrated circuit the body effect adds to this: the voltage between source and substrate raises the threshold and reduces the gain a little more."},{"t":"Large signal: the distortion of the parabola","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#distorsion","x":"03 Large signal: the distortion of the parabola With \\( v_{gs} = \\hat V \\sin \\omega t \\) added to the bias, the current is \\( \\frac{k}{2} (V_{ov} + \\hat V \\sin \\omega t)^2 \\). Expanding the square gives a term in \\( \\sin^2 \\omega t = (1 - \\cos 2\\omega t)/2 \\): a second harmonic, and no other. The ratio is exact, not an approximation: \\[ HD_2 = \\frac{\\hat V}{4\\, V_{ov}} \\] It holds as long as the transistor stays in saturation throughout the cycle: \\( \\hat V The comparison with the bipolar, \\( HD_2 \\approx \\hat V / 4 V_T \\), shows the other side of transconductance: with \\( V_{ov} = 0.2 \\) V the FET distorts almost eight times less for the same input. More \\( V_{ov} \\) means more linearity and less gain; less \\( V_{ov} \\), the opposite. Choosing \\( V_{ov} \\) is the central decision in FET design. With larger signals the limits appear: if \\( \\hat V \\) exceeds \\( V_{ov} \\), the transistor cuts off during part of the cycle; if the drain voltage falls too low, it enters the ohmic region. In both cases the output is clipped and all the harmonics appear. Lab · the common source with small and large signals A MOSFET in common source with a 12 V supply and a 1 V threshold. The current is calculated with the full model (saturation, ohmic region and cutoff) at each instant, without linearizing, and the harmonics of the output are analyzed. Quiescent current \\( I_D \\) = Overdrive voltage \\( V_{ov} \\) = Drain resistance \\( R_D \\) = Unbypassed source resistance \\( R_S \\) = Input amplitude "},{"t":"JFET, MOS and short channel","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#jfet-mos","x":"04 JFET, MOS and short channel The JFET follows the same parabola with \\( V_P \\) instead of \\( V_{th} \\) and has the advantage of low noise with high-impedance sources; its problem is parameter spread. The short-channel MOS departs from the parabola because of velocity saturation: its current grows almost linearly with \\( V_{ov} \\), which makes it more linear but with less \\( g_m \\). Below threshold, the MOS becomes exponential again, like a bipolar, with maximum \\( g_m/I_D \\): this is the regime of ultra-low-power circuits."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case Why a FET Input of a high-impedance instrument The gate does not load the source Low-noise radio-frequency amplifier GaAs and GaN transistors with very low noise at high frequencies Analog CMOS integrated circuit It is the only transistor available, and the design is done by choosing \\( V_{ov} \\) or \\( g_m/I_D \\) Tube and FET audio amplifiers Distortion that is mostly second harmonic has a characteristic sound"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Common source with a 2N7000 Bias a 2N7000 at 2 mA with \\( R_D = 2.2 \\) kΩ, measure \\( V_{GS} \\) and estimate \\( V_{ov} \\). Measure the gain with 10 mV at the input and compare it with \\( 2 I_D R_D / V_{ov} \\). Activity 2 · Second harmonic only With the oscilloscope's FFT, measure the second and third harmonics for three amplitudes. Verify that the second grows in proportion to the amplitude and that the third barely appears until clipping. Activity 3 · Comparing with the bipolar Build a common emitter with the same current and the same load resistance, and compare gain and distortion for the same input."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Expecting the gain of the bipolar from the FET. At equal current, \\( g_m \\) is several times smaller. Confusing the saturation of the FET with that of the bipolar. In the FET it is the amplifying region. Forgetting that the parabola only holds in saturation. If the drain voltage falls, the transistor enters the ohmic region and clips. Applying \\( \\hat V / 4V_T \\) to a FET. Its distortion scale is \\( V_{ov} \\), not \\( V_T \\)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment With \\( I_D = 1 \\) mA and \\( V_{ov} = 0.25 \\) V, what is \\( g_m \\)? \\( 2 \\times 1 / 0.25 = 8 \\) mS. What second harmonic does a common source with \\( V_{ov} = 0.4 \\) V and a 40 mV input produce? \\( 40 / (4 \\times 400) = 2.5 \\) %. Why does a square law not generate a third harmonic? Because squaring a sum produces only a term in \\( \\sin^2 \\), which has a DC component and a second harmonic. What gain does a source follower with \\( g_m R_S = 4 \\) have? \\( 4/5 = 0.8 \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Behzad Razavi. Design of Analog CMOS Integrated Circuits. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2017. The basic MOS configurations, with a physical intuition that is hard to find in any other text. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The MOS as an amplifier in parallel with the bipolar, for comparing them. Willy M. C. Sansen. Analog Design Essentials. Springer, 2006. The distortion of MOS stages and design with \\( g_m/I_D \\), in the form of annotated slides."},{"t":"Light through a thread of glass","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#por-que","x":"01 Light through a thread of glass An optical fiber is a dielectric waveguide that works at frequencies of about 200 THz. It has a silica glass core surrounded by a cladding of slightly lower refractive index, and the light is trapped in the core. Compared with a copper cable it has three decisive advantages: it loses 0.2 dB per kilometer instead of tens of dB, it supports terahertz bandwidths, and it is not affected by electromagnetic interference or by ground potential differences between its ends. That is why almost all of the world's data traffic travels over fiber: the submarine cables between continents, the backbones between cities, the connection of cellular base stations and, more and more, the last stretch to every home."},{"t":"Total internal reflection and numerical aperture","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#guiado","x":"02 Total internal reflection and numerical aperture A ray that travels along the core and reaches the cladding at an angle greater than the critical angle is entirely reflected, without loss, as seen in geometric optics. This limits the angle at which light can enter through the face of the fiber and be guided: \\[ NA = \\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\] The numerical aperture NA sets the acceptance cone of the fiber. With \\( n_1 = 1.48 \\) in the core and \\( n_2 = 1.46 \\) in the cladding, NA = 0.24 and the acceptance half-angle is 14°. A large aperture makes it easier to couple the light from a cheap source but, as will be seen shortly, it allows more distinct paths inside the fiber."},{"t":"Multimode and single-mode","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#modos","x":"03 Multimode and single-mode As in a metal waveguide, only certain field configurations propagate in the fiber, the modes. How many fit is given by the normalized frequency, which combines the core radius \\( a \\), the aperture and the wavelength: \\[ V = \\frac{2\\pi a}{\\lambda}\\, NA \\] With \\( V < 2.405 \\) a single mode propagates: the fiber is single-mode. With a large V, a step-index fiber supports about \\( V^2/2 \\) modes. Fiber Core / cladding Aperture Use Multimode OM3 / OM4 50 / 125 µm 0.2 Data centers and building networks, up to hundreds of meters Single-mode G.652 8.2 / 125 µm Around 0.12 Backbones, FTTH access, links of tens of kilometers Single-mode G.657 Similar to the G.652 Higher Indoor installations, tolerates tight bends With an 8.2 µm core and NA = 0.12, V is 2.36 at 1310 nm: the fiber is single-mode above about 1285 nm, its cutoff wavelength. The 50 µm multimode fiber at 850 nm has V ≈ 37 and hundreds of modes."},{"t":"Attenuation and windows","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#atenuacion","x":"04 Attenuation and windows Light is lost through absorption by the glass and its impurities, and above all through Rayleigh scattering: microscopic fluctuations in the density of the glass deflect the light with an intensity proportional to \\( 1/\\lambda^4 \\). Between 850 and 1550 nm this loss drops by a factor of 11. Toward the far infrared the absorption of the silica itself grows, and between the two lies the minimum, near 1550 nm. Window Typical loss Source and fiber 850 nm About 3 dB/km Low-cost VCSEL on multimode 1310 nm About 0.35 dB/km Laser on single-mode; almost zero chromatic dispersion 1550 nm About 0.2 dB/km DFB laser on single-mode; amplified with erbium-doped fiber To the loss of the fiber are added that of each fusion splice (about 0.1 dB) and that of each connector (up to 0.5 dB), and bends that are too tight, which let light escape from the core."},{"t":"Dispersion: the pulse broadens","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#dispersion","x":"05 Dispersion: the pulse broadens A light pulse arrives wider than it left, and if it broadens too much it overlaps the next one. There are two main causes: Modal dispersion, in multimode fibers: each mode travels a different path and arrives at a different moment. In a step-index fiber with \\( n_1 - n_2 = 0.02 \\), the difference is about 67 ns per kilometer. Modern multimode fibers are graded-index: the index drops toward the edge of the core, the modes that travel along the outside go faster and the difference shrinks by orders of magnitude. It is specified with the bandwidth–distance product: 2000 MHz·km in an OM3 at 850 nm. Chromatic dispersion, in all of them: the velocity depends on the wavelength, and no source emits just one. \\[ \\Delta t = D \\cdot L \\cdot \\Delta\\lambda \\] \\( D \\) is the dispersion coefficient of the fiber, which in a G.652 is almost zero at 1310 nm and about 17 ps/(nm·km) at 1550 nm. With 50 km and a source 0.1 nm wide in spectrum, the pulse broadens by 85 ps. At 10 Gb/s each bit lasts 100 ps, so chromatic dispersion limits long links at 1550 nm. A 10 Gb/s transmitter without compensation tolerates an accumulated dispersion \\( D \\cdot L \\) of the order of 1000 ps/nm, about 60 km of G.652. Beyond that, compensating fibers or coherent receivers that correct it by digital processing are used."},{"t":"Emitters, receivers and the link budget","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#enlace","x":"06 Emitters, receivers and the link budget The source is an LED, a VCSEL or a semiconductor laser, and the receiver, a PIN or avalanche photodiode, as seen in optoelectronics. The design of a link starts with a power budget in decibels, just like the one for power in lines: all the losses are subtracted from the transmitter power, and what arrives has to exceed the receiver sensitivity with a margin, typically 3 dB, for aging and future repairs. Lab · the budget of a fiber link The optical power in dBm along the link: two 0.5 dB connectors, a splice every 2 km (the length of a reel) and the slope of the fiber attenuation. The receiver is a PIN with the typical sensitivity for the chosen speed. The values are typical catalog values. 850 nm · multimode OM3 · VCSEL 1310 nm · single-mode G.652 · laser 1550 nm · single-mode G.652 · DFB laser 1 Gb/s 10 Gb/s Link length ="},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#electronica","x":"07 Where it appears in electronics System How it uses fiber Fiber to the home (GPON) One single-mode fiber per house, with passive optical splitters; downstream at 1490 nm and upstream at 1310 nm Data center Pluggable transceivers with VCSELs on multimode for the short runs Submarine cable Single-mode at 1550 nm with erbium amplifiers every 50 to 100 km and tens of wavelengths per fiber Cellular base station Fiber between the baseband unit and the radio on the tower Electrical substation Total galvanic isolation between equipment at very different ground potentials Installation, connectors and field measurements are covered in optical fiber, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Activity 1 · Numerical aperture Illuminate a multimode fiber with a red laser, project the output cone onto a screen and measure its diameter at a known distance. Calculate the half-angle and the numerical aperture. Activity 2 · Loss budget With a source and an optical power meter, measure the loss of a reel of fiber, of a connector and of a splice. Build the budget of a link and compare it with the end-to-end measurement. Activity 3 · Bends Wind a single-mode fiber around mandrels of decreasing diameter and measure the increase in loss at 1310 and at 1550 nm. Explain why 1550 nm is more sensitive."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Mixing multimode and single-mode. A single-mode transceiver on a multimode fiber, or the reverse, loses tens of dB or suffers modal dispersion. Looking at the tip of a connected fiber. Radiation at 1310 and 1550 nm is invisible and can damage the retina. Forgetting dispersion in the budget. At 10 Gb/s over multimode, the power would be enough for kilometers but modal dispersion limits it to a few hundred meters. Dirty connectors. A dust particle on an 8 µm core adds decibels; they are cleaned and inspected before every connection."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment What is the numerical aperture of a fiber with \\( n_1 = 1.48 \\) and \\( n_2 = 1.46 \\)? \\( \\sqrt{1.48^2 - 1.46^2} \\approx 0.24 \\), with an acceptance half-angle of 14°. Is a fiber with an 8.2 µm core and NA = 0.12 single-mode at 1310 nm? Yes: \\( V = 2\\pi \\cdot 4.1 \\cdot 0.12 / 1.31 \\approx 2.36 < 2.405 \\). How much arrives from a 0 dBm transmitter through 40 km of fiber at 0.25 dB/km with 1 dB of connectors? \\( 0 - 10 - 1 = -11 \\) dBm. Why do 10 Gb/s links over multimode reach only about 300 m? Because of modal dispersion: with 2000 MHz·km and about 6.7 GHz of required bandwidth, the maximum distance is 0.3 km, much less than the power would allow."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Gerd Keiser. Optical Fiber Communications. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2015. The most widely used course text: fibers, sources, detectors and link design with power and rise-time budgets. Govind P. Agrawal. Fiber-Optic Communication Systems. 5th ed., Wiley, 2021. The rigorous treatment of dispersion, optical amplifiers and coherent systems. John M. Senior. Optical Fiber Communications: Principles and Practice. 3rd ed., Pearson, 2009. Very complete on the physics of the fiber and on components, with many numerical examples."},{"t":"The filter template","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#plantilla","x":"01 The filter template A filter is specified with a template: in the passband, up to \\( \\omega_p \\), the attenuation cannot exceed \\( A_p \\); in the stopband, from \\( \\omega_s \\) on, it has to be at least \\( A_s \\). Between the two lies the transition band. The narrower the transition and the more demanding \\( A_p \\) and \\( A_s \\), the higher the order needed: more poles, more components. Highpass, bandpass and bandstop filters are designed from a normalized lowpass with a frequency transformation, so everything starts with the lowpass. The basic filters are presented in the technician program, in filters."},{"t":"The approximations","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#aproximaciones","x":"02 The approximations The ideal filter —gain 1 up to the cutoff and 0 after it— cannot be built. An approximation is a realizable transfer function that comes close to it in a particular way, and each one chooses what to sacrifice: Approximation Passband Transition Step response Butterworth Maximally flat Moderate Moderate overshoot Chebyshev With ripple of \\( A_p \\) Steeper for the same order Greater overshoot and ringing Inverse Chebyshev Flat Steep, with zeros in the stopband Moderate Elliptic (Cauer) With ripple The steepest possible The worst Bessel Rolls off gently Very slow Almost no overshoot: constant group delay \\[ |H(j\\omega)|^2 = \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{2n}} \\] \\[ |H(j\\omega)|^2 = \\frac{1}{1 + \\varepsilon^2\\, T_n^2(\\omega)} \\] Butterworth normalized to −3 dB at \\( \\omega = 1 \\), and Chebyshev with the polynomials \\( T_n \\), normalized to the edge of the ripple, with \\( \\varepsilon^2 = 10^{A_p/10} - 1 \\). Outside the passband both fall at 20n dB per decade, but Chebyshev starts from lower down. The minimum order comes from the template: \\[ n_{B} \\ge \\frac{\\log\\sqrt{\\frac{10^{A_s/10} - 1}{10^{A_p/10} - 1}}}{\\log(\\omega_s/\\omega_p)} \\] \\[ n_{C} \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{arccosh}\\sqrt{\\frac{10^{A_s/10} - 1}{10^{A_p/10} - 1}}}{\\operatorname{arccosh}(\\omega_s/\\omega_p)} \\] For 40 dB of rejection at twice the cutoff frequency, 7 Butterworth poles are needed (with 3 dB in the passband) or 5 Chebyshev poles with 0.5 dB of ripple."},{"t":"Passive ladder filters","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#pasivos","x":"03 Passive ladder filters The classic passive filter is an LC ladder between the generator resistance and the load resistance, with series inductors and shunt capacitors: the Cauer form from network functions. Its values are tabulated in normalized form, for 1 Ω and 1 rad/s; for Butterworth they are \\( g_k = 2\\sin\\frac{(2k - 1)\\pi}{2n} \\): a third-order filter has 1 H, 2 F and 1 H. They are denormalized by dividing the inductances and capacitances by \\( \\omega_c \\) and scaling by the impedance. Doubly terminated ladders have a notable advantage: their response is very insensitive to component tolerances."},{"t":"Active filters","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#activos","x":"04 Active filters At low frequencies inductors are large, expensive and far from ideal. An active filter replaces the ladder with a cascade of second-order sections using operational amplifiers, each one with a pair of poles. The most widely used is the Sallen-Key: with unity gain and equal resistors \\( R \\), the capacitors set the frequency and the quality factor of the section: \\[ C_1 = \\frac{2Q}{\\omega_0 R} \\] \\[ C_2 = \\frac{1}{2Q\\,\\omega_0 R} \\] \\( C_1 \\) is the feedback capacitor and \\( C_2 \\) the one to ground. Each section realizes a pair of poles with its \\( \\omega_0 \\) and its \\( Q \\); if the order is odd, a first-order RC network is added. The sections are ordered with increasing Q, so that the high-Q ones do not saturate on signals that the next section was going to attenuate. The op amp has to have a gain-bandwidth product well above the \\( Q \\cdot f_0 \\) of each section; otherwise, the response is distorted. Lab · Butterworth versus Chebyshev The response of a normalized lowpass of the chosen type and order, with the specification template shaded. Below, the minimum order to meet it and the unity-gain Sallen-Key sections for a 1 kHz cutoff with 10 kΩ resistors. Butterworth Chebyshev 0.5 dB Chebyshev 1 dB 40 dB rejection at 2 times the cutoff 60 dB rejection at 3 times the cutoff 30 dB rejection at 1.5 times the cutoff Order n ="},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Application Typical filter Anti-aliasing before a converter Active Butterworth or Bessel of order 2 to 8 Transmitter output stage LC ladder, Chebyshev or elliptic, for the harmonics Pulse measurement or high-quality audio Bessel, for its step response without overshoot Loudspeaker crossover Linkwitz-Riley: two Butterworth filters in cascade"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · A fourth-order Butterworth Build the two Sallen-Key sections from the lab for 1 kHz, measure the frequency response and compare it with the theoretical one. Activity 2 · Step response Compare the square-wave response of a Butterworth and a 1 dB Chebyshev of the same order. Activity 3 · An LC filter Denormalize a fifth-order LC Butterworth for 10 MHz and 50 Ω, build it and measure it with a network analyzer."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mixing up the normalizations. Butterworth is normalized to −3 dB; Chebyshev, to the edge of the ripple. Using a slow op amp in a high-Q section. The peak shifts and grows. Ordering the sections wrongly. The high-Q ones go last. Choosing by the frequency response alone. If the waveform matters, you have to look at the group delay."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How much does a fourth-order Butterworth attenuate at twice its cutoff frequency? \\( 10\\log(1 + 2^8) \\approx 24.1 \\) dB. What Q values do the sections of a fourth-order Butterworth have? 0.541 and 1.307, both with \\( \\omega_0 = 1 \\). What capacitors does a unity-gain Sallen-Key with Q = 0.707, 1 kHz and 10 kΩ use? \\( C_1 = 2 \\cdot 0.707 / (2\\pi \\cdot 1000 \\cdot 10^4) \\approx 22.5 \\) nF and \\( C_2 \\approx 11.3 \\) nF. Why does a Chebyshev need a lower order than a Butterworth? Because it accepts ripple in the passband and in exchange falls faster in the transition."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Active and passive analog filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Arthur B. Williams and Fred J. Taylor. Electronic Filter Design Handbook. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2006. The tables of poles, sections and normalized ladders for all the approximations. Rolf Schaumann, Haiqiao Xiao and Mac E. Van Valkenburg. Design of Analog Filters. 2nd ed., Oxford University Press, 2009. The design of active and passive filters with the complete theory. Texas Instruments. Op Amps for Everyone, active filters chapter. Texas Instruments, various editions. The practical design of Sallen-Key and multiple-feedback filters, with coefficient tables."},{"t":"Two families of digital filters","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#dos-familias","x":"01 Two families of digital filters A digital filter is a difference equation that a processor computes at every sample. If it uses only inputs, its impulse response is finite ( FIR ); if it feeds back previous outputs, it is infinite ( IIR ). The choice between the two is the first design decision: FIR IIR Stability Always stable: it has no poles away from the origin It has to be ensured: the poles inside the unit circle Phase Can have exactly linear phase Nonlinear phase, like analog filters Order for a steep transition High: tens or hundreds of coefficients Low: a few poles are enough Cost per sample One multiplication per coefficient Few multiplications Sensitivity to quantization Low High: rounding a coefficient moves the poles"},{"t":"Linear phase and group delay","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#fase","x":"02 Linear phase and group delay The group delay \\( \\tau(\\Omega) = -d\\varphi/d\\Omega \\) says how many samples each frequency takes to pass through. If it is the same for all of them, the waveform comes out intact, just delayed. An FIR with symmetric coefficients has exactly linear phase: \\[ h[n] = h[M - 1 - n] \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\tau = \\frac{M - 1}{2} \\] A symmetric FIR of \\( M \\) coefficients delays all frequencies by the same amount: \\( (M-1)/2 \\) samples. An IIR delays each frequency differently, and distorts pulses even if its magnitude is flat. That is why FIR filters dominate in digital communications, professional audio and instrumentation, where the shape of the pulses matters, and IIR filters where cost or latency matters: control, tone detection, low-power equalizers."},{"t":"The two simplest filters","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#ejemplos","x":"03 The two simplest filters \\[ y[n] = \\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1} x[n-k] \\] \\[ y[n] = \\alpha\\, x[n] + (1 - \\alpha)\\, y[n-1] \\] The moving average is an FIR of \\( M \\) equal coefficients; exponential smoothing, a one-pole IIR at \\( 1 - \\alpha \\). Both are lowpass and both reduce white noise: the variance drops to \\( 1/M \\) in the first and to \\( \\alpha/(2 - \\alpha) \\) in the second. The moving average needs to store \\( M \\) samples and, computed recursively, two additions per sample; the exponential needs a single variable and one multiplication. They are the filters in almost all measurement firmware. Lab · moving average versus exponential smoothing At the top, the magnitude of the frequency response of the two filters from 0 to π. Below, their response to a step with white noise (the same noise sequence for both). You adjust the length of the average and the coefficient of the exponential. Moving average, M = Exponential, α ="},{"t":"Structures and implementation","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#estructuras","x":"04 Structures and implementation The same transfer function can be computed with different structures: direct form, transposed direct form, cascade of second-order sections (biquads) or lattice. Mathematically they are equivalent; numerically they are not. A high-order IIR in direct form is very sensitive to the rounding of its coefficients, and in practice it is always implemented as a cascade of biquads, just as active filters are built with second-order sections. On a microcontroller without floating point, work is done in fixed point, with scaled integers. You have to watch out for overflow in the sums, for rounding, which adds noise, and for limit cycles: small oscillations that an IIR sustains through rounding even when the input is zero. Signal processors multiply and accumulate in one cycle, with wide accumulators to avoid overflow, as seen in processor architecture."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Filtering a measurement Read a noisy sensor with a microcontroller, apply a moving average and an exponential with the same noise reduction, and compare their response to an abrupt change. Activity 2 · Linear phase Filter a square wave with a symmetric FIR and with an IIR of the same cutoff frequency, and compare the shape of the edges on the oscilloscope. Activity 3 · Fixed point Implement the exponential in 16-bit fixed point and find the smallest α that still converges to the final value."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the filter delay. A 32-sample average delays by 15.5 samples: in a control loop it can destabilize it. Implementing a high-order IIR in direct form. The rounding of the coefficients makes it unstable. Using a very small α in fixed point. The product is rounded to zero and the output stalls short of the final value. Believing that the moving average is a good lowpass filter. Its sidelobes let quite a lot through; it is good against noise, not for separating frequencies."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How much does a symmetric 31-coefficient FIR delay at 48 kHz? 15 samples: 0.31 ms. What α gives the same noise reduction as an 8-sample average? \\( \\alpha/(2-\\alpha) = 1/8 \\Rightarrow \\alpha = 2/9 \\approx 0.22 \\). Where is the pole of the exponential with α = 0.1? At \\( z = 0.9 \\): inside the circle, stable. Why is an FIR always stable? Because all its poles are at the origin: the output is a finite sum of bounded inputs."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"IIR and FIR digital filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. FIR and IIR filters explained intuitively, including structures and fixed point. Alan V. Oppenheim and Ronald W. Schafer. Discrete-Time Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Pearson, 2010. The complete theory of structures and of the effects of quantization. Steven W. Smith. The Scientist and Engineer's Guide to Digital Signal Processing. California Technical Publishing, 1997. The moving-average chapter is the best analysis of why it is so useful against noise."},{"t":"From atomic levels to bands","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#bandas","x":"01 From atomic levels to bands The unit on atomic models ended with a pending idea: when \\( 10^{22} \\) atoms come together in a crystal, Pauli forces the single atomic level of each one to split into as many levels as there are atoms, so close together that they form a continuous band of allowed energies. Between two allowed bands there can be a band gap, of width \\( E_g \\), where no possible state exists. All the electricity of a material depends on how this scheme is arranged. Material Bands \\( E_g \\) Example Conductor The valence band is partially filled, or overlaps the conduction band — Copper, aluminum Semiconductor Full valence band, narrow band gap: thermal agitation is enough to cross it 0.5 to 1.5 eV Silicon 1.12 eV · germanium 0.66 eV · gallium arsenide 1.42 eV Insulator Full valence band, wide band gap: almost nobody crosses it > 4 eV Diamond, 5.5 eV The difference between a semiconductor and an insulator is not one of nature, it is one of the size of \\( E_g \\) compared with \\( kT \\): at room temperature, \\( kT \\approx 26 \\) meV, an \\( E_g \\) of 1.1 eV still lets a fraction —small, but not zero— of electrons cross on their own."},{"t":"The effective mass","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#masaefectiva","x":"02 The effective mass Inside a crystal, an electron feels not only the external force: it also feels the periodic potential of all the nuclei. The net result can be summarized, for most calculations, in a single number: the effective mass \\( m^* \\), which replaces \\( m_e \\) in \\( F=m^*a \\) and makes the electron in a crystal behave like a free particle, but lighter or heavier depending on the curvature of its energy band. Material \\( m^*_e / m_e \\) (electrons) \\( m^*_h / m_e \\) (holes) Silicon 0.26 0.39 Germanium 0.12 0.30 Gallium arsenide 0.067 0.45 The electron in gallium arsenide weighs, for practical purposes, fifteen times less than a free one. It responds faster to an electric field and reaches a higher velocity before colliding with the lattice: that is why GaAs is used in the highest-frequency transistors, even though it is more expensive and harder to work with than silicon."},{"t":"Intrinsic carriers and the law of mass action","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#masasa","x":"03 Intrinsic carriers and the law of mass action In a pure crystal —with no added impurity—, thermal agitation moves some electrons from the valence band to the conduction band. Each electron that crosses leaves behind a hole: an empty state that behaves, for almost all purposes, like a mobile positive charge. The concentration of each one, in that pure material, is the intrinsic carrier concentration \\( n_i \\). \\[ n_i \\propto T^{3/2} e^{-E_g/2kT} \\qquad\\qquad n\\,p = n_i^{\\,2} \\] The second equality, the law of mass action, always holds in thermal equilibrium, doped or not: if something makes \\( n \\) go up, \\( p \\) goes down to keep the product constant. It is the most widely used equation in all of device physics. Lab · carriers, Fermi level and the law of mass action You choose the material, doping type and concentration; the band diagram places the Fermi level and the panel calculates \\( n \\), \\( p \\) and \\( n_i \\), verifying the law of mass action in each case. germanium · E_g = 0.66 eV silicon · E_g = 1.12 eV gallium arsenide · E_g = 1.42 eV intrinsic (undoped) n-type doped (donors) p-type doped (acceptors) Doping concentration = 1.00 · 10¹⁶ cm⁻³ Temperature = 300 K"},{"t":"Doping and the Fermi level","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#dopado","x":"04 Doping and the Fermi level Adding impurities —phosphorus to donate electrons, boron to accept them, in silicon— does not change \\( n_i \\), which depends only on the material and the temperature. What changes is how far \\( n \\) departs from \\( p \\), and with that, where the Fermi level falls relative to the midpoint of the band gap, \\( E_i \\). \\[ E_F - E_i = kT \\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{n}{n_i}\\right) \\] n-type doping raises \\( E_F \\) toward the conduction band; p-type doping lowers it toward the valence band. A typical extrinsic semiconductor, with \\( 10^{16} \\) donors per cm³ in silicon, raises \\( E_F \\) about 0.35 eV above \\( E_i \\): much closer to the conduction band than to the middle. The same calculation explains why a semiconductor device stops working if it is heated too much: \\( n_i \\) grows exponentially with \\( T \\), and a point is reached —the intrinsic temperature — at which it exceeds the doping concentration. From then on the material forgets that it was doped and behaves as intrinsic: germanium, with its narrow band gap, reaches that temperature well before silicon, and that is one of the reasons germanium practically disappeared from modern electronics."},{"t":"The end of the bridge","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#electronica","x":"05 The end of the bridge This unit closes the subject where its curriculum description promised: in silicon. Each earlier topic contributed a piece that this last one uses without explaining it again. The photon from the photoelectric effect unit is the light that a photodiode converts into current by crossing this very band gap. The tunneling of the barrier is the leakage through the gate oxide of a MOSFET. The Fermi-Dirac statistics of the previous unit is, quite literally, the formula \\( n = N_c e^{-(E_c-E_F)/kT} \\) that was just used in the lab on this page. And the Pauli exclusion principle, two units back, is the ultimate reason bands exist instead of isolated levels. From here on, everything rests on this A diode is a junction between an n-doped region and a p-doped one, with their Fermi levels forced to equalize at equilibrium. A bipolar transistor is two such junctions, back to back. A MOSFET controls, with an electric field, how many carriers are available in a region of the crystal. None of those three sentences makes sense without the band gap, the Fermi level and the law of mass action on this page."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying the law of mass action With the lab on this page, for n-type doped silicon at \\( 10^{17} \\) cm⁻³, note down \\( n \\) and \\( p \\). Verify by hand that \\( n\\,p = n_i^2 \\) and calculate how many orders of magnitude separate the majority carriers from the minority ones. Activity 2 · The intrinsic temperature Raise the temperature until \\( n_i \\) of germanium approaches \\( 10^{16} \\) cm⁻³. Repeat for silicon. Compare the two temperatures and explain why germanium diodes fail sooner with heat. Activity 3 · Where the Fermi level falls Calculate \\( E_F - E_i \\) for p-type doped silicon at \\( 10^{15} \\), \\( 10^{17} \\) and \\( 10^{19} \\) cm⁻³. Plot it against the logarithm of the concentration and verify that the relationship is linear."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Thinking that doping changes \\( n_i \\). \\( n_i \\) depends only on the material and the temperature; doping changes \\( n \\) and \\( p \\) separately, not their product. Forgetting the law of mass action when calculating the minority carrier. Once the majority carrier is known, the minority one follows directly from \\( p = n_i^2/n \\). Confusing effective mass with the actual mass of the electron. It is a parameter that summarizes the effect of the crystal, and it can be larger, smaller or —in some bands— even negative. Believing that the Fermi level is an occupied energy level. In a semiconductor it typically falls right in the band gap, where there is no state at all. Ignoring the temperature dependence of \\( n_i \\) when designing a circuit. It is exponential, and it is the cause of a good share of thermal failures in power electronics. Thinking that germanium is inferior to silicon in everything. Its higher \\( n_i \\) is a thermal disadvantage, but its narrow band gap also gives a lower forward voltage drop, useful in some signal diodes."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What distinguishes a semiconductor from an insulator? The size of the band gap \\( E_g \\): narrow in the semiconductor (thermal agitation crosses it), wide in the insulator. What does the effective mass represent? The net effect of the periodic potential of the crystal on the electron, summarized in a single number that replaces the actual mass in \\( F=m^*a \\). What does the law of mass action say? That the product \\( n\\,p = n_i^2 \\) is constant in thermal equilibrium, for a given material and temperature, doped or not. What happens to the Fermi level with n-type doping? It rises from the midpoint of the band gap toward the conduction band. Why can a semiconductor device stop working if it is heated too much? Because \\( n_i \\) grows exponentially with temperature and, past the intrinsic temperature, exceeds the doping concentration: the material stops behaving as extrinsic. Why is gallium arsenide used in high-frequency transistors? Because its electron effective mass is much lower than that of silicon, so its electrons respond faster to an electric field."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Semiconductor physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Simon M. Sze and Kwok K. Ng. Physics of Semiconductor Devices. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2007. The reference book for this whole unit: bands, effective mass, carrier statistics and the law of mass action, with the tables of constants for each material. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2014. A more gradual introduction to the same topics, designed for the first devices course that follows this subject. Charles Kittel. Introduction to Solid State Physics. 8th ed., Wiley, 2004. The origin of the band structure from the crystal, with the detail that this unit takes for granted. Robert F. Pierret. Semiconductor Device Fundamentals. Addison-Wesley, 1996. The classic treatment of the Fermi level and the intrinsic temperature, with the \\( n_i(T) \\) graphs for the most widely used materials."},{"t":"Pressure","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#presion","x":"01 Pressure A fluid at rest cannot sustain shear stresses: it only exerts forces perpendicular to any surface it touches. That force per unit area is the pressure, and it is a scalar: at a point it has the same value in all directions. \\[ p = \\frac{F}{A} \\quad [ \\mathrm{Pa} ] = [ N / m^2 ] \\] Unit Equivalent to Where it is used 1 Pa 1 N/m² International System unit 1 bar 10⁵ Pa Pneumatics and industry 1 atm \\( 101 325 Pa \\approx 1.013 bar \\) Standard atmospheric pressure 1 mmHg (torr) 133.3 Pa Medicine and vacuum 1 psi 6895 Pa Gauges of Anglo-Saxon origin Absolute and gauge pressure Most pressure gauges measure above atmospheric: that is gauge pressure. Absolute pressure is the sum of the two. A tire “at 2 bar” actually has 3 bar absolute, and that distinction matters when the gas equation is used."},{"t":"Hydrostatic pressure","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#hidrostatica","x":"02 Hydrostatic pressure In a liquid at rest the pressure increases with depth, and does not depend on the shape of the container or on the amount of liquid: \\[ p = p_0 + \\rho g h \\] Only the density and the depth count. This is the hydrostatic paradox: a thin tube can exert the same pressure as a lake. In water, every 10 meters of depth adds roughly one atmosphere. That is why communicating vessels equalize their levels, and why a mason’s water level works with a transparent hose: two points at the same pressure are at the same height. Pascal’s principle A change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished to all its points. In a hydraulic press, F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂: with areas in a ratio of 1 to 100, a force is multiplied by a hundred. What is not multiplied is the work, because the large piston travels a hundred times less."},{"t":"Archimedes’ principle","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#arquimedes","x":"03 Archimedes’ principle Since pressure grows with depth, the lower face of a submerged body receives more pressure than the upper one. That difference produces a net upward force: \\[ E = \\rho_{\\mathrm{fluid}} \\, V_{\\mathrm{submerged}} \\, g \\] The buoyant force is the weight of the displaced fluid, and does not depend on the weight of the body. If… Then Submerged fraction ρ body < ρ fluid It floats ρ body /ρ fluid ρ body = ρ fluid Equilibrium at any depth All of it ρ body > ρ fluid It sinks All of it, and rests on the bottom A floating body does so with exactly the fraction of its volume that equals its relative density: an iceberg, with ρ = 917 kg/m³ in seawater of 1025, leaves less than 11% of its volume above the surface. Lab · buoyancy and hydrostatic pressure Change the densities and see how far the cube sinks. The gauge measures the pressure at the depth you choose. Density of the liquid 1000 kg/m³ Density of the cube 700 kg/m³ Edge of the cube 20 cm Depth of the gauge 1.00 m"},{"t":"Measuring pressure","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#medicion","x":"04 Measuring pressure Instrument Principle Output U-tube manometer Liquid column: \\( p = \\rho \\cdot g \\cdot \\Delta h \\) Visual reading Mercury barometer Column balanced by the atmosphere: 760 mm Visual reading Bourdon tube A curved tube straightens when pressurized Needle Piezoresistive sensor Strain gauges on a silicon diaphragm Electrical signal Capacitive sensor The diaphragm changes a capacitance Electrical signal The last two are pure electronics: a BMP280 measures atmospheric pressure with a silicon diaphragm and delivers the value over I²C, and from it the altitude is calculated. The same principle, with a different range, is in the oil pressure sensor of an engine and in a water tank sensor. Surface tension At the interface between a liquid and another medium a force per unit length appears that tends to minimize the surface. It explains spherical drops, capillarity and, in electronics, the behavior of molten solder: a well-made solder joint forms a concave meniscus because the solder wets the copper."},{"t":"What it is good for in electronics","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#electronica","x":"05 What it is good for in electronics Level and pressure sensors. Measuring the height of a tank is measuring ρ·g·h with a sensor at the bottom. Barometric altimeters. A drone estimates its height with a pressure sensor with centimeter resolution. Sealed equipment. A submersible enclosure is designed for the pressure at its working depth, and its IP rating is tested at that pressure. Liquid cooling. The circuit is sized with static pressures and head losses. Industrial pneumatics. The actuators of an automated line are driven by solenoid valves and calculated with F = p·A."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Density by flotation Measure what fraction of a body is submerged in water and deduce its density. Compare with the value obtained by weighing it and measuring its volume by displacement. Activity 2 · Buoyant force with a scale Weigh an object in air and submerged. The difference is the buoyant force, and from it you get the volume of the body. It is the method by which Archimedes tested the crown. Activity 3 · Pressure sensor and column height With a differential pressure sensor connected to a microcontroller, measure the pressure at the bottom of a graduated cylinder at different water heights. Plot p against h: the slope is ρ·g, and from it you obtain the density of the liquid."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Believing that pressure depends on the amount of liquid and not on the depth. Mixing absolute with gauge pressure. Thinking that the buoyant force depends on the weight of the body. It depends on the submerged volume and on the density of the fluid. Forgetting that the buoyant force also acts in air. It is negligible for a solid, but it is what holds up a balloon. Assuming the hydraulic press multiplies energy. It multiplies force and divides travel: the work is conserved. Using g = 10 m/s² in a precision calculation and carrying a 2% error."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the pressure at 10 m depth in water? p = ρgh = 1000·9.8·10 = 98 kPa gauge, almost one atmosphere. In absolute terms, about 199 kPa. A 700 kg/m³ body floats in water. What fraction is submerged? 70%: the submerged fraction is the ratio of densities. What is the buoyant force on a cube with a 20 cm edge fully submerged in water? V = 8·10⁻³ m³, so E = 1000·8·10⁻³·9.8 = 78.4 N, the weight of 8 liters of water. In a press with A₂/A₁ = 50, what force is needed to lift 5000 N? F₁ = 5000/50 = 100 N. The small piston must travel 50 times farther. Why does a steel ship float? Because its average density —steel plus the air enclosed by the hull— is lower than that of water. What matters is the density of the whole, not that of the material. How does a barometric sensor measure altitude? By the drop in pressure with height: near sea level, roughly 12 Pa per meter. A sensor with 1 Pa resolution can distinguish less than 10 cm."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Fluids at rest","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapter 14 covers pressure, Pascal and Archimedes with clear examples. Yunus A. Çengel and John M. Cimbala. Fluid Mechanics: Fundamentals and Applications. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2018 (Spanish edition, “Mecánica de fluidos: fundamentos y aplicaciones”). The engineering text: chapters 2 and 3, with real measuring instruments. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). Good treatment of surface tension and capillarity."},{"t":"Second-degree polynomials, written with a matrix","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#que-es","x":"01 Second-degree polynomials, written with a matrix A quadratic form is a homogeneous polynomial of degree two: all its terms have degree two. In two variables, q(x, y) = a x² + b xy + c y². What makes it interesting is that it can be written with a symmetric matrix, splitting the cross term in half: \\[ q (x, y) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc} x & y \\end{array} \\right] \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc} a & \\frac{b}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{2} & c \\end{array} \\right] \\left[ \\begin{array}{c} x \\\\ y \\end{array} \\right] = X^T A X \\] The matrix is deliberately built symmetric: that way its eigenvalues are real and its eigenvectors perpendicular. Writing it this way is not a notational whim: it turns a question about a polynomial —is it always positive? what curve does it describe?— into a question about a matrix, which we already know how to answer."},{"t":"The conics","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#conicas","x":"02 The conics Adding linear terms and a constant gives the general second-degree equation in two variables, which describes all the conics: the curves obtained by cutting a cone with a plane. \\[ A x^2 + B x y + C y^2 + D x + E y + F = 0 \\] Discriminant \\( B^2 - 4AC \\) Conic Standard form Where it shows up < 0, with A = C and B = 0 Circle \\( x^{2} + y^{2} = r^{2} \\) Locus of constant magnitude; the \\( |\\Gamma| \\) circle of the Smith chart < 0 Ellipse \\( \\dfrac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} = 1 \\) Lissajous figure of two signals in quadrature; orbits = 0 Parabola \\( y^{2} = 4px \\) Parabolic antenna, projectile motion, power as a function of current > 0 Hyperbola \\( \\dfrac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} - \\dfrac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} = 1 \\) Constant-power curve \\( v \\cdot i = P \\); time-difference location The discriminant B² − 4AC does not change when the axes are rotated: that is why it can be used to classify without solving anything. The limiting cases —two lines, a point, nothing— are called degenerate conics and appear when the plane passes through the vertex of the cone."},{"t":"The cross term is a rotation","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#rotacion","x":"03 The cross term is a rotation If B ≠ 0, the conic is rotated with respect to the coordinate axes. You can always choose a pair of new axes, rotated by an angle θ, in which the cross term disappears: \\[ \\tan (2 \\theta) = \\frac{B}{A -C} \\] If A = C, the angle is 45°. The new axes are the directions of the eigenvectors of the matrix of the form. Principal axes theorem Every symmetric matrix can be diagonalized with a rotation matrix. In the new axes the quadratic form has no cross terms and its coefficients are the eigenvalues: q = λ₁ x′² + λ₂ y′². That is all that is needed to classify it, and it is the same result that in mechanics gives the principal axes of inertia and in statistics, the principal components. It is developed in eigenvalues and eigenvectors."},{"t":"Classification by sign","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#signo","x":"04 Classification by sign A quadratic form is classified according to which signs it can take: Class Condition on q(X) with X ≠ 0 Eigenvalues Leading principal minors Positive definite q > 0 always All positive All positive Negative definite q < 0 always All negative Alternate: −, +, −… Semidefinite q ≥ 0 (or ≤ 0), with some X ≠ 0 where it equals 0 Some zero Some zero Indefinite Takes values of both signs Of both signs Does not follow the patterns above Sylvester’s criterion lets you decide without computing eigenvalues: for 2 × 2, the form is positive definite if a > 0 and det(A) > 0. Example q = 2x² + 2xy + 3y² has matrix [[2, 1], [1, 3]]: a = 2 > 0 and det = 6 − 1 = 5 > 0, so it is positive definite. It is never negative, and it equals zero only at the origin. Its eigenvalues are (5 ± √5)/2 ≈ 3.62 and 1.38, both positive, as they had to be."},{"t":"Seeing it","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#laboratorio-conica","x":"05 Seeing it Change the coefficients and watch which curve appears, what the discriminant says and what happens to the eigenvalues and the rotation angle. Lab · the conic and its matrix A B C D E F Circle Ellipse Parabola Hyperbola Ellipse rotated 45° Degenerate: two lines x·y = 1"},{"t":"Quadratic forms in engineering","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#aplicaciones","x":"06 Quadratic forms in engineering Energy. The energy stored in an inductor and a capacitor is E = ½L i² + ½C v²: a positive definite quadratic form in the state variables. Its being positive definite is what guarantees that the energy cannot be negative, and it is the basis of the Lyapunov stability criterion in control systems. Dissipated power. P = i² R, and in a network with several currents, P = IᵀRI. That it is positive definite is equivalent to saying that a passive network cannot deliver energy. Lissajous figures. Two sinusoidal signals of the same frequency trace on the oscilloscope in XY mode an ellipse whose tilt and flatness give the phase shift. It is a conic, and the cross term is precisely the phase shift. You can try it in the instrument simulator. Least squares. The error of a fit is a quadratic form in the parameters; the minimum exists and is unique precisely because it is positive definite. In three variables, the same setup gives the quadrics: ellipsoids, hyperboloids of one and two sheets, elliptic and hyperbolic paraboloids —the “saddle”— and cones. The classification again comes out of the signs of the three eigenvalues."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · Classify and reduce For 5x² + 4xy + 5y² = 9: write the matrix, compute the discriminant and the eigenvalues, find the rotation angle and write the equation in the new axes. Check with the lab above. Exercise 2 · Lissajous With two generators of the same frequency in XY mode, vary the phase shift from 0° to 90° and record how the figure goes from a line to an ellipse and to a circle. Relate each case to the value of the cross term B. Exercise 3 · Positive definite energy Write the energy of an LC circuit as a quadratic form in (i, v), verify with Sylvester’s criterion that it is positive definite and discuss what it would mean if it were not."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Putting the whole B off the diagonal instead of B/2: the matrix has to be symmetric and the product XᵀAX adds it twice. Classifying by looking only at A and C without considering the cross term. Confusing the discriminant with the determinant: B² − 4AC is −4·det(A), so the signs go the other way. Forgetting that rotation does not change the type of conic, only its orientation. Taking a form with det > 0 and a < 0 as positive definite: that is negative definite."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What matrix corresponds to q = 3x² − 4xy + y²? [[3, −2], [−2, 1]]: the cross coefficient is split in halves. Classify the conic 4x² + y² − 16 = 0. B² − 4AC = 0 − 16 = −16 < 0 and A ≠ C: it is an ellipse, with semi-axes 2 in x and 4 in y. Is q = x² + 4xy + y² positive definite? No. The matrix is [[1, 2], [2, 1]], with det = 1 − 4 = −3 < 0: the eigenvalues are 3 and −1, of opposite signs, so the form is indefinite. For example q(1, −1) = −2. What rotation angle eliminates the cross term in x² + 2xy + y²? Since A = C, the angle is 45°. The conic is degenerate: (x + y)² = 0 is a double line. Why is the energy of a passive circuit a positive definite form? Because it is never negative and equals zero only when all currents and voltages are zero. If it could be negative, the circuit would deliver energy without a source."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Quadratic forms and conics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Charles H. Lehmann. Analytic Geometry. (Spanish edition, “Geometría analítica”), Limusa. The conics in detail: focal properties, rotation and translation of axes. Howard Anton. Elementary Linear Algebra. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción al álgebra lineal”), Limusa Wiley, 2011. The chapter on quadratic forms, with the principal axes theorem and Sylvester’s criterion. Gilbert Strang. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Cengage Learning, 2007. Relates positive definite matrices to minima, energy and stability."},{"t":"The value inside, read from the boundary","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#enunciado","x":"01 The value inside, read from the boundary The previous theorem says when an integral is zero. This one does something much stronger: it says that the values of an analytic function on a closed curve completely determine what the function is worth everywhere in the interior. \\[ f(z_0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi j} \\oint_C \\frac{f(z)}{z - z_0}\\,dz \\] With \\( f \\) analytic inside and on \\( C \\), and \\( z_0 \\) an interior point, traversed counterclockwise. There is nothing equivalent in real calculus. A real function can take any value in the interior of an interval without the endpoints knowing about it. An analytic one cannot: it is so tied to itself that the boundary defines it. The physical version of the same thing The real part of \\( f \\) is a harmonic function, that is, a potential. That the boundary determines the interior is exactly the Dirichlet problem: once the potential is fixed on the electrodes, the potential is determined throughout the region. Cauchy’s formula is the complex version of that fact, and from it come the Poisson formulas used to solve it."},{"t":"Where it comes from","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#salida","x":"02 Where it comes from The proof is a good example of how Cauchy’s theorem is used. The function \\( f(z)/(z - z_0) \\) is analytic everywhere except at \\( z_0 \\), so the contour \\( C \\) can be deformed into a tiny circle of radius \\( \\varepsilon \\) around \\( z_0 \\) without changing the value. On that circle, with \\( z = z_0 + \\varepsilon e^{j\\theta} \\): \\[ \\oint \\frac{f(z)}{z - z_0}\\,dz = \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\frac{f(z_0 + \\varepsilon e^{j\\theta})}{\\varepsilon e^{j\\theta}} \\, j\\,\\varepsilon e^{j\\theta}\\,d\\theta = j\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(z_0 + \\varepsilon e^{j\\theta})\\,d\\theta \\] The \\( \\varepsilon \\) cancels, and since the result does not depend on \\( \\varepsilon \\), it can be taken as small as desired. There continuity does the rest: \\( f(z_0 + \\varepsilon e^{j\\theta}) \\) tends to \\( f(z_0) \\), which comes out of the integral and leaves \\( 2\\pi j\\,f(z_0) \\). Incidentally, that intermediate equality holds for any radius and is a result in its own right: \\[ f(z_0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} f\\big(z_0 + r\\,e^{j\\theta}\\big)\\,d\\theta \\] The mean value property: the value at the center is the average of the values over any circle. Taking real parts, this says that the potential at the center of a disk is the average of the potential on the boundary. It is the property used by any relaxation method to solve Laplace numerically: the neighbors are averaged and you iterate."},{"t":"The average that does not depend on the radius","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#lab","x":"03 The average that does not depend on the radius Lab · the boundary determines the center On the left, the circle; on the right, the curve traced by the values of \\( f \\) on it. The yellow point is the average of that curve, and it has to fall right on \\( f(z_0) \\). Change the radius: as long as the function is analytic inside, the average does not move. f(z) = z² f(z) = e^z f(z) = sin z f(z) = z³ − z f(z) = 1/(z − 2) · pole at z = 2 f(z) = |z|² · not analytic Radius = 0.80"},{"t":"And the derivatives, as a bonus","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#derivadas","x":"04 And the derivatives, as a bonus Differentiating the formula with respect to \\( z_0 \\) —which is allowed, and the formal proof is in any book— yields a whole family of formulas: \\[ f^{(n)}(z_0) = \\frac{n!}{2\\pi j} \\oint_C \\frac{f(z)}{(z - z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz \\] Read forwards, it lets you compute the nth derivative with an integral. Read backwards, it is what turns many contour integrals into a simple derivative: \\[ \\oint_C \\frac{f(z)}{(z - z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz = \\frac{2\\pi j}{n!}\\,f^{(n)}(z_0) \\] And read as a statement, it says something much deeper: if \\( f \\) is differentiable once on a neighborhood, the formula guarantees that all the derivatives exist, because the right-hand side is defined for any \\( n \\). That is the proof of what had been announced since topic 3: in the complex plane, differentiable once implies differentiable infinitely many times."},{"t":"What follows","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#consecuencias","x":"05 What follows Result What it says Where it is used Cauchy’s inequality \\( |f^{(n)}(z_0)| \\le \\dfrac{n!\\,M}{r^{n}} \\) Bounding derivatives without computing them Liouville’s theorem Entire and bounded \\( \\Rightarrow \\) constant The fundamental theorem of algebra follows from it Maximum modulus \\( |f| \\) attains its maximum on the boundary, never inside The peak of \\( |H| \\) over a region is on the edge Mean value \\( f(z_0) \\) is the average over any circle Relaxation methods for solving Laplace Liouville’s deserves a line of its own because of how unexpected it is: an entire function that never exceeds a fixed value has to be constant. Applied to \\( 1/p(z) \\) with \\( p \\) a polynomial with no roots, it leads to an absurdity, and hence every polynomial of degree \\( \\ge 1 \\) has at least one complex root. The fundamental theorem of algebra comes out as a corollary of a contour integral."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Averaging by hand For \\( f(z) = z^2 \\) and \\( z_0 = 1 \\), evaluate the function at eight equally spaced points of the circle of radius 0.5, average the eight complex values and check that you get \\( 1 \\), which is \\( f(1) \\). Repeat with radius 1 and verify that the average did not change. Activity 2 · An integral that comes out as a derivative Compute \\( \\oint \\dfrac{e^{z}}{(z-1)^3}\\,dz \\) over \\( |z| = 2 \\) using the formula for derivatives with \\( n = 2 \\). Verify that it gives \\( \\pi j\\,e \\). Activity 3 · Relaxation on a grid Set up a 20 × 20 grid in a spreadsheet, fix the potential at the edges and replace each interior cell with the average of its four neighbors, iterating until it settles. It is the mean value property turned into a computational method."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Using the formula with singularities of \\( f \\) inside. \\( f \\) has to be analytic on the whole interior; the only special point allowed is \\( z_0 \\). Forgetting the \\( n! \\) in the derivative formula. It is the mistake that costs the most on an exam. Mixing up the exponent. For the nth derivative the denominator is raised to \\( n+1 \\). Believing that the average depends on the radius. It does not, and that is precisely the result. Applying it to non-analytic functions. With \\( |z|^2 \\) the average over the circle is \\( |z_0|^2 + r^2 \\): it does not match the value at the center and, on top of that, it depends on the radius."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Write Cauchy’s formula. \\( f(z_0) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi j}\\displaystyle\\oint_C \\dfrac{f(z)}{z-z_0}\\,dz \\), with \\( f \\) analytic inside and on \\( C \\). What is \\( \\oint \\dfrac{e^z}{z}\\,dz \\) over \\( |z| = 1 \\)? \\( 2\\pi j\\,e^{0} = 2\\pi j \\): it is the formula with \\( f(z) = e^z \\) and \\( z_0 = 0 \\). And \\( \\oint \\dfrac{\\operatorname{sin} z}{(z - \\pi/2)^2}\\,dz \\)? With \\( n = 1 \\): \\( 2\\pi j \\cdot \\cos(\\pi/2) = 0 \\). The derivative of the sine at \\( \\pi/2 \\) vanishes. What does the mean value property say? That \\( f(z_0) \\) is the average of the values of \\( f \\) over any circle centered there, regardless of the radius. Why is an analytic function infinitely differentiable? Because the derivative formula gives an expression valid for every \\( n \\) starting from a single integral. Being differentiable once on a neighborhood is enough. Where is the maximum of \\( |f| \\) in a region? On the boundary, never in the interior, unless \\( f \\) is constant. This is the maximum modulus principle."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Cauchy’s integral formula","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The formula, the derivative formula, Liouville and maximum modulus, in that order and with the proofs. A. David Wunsch. Complex Variables with Applications. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja con aplicaciones”), Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1997. Poisson’s formula and the Dirichlet problem solved with this tool. Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Contour integrals solved with the derivative formula, which is half of the exam exercises."},{"t":"The experiment that waves cannot explain","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#experimento","x":"01 The experiment that waves cannot explain Light shining on a metal plate inside an evacuated tube knocks out electrons, which a collector picks up as current. None of that surprises wave theory: a wave carries energy, and that energy can free charges. What wave theory does not predict is how that current depends on the light that produces it. What is observed What the wave predicts What is measured Cutoff frequency It should not exist: just wait long enough Below \\( f_0 \\) no electron comes out, whatever the intensity Energy of the electrons It should grow with intensity It depends only on the frequency; intensity changes the number, not the energy Emission time With weak light there should be a delay It is immediate, even with the weakest light that can be measured The three facts point in the same direction: there is something in light that is not distributed continuously. That was Einstein's way in, in 1905, the same year he published special relativity, and he used the same idea of Planck's that until then had been taken as a mathematical device for the blackbody."},{"t":"Einstein's equation","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#einstein","x":"02 Einstein's equation Einstein took Planck's quanta seriously: if light of frequency \\( f \\) comes in packets of energy \\( hf \\), an electron at the surface absorbs one whole photon or none. Part of that energy is spent escaping from the metal —the work function \\( \\phi \\), different for each material— and the rest remains as kinetic energy. \\[ K_{max} = hf - \\phi \\qquad\\qquad f_0 = \\frac{\\phi}{h} \\qquad\\qquad eV_0 = K_{max} \\] \\( V_0 \\) is the stopping potential: the reverse voltage that has to be applied so that not even the fastest electron reaches the collector. Measuring it is measuring \\( K_{max} \\) directly, in electronvolts. This single equation explains the three facts in the table above. Below \\( f_0 = \\phi/h \\) a photon cannot pay the work function and nothing comes out, no matter how many photons arrive. Above it, each absorbed photon gives exactly \\( hf - \\phi \\) of kinetic energy, so more intensity means more photons and more current, but the same energy per electron. And there is no delay because absorption happens one photon at a time, not as a gradual accumulation. Lab · Millikan's experiment You choose the metal and the light, and the graph plots the stopping potential against frequency. The five lines have the same slope —it is \\( h/e \\), and it does not depend on the metal— and cross the axis at a different point depending on the work function. It is the experiment with which Millikan, between 1914 and 1916, measured \\( h \\) by a completely different rout"},{"t":"Wave-particle duality and the Compton effect","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#dualidad","x":"03 Wave-particle duality and the Compton effect Interference and diffraction —the whole previous unit— can only be explained if light is a wave. The photoelectric effect can only be explained if light arrives in packets. Light is not one thing instead of the other: it is both, and which one is convenient to use depends on the experiment. This is called wave-particle duality, and it is not a flaw of the theory but the first symptom that a new physics is needed: quantum mechanics. The final confirmation that the photon is a particle with momentum of its own came in 1923, when Arthur Compton made X-rays collide with free electrons and measured that the scattered light came out with a lower frequency than the incident light, lower the larger the deflection angle. That is exactly what happens in a billiard-ball collision between two particles. \\[ \\Delta\\lambda = \\lambda' - \\lambda = \\frac{h}{m_ec}(1-\\cos\\theta) \\] \\( h/m_ec = 2.426 \\) pm is the “Compton wavelength” of the electron. At 90° the shift is 2.43 pm; at 180°, double that. For visible light (hundreds of nanometers) the effect is unmeasurable; for X-rays (tens of picometers) it is enormous. Two independent tests, one and the same number The photoelectric effect measures \\( h \\) from energies (electronvolts and optical frequencies). The Compton effect measures it from momentum (X-ray wavelengths). Blackbody radiation, in the previous topic, measures it from a thermal spectrum. Three experiments that look nothing alike give "},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#electronica","x":"04 Where it appears in electronics Device What it does The effect at work Photomultiplier tube Detects photons one at a time, with an electron cascade External photoelectric effect: the photon knocks the electron out of the photocathode Camera tubes (vidicon, iconoscope) First TV cameras, before the CCD Photoelectric effect on a mosaic of photocathodes Photodiode and CCD/CMOS Convert light into current inside a semiconductor Internal photoelectric effect: the electron does not leave the material, it moves up a band Solar cell Generates a voltage from light, without an external battery Photovoltaic effect: a relative of the internal photoelectric effect, in a p-n junction Photoresistor (LDR) Changes its resistance with light Photoconductive effect: the electron does not escape either, it just changes band The photomultiplier is the direct heir of Millikan's experiment: it multiplies the signal of a single photoelectron by a million or more, with a chain of electrodes at increasing voltage (dynodes), and today it remains the reference detector when photons have to be counted one at a time, as in a positron emission tomography scanner or a gamma-ray telescope. The photodiode and the CCD in an ordinary camera solve the same problem —converting light into an electrical signal— with the internal photoelectric effect: the electron does not need to escape from the material, it is enough for it to jump from the valence band to the conduction band. That conduction band is, precisely, t"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring h with a straight line With the \\( V_0 \\) versus \\( f \\) data for a metal (or the data given by the lab on this page, read with precision), fit a straight line by least squares. The slope gives \\( h/e \\) and the y-intercept gives \\( -\\phi/e \\). Compare the \\( h \\) obtained with the accepted value. Activity 2 · Intensity versus frequency With the metal and the wavelength fixed, vary only the intensity and record how the saturation current and the stopping potential change. Repeat changing only the frequency, with the intensity fixed. Confirm which of the two variables moves each quantity. Activity 3 · The cutoff frequency of each metal Calculate the cutoff wavelength of the five metals in the lab and place them on the spectrum (visible, UV-A, UV-C). Explain why cesium and sodium are used in photocells for visible light and platinum only responds to vacuum UV."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that more intensity gives faster electrons. More intensity gives more electrons; the energy of each one depends only on the frequency. Thinking that any frequency will do if you wait long enough. Below \\( f_0 \\) nothing comes out, ever, whatever the intensity or the exposure time. Confusing the stopping potential with the supply voltage of the tube. \\( V_0 \\) is the one that makes the current drop exactly to zero, not just any voltage. Believing that the photoelectric effect shows that light “is not” a wave. It shows that it is also a particle. Interference is still true. Confusing the external photoelectric effect with the photoconductive or photovoltaic one. In the external one the electron escapes from the material; in the other two it changes band without leaving it. Treating the number \\( h \\) of this unit as different from the one in blackbody radiation. It is the same one, measured by another route."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Why does classical wave theory not explain the cutoff frequency? Because in that theory the energy of light depends on its intensity, not on its frequency: with enough intensity, any frequency should eventually knock out electrons, and that does not happen below \\( f_0 \\). What does the stopping potential \\( V_0 \\) measure? The maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons, expressed in electronvolts: \\( eV_0 = K_{max} \\). What changes when the light intensity is increased, with the frequency fixed? The saturation current increases, that is, the number of photoelectrons per second. The energy of each one does not change. What is the work function \\( \\phi \\)? The minimum energy needed to knock an electron out of the surface of a given metal. What evidence does the Compton effect provide for the particle nature of light? That the photon carries momentum and transfers it like a particle in a collision: the scattered light changes wavelength with the angle, just as a mechanical collision predicts. Why did Einstein receive the Nobel Prize for the photoelectric effect and not for relativity? Because by 1921 the photoelectric equation had already been confirmed with experimental precision by Millikan, whereas relativity still did not have such direct backing in the eyes of the committee."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The photoelectric effect and duality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Robert Eisberg and Robert Resnick. Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles. 2nd ed., John Wiley & Sons, 1985 (Spanish edition, “Física cuántica: átomos, moléculas, sólidos, núcleos y partículas”, Limusa). Chapter 2 develops the photoelectric effect and the Compton effect with the same rigor with which it treated the blackbody. Robert A. Millikan. A Direct Photoelectric Determination of Planck's “h”. Physical Review, vol. 7, 1916. The paper that finished confirming Einstein's equation, after its author had spent years trying to refute it. Arthur H. Compton. A Quantum Theory of the Scattering of X-rays by Light Elements. Physical Review, vol. 21, 1923. The experiment that definitively gave light momentum of its own. Raymond Serway and John Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers. 9th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). The table of work functions of the elements and the standard didactic treatment of this unit."},{"t":"The principle","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#principio","x":"01 The principle A linear supply regulates by dissipating the difference between input and output, as seen in linear regulated power supplies. A switching supply, by contrast, chops the voltage with a switch that opens and closes at a fixed frequency, and filters the result with an inductor and a capacitor, which ideally dissipate nothing. With the duty cycle \\( D \\), the fraction of the period during which the switch conducts, the ratio between output and input is set. Efficiency reaches 85 to 97 %, and the size of the magnetic components falls as the frequency rises. A more practical introduction is on the switching power supplies page of the technician program."},{"t":"The topologies","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#topologias","x":"02 The topologies In steady state, the average voltage across the inductor is zero: the continuous-conduction relations follow from that balance. Topology V o /V i in continuous conduction Use Buck ( step-down ) \\( D \\) Points of load, processor regulators Boost ( step-up ) \\( 1/(1-D) \\) Batteries to higher voltages, power factor correction Buck-boost \\( -D/(1-D) \\) Inverted outputs, or outputs both higher and lower than the input Flyback \\( \\frac{N_s}{N_p}\\,\\frac{D}{1-D} \\) Isolated supplies up to about 100 W: chargers Forward \\( \\frac{N_s}{N_p}\\,D \\) Isolated supplies of hundreds of W Half bridge, full bridge, LLC depends on the topology High power, high efficiency Isolated supplies use a high-frequency transformer; in the flyback that transformer is actually a coupled inductor that stores the energy in its air gap, as seen in magnetic materials."},{"t":"Continuous and discontinuous conduction","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#discontinua","x":"03 Continuous and discontinuous conduction If the load is light, the inductor current reaches zero before the period ends: this is discontinuous conduction. There the voltage ratio stops being \\( D \\) and starts to depend on the load. In the buck converter, with \\( K = 2L/(R\\,T) \\): \\[ K \\ge 1 - D: \\quad \\frac{V_o}{V_i} = D \\] \\[ K In continuous conduction, the current ripple is \\( \\Delta I = (V_i - V_o) D T / L \\) and the voltage ripple, with an ideal capacitor, \\( \\Delta V = \\Delta I / (8 f C) \\). In practice the equivalent series resistance of the capacitor usually dominates the voltage ripple. Lab · buck converter A 24 V, 100 kHz buck converter with a 100 μF output capacitor, operating open loop. The curve is the inductor current over three periods; the dashed line, the load current. Duty cycle D = Load resistance R = Inductance L ="},{"t":"Control, rectification and noise","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#control","x":"04 Control, rectification and noise The output is regulated with a loop that adjusts \\( D \\). In voltage-mode control, an error amplifier compares the output with a reference and a pulse-width modulator generates \\( D \\); the LC filter adds two poles and the loop is compensated as seen in stability and compensation. In current-mode control, an inner loop limits the peak switch current in each cycle: the plant becomes first order and the overcurrent protection is automatic. At a low output voltage, the drop of the freewheeling diode eats up a good part of the efficiency; it is replaced by a MOSFET that conducts in synchronism, synchronous rectification. Supplies that draw more than about 75 W from the line include a boost stage for power factor correction, which forces the input current to follow the sinusoidal shape of the voltage. Fast edges generate conducted and radiated interference, which is controlled with input filters, careful layout of the board and shielding."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Open-loop buck converter With a laboratory buck converter at a fixed duty cycle, vary the load and measure the output voltage and the inductor current with a current probe; identify the transition to discontinuous conduction. Activity 2 · Efficiency Measure the efficiency of a commercial power supply as a function of load, with a diode and with synchronous rectification. Activity 3 · Step response Apply a load step to a feedback-regulated supply and measure the overshoot and the settling time of the output."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using V o = D V i with a light load. In discontinuous conduction the output rises. Choosing the inductor without looking at its saturation current. The peak, not the average, is what saturates the core. Calculating the ripple from the capacitance alone. The equivalent series resistance of the capacitor usually dominates. Making large loops with the switched current. They are antennas; the input loop must be as small as possible."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What duty cycle does a boost converter need to go from 12 to 48 V? D = 0.75. In the lab, with D = 0.5 and 22 μH, what current ripple is there in continuous conduction? \\( \\Delta I = 12 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 10\\ \\mu s / 22\\ \\mu H \\approx 2.73 \\) A. At what load resistance does it enter discontinuous conduction? When \\( 2L/(RT) What advantage does current-mode control have? It simplifies the compensation of the voltage loop and limits the switch current in each cycle."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Switching power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Robert W. Erickson and Dragan Maksimović. Fundamentals of Power Electronics. 3rd ed., Springer, 2020. The analysis of DC-DC converters, their modeling and their control. Abraham I. Pressman, Keith Billings and Taylor Morey. Switching Power Supply Design. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2009. The practical design of switching power supplies, including their transformers. Sanjaya Maniktala. Switching Power Supplies A–Z. 2nd ed., Newnes, 2012. The design details that the formulas do not tell you."},{"t":"Transistors instead of resistors","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#por-que","x":"01 Transistors instead of resistors In an integrated circuit, resistors are expensive —they take up a lot of area—, imprecise —they vary by 20 % from one wafer to another— and cannot be very large. Transistors, on the other hand, are cheap and, most importantly, identical to each other: two neighboring transistors on the same wafer have almost identical parameters. This gives rise to the technique that distinguishes integrated analog design: using transistors as current sources for biasing, and as loads for amplifiers. An ideal current source delivers the same current at any voltage, that is, it has infinite output resistance. Real ones are described by two numbers: their output resistance \\( R_{out} \\) and their useful voltage range (the minimum voltage at which they still work, called the compliance voltage)."},{"t":"The current mirror","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#espejo","x":"02 The current mirror Two identical transistors with the same base-emitter voltage conduct the same current. If one of them is connected as a diode (base tied to collector) and a reference current is forced through it, the other one copies it. In the bipolar: \\[ I_{out} = \\frac{I_{ref}}{1 + 2/\\beta} \\left( 1 + \\frac{V_{CE2} - V_{BE}}{V_A} \\right) \\] \\[ R_{out} = r_o = \\frac{V_A}{I_{out}} \\] The factor \\( 1 + 2/\\beta \\) is the error due to the base currents: with β = 100, the copy comes out 2 % low. The second factor is the Early effect. In MOS there is no gate current and the current ratio is set by the geometry: \\( I_{out} = I_{ref}\\,(W/L)_2 / (W/L)_1 \\), with the same error from channel-length modulation. With several output transistors connected to the same diode, a single reference biases an entire chip."},{"t":"The Widlar current source","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#widlar","x":"03 The Widlar current source To obtain small currents without enormous resistors, Bob Widlar added a resistor in the emitter of the output transistor. The two base-emitter voltages are no longer equal, and their difference drops across that resistor: \\[ V_T \\ln \\frac{I_{ref}}{I_{out}} = I_{out}\\, R_E \\] To go down from 1 mA to 10 µA you need \\( R_E = 25.85\\text{ mV} \\times \\ln 100 / 10\\text{ µA} \\approx 11.9 \\) kΩ. With a simple mirror, a 10 µA reference from 15 V would require a 1.4 MΩ resistor. The emitter resistor also provides feedback and raises the output resistance to approximately \\( r_o (1 + g_m R_E) \\). Widlar used this idea to design the first successful integrated operational amplifiers, such as the μA702 and the LM101."},{"t":"The cascode","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#cascodo","x":"04 The cascode Stacking a second transistor on the output, in common base, leaves the bottom transistor with its collector voltage fixed and the Early effect almost disappears. The output resistance is multiplied: Source Output resistance Minimum operating voltage Simple mirror \\( r_o \\) \\( V_{CE(sat)} \\approx 0.2 \\) V Widlar \\( \\approx r_o (1 + g_m R_E) \\) \\( I_{out} R_E + V_{CE(sat)} \\) Bipolar cascode \\( \\approx \\beta\\, r_o / 2 \\) \\( V_{BE} + V_{CE(sat)} \\approx 0.9 \\) V MOS cascode \\( \\approx g_m r_o^2 \\) \\( V_{th} + 2 V_{ov} \\) The price of the cascode is range: it consumes more voltage, a serious problem when the supply is 1 V. That is why modern design looks for low-voltage cascode configurations. Lab · how close to an ideal source The output current of three bipolar sources as a function of the voltage across their output, with the same reference. The chosen curve is highlighted; the others stay in the background. An ideal source would be a horizontal line. simple mirror Widlar source cascode mirror Reference current \\( I_{ref} \\) = Widlar emitter resistance \\( R_E \\) = Early voltage \\( V_A \\) = β ="},{"t":"The active load","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#carga-activa","x":"05 The active load In a common emitter with a collector resistor, the gain \\( g_m R_C = I_C R_C / V_T \\) is limited by the supply: \\( I_C R_C \\) cannot exceed a few volts, and the gain ends up at a few hundred. If the resistor is replaced by a current source —a mirror PNP transistor—, the load seen by the collector is its output resistance, which is enormous, while the DC drop is small. The gain becomes the intrinsic gain of the transistors: \\[ A_v = -g_m\\, (r_{o1} \\parallel r_{o2}) \\approx -\\frac{V_A}{2\\, V_T} \\] With \\( V_A = 100 \\) V in both transistors, about 1900 in a single stage, something impossible with a resistor. The active load is the reason an integrated operational amplifier reaches gains of one hundred thousand with two or three stages, and it appears again in the differential amplifier."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · A mirror with discrete transistors Build a mirror with two BC548s and a 1 mA reference. Measure the output current for voltages from 1 to 10 V and estimate the output resistance. Repeat with the two transistors bonded together with thermal grease to equalize their temperature. Activity 2 · A Widlar source Add to the mirror an emitter resistor calculated for 50 µA and verify the current and the increase in output resistance. Activity 3 · Better with an integrated circuit Repeat the mirror with transistors from an integrated array (such as the CA3096 or a matched pair) and compare the error with that of the loose transistors."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Building mirrors with loose transistors and expecting accuracy. The copy depends on matching and temperature. Forgetting the minimum voltage. Below it, the output transistor saturates and the source stops being a source. Ignoring the base currents. The \\( 2/\\beta \\) error accumulates if many outputs are copied from the same diode. Measuring the gain with an active load using a 1 MΩ probe. The probe loading is lower than the output resistance and the measured gain drops."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What error does a simple mirror with β = 50 have? \\( 1/(1 + 2/50) = 0.96 \\): the copy comes out 4 % low. What emitter resistance does a Widlar source need to go from 1 mA to 100 µA? \\( 25.85 \\text{ mV} \\times \\ln 10 / 100 \\text{ µA} \\approx 595 \\) Ω. What is the output resistance of a simple mirror at 100 µA with \\( V_A = 80 \\) V? \\( 80 / 100\\text{ µA} = 800 \\) kΩ. Why does an active load give more gain than a resistor? Because it presents a very high small-signal resistance with a small DC drop."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Current sources and active loads","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. Chapter 4 is the reference on mirrors, Widlar sources and cascodes, in bipolar and MOS. Behzad Razavi. Design of Analog CMOS Integrated Circuits. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2017. Mirrors and active loads in CMOS, with low-voltage cascodes. Robert J. Widlar. Some Circuit Design Techniques for Linear Integrated Circuits. IEEE Transactions on Circuit Theory, 1965. The paper in which the source that bears his name appears."},{"t":"From the mains to a stable DC voltage","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#estructura","x":"01 From the mains to a stable DC voltage A linear power supply has four blocks: a transformer that steps down the mains voltage and isolates, a rectifier, a filter capacitor that converts the pulses into a DC voltage with ripple, and a regulator that eliminates the ripple and the variations of the mains and of the load. The regulator is a feedback amplifier: it compares a fraction of the output with a reference and drives a series transistor that absorbs the voltage difference. Rectifier diodes were presented in semiconductors and diodes, in the technician program, and there is a publication with the complete design of a regulated power supply with an LM317."},{"t":"Rectifier and filter capacitor","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#filtro","x":"02 Rectifier and filter capacitor With a bridge, the capacitor charges at the peaks of each half-cycle and discharges through the load the rest of the time. The peak is that of the secondary voltage minus two diode drops, and the ripple depends on how much charge is lost between peaks: \\[ V_{\\max} \\approx \\sqrt{2}\\, V_s - 2 V_D \\] \\[ \\Delta V \\approx \\frac{I_L}{2 f C} \\] \\( f \\) is the mains frequency. The formula assumes that the capacitor discharges during the whole half-cycle, and overestimates the ripple: in practice the charging lasts a fraction of the cycle. The winding resistance lowers the real peak by quite a bit more than the diode drops do. Recharging occurs over a short angle, so the current through the diodes and the secondary arrives in pulses of several times the load current. That is why the transformer is sized with an apparent power greater than the DC power it delivers, and the diodes and the capacitor for their peak current and their RMS ripple current."},{"t":"The series regulator","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#regulador","x":"03 The series regulator The classic linear regulator has a voltage reference, a compensated zener or a 1.25 V bandgap reference, an error amplifier and a pass transistor. Feedback makes the output follow the reference multiplied by the divider, and reduces by the loop gain the effect of the mains and of the load: \\[ V_o = V_{ref} \\left(1 + \\frac{R_2}{R_1}\\right) \\] Datasheets give the line regulation (change in output per volt of input), the load regulation (per ampere) and the ripple rejection, which in a 7805 is 60 dB or more at the ripple frequency: 1 V at the input becomes less than 1 mV at the output. To regulate, the pass transistor needs a minimum voltage between input and output, the dropout voltage ( dropout ). In regulators with an NPN emitter output, such as the 7805, it is about 2 V; in LDOs, with a PNP or a PMOS pass device, it falls to a few tenths of a volt. If at the bottom of the ripple the input falls below \\( V_o + V_{DO} \\), the ripple passes to the output."},{"t":"Dissipation and efficiency","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#disipacion","x":"04 Dissipation and efficiency \\[ P_D = (\\overline{V_{in}} - V_o)\\, I_L \\] \\[ \\eta_{reg} = \\frac{V_o}{\\overline{V_{in}}} \\] Everything left over between the average input and the output is dissipated in the pass transistor. With 9 V average, 5 V and 1 A, the regulator dissipates 4 W and the regulator efficiency is 56 %, not counting the transformer or the diodes. The design is a compromise: the secondary voltage has to be enough for the bottom of the ripple to exceed the regulator drop with the mains low, but every extra volt is heat. LDOs allow the secondary and the dissipation to be lowered; switching power supplies solve the problem at the root, in exchange for switching noise. Lab · rectifier, filter and 5 V regulator Time-domain simulation of a diode bridge (0.8 V each) fed from a secondary with 0.5 Ω of winding resistance, at 50 Hz, with a filter capacitor and a constant-current load. The gray trace is the rectified voltage; the blue one, that of the capacitor; the red line, the minimum the regulator needs, and the green one, the output. 7.5 V RMS secondary 9 V RMS secondary 12 V RMS secondary C = 1000 μF C = 2200 μF C = 4700 μF C = 10,000 μF 7805 regulator (2 V dropout) LDO regulator (0.4 V dropout) Load current = Mains voltage = Integrated regulators add protections: current limiting, sometimes with foldback ( foldback ) to reduce the dissipation in a short circuit, thermal shutdown and safe-operating-area protection of the pass transistor."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Ripple Measure the ripple across the capacitor with the oscilloscope for several load currents and compare it with \\( I_L/(2fC) \\). Also measure the peak current through the diodes with a small series resistor. Activity 2 · Regulator dropout Lower the mains voltage with a variable autotransformer and observe at what point the ripple appears at the output of a 7805 and of an LDO. Activity 3 · Regulation Measure the line and load regulation of an adjustable regulator and compare them with the datasheet."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Looking at the average voltage and not at the bottom of the ripple. The regulator stops regulating at the minimum, not at the average. Designing with the nominal mains. The mains can be 10 % low and the supply must keep regulating. Forgetting the transformer resistance. Under load, the real peak ends up well below \\( \\sqrt 2 V_s \\). Raising the secondary “just in case”. Every extra volt turns into heat in the regulator."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What ripple does the formula estimate for 0.5 A with 2200 μF at 50 Hz? \\( 0.5/(2 \\cdot 50 \\cdot 2.2\\cdot10^{-3}) \\approx 2.3 \\) V. What output does an LM317 give with R 1 = 240 Ω and R 2 = 720 Ω? \\( 1.25 \\cdot (1 + 3) = 5 \\) V. A 12 V regulator delivers 0.8 A with 17 V average input. How much does it dissipate? 4 W. Why does an LDO allow a lower-voltage transformer to be used? Because it needs only a few tenths of a volt between input and output, instead of about 2 V."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Linear regulated power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The chapter on linear regulators, with the design of complete power supplies. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 8th ed., Oxford University Press, 2020. The rectifier with a capacitor and the conduction angle. Texas Instruments. Datasheets of the LM7800 and LM317 regulators and of the LDO family. Available on the manufacturer's website. The specifications for regulation, ripple rejection, dropout and protections."},{"t":"Half of every measurement","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#para-que","x":"01 Half of every measurement To measure the response of an amplifier, a filter or a receiver, it must be excited with a known signal. The signal source is the other half of the measurement bench, and its imperfections —distortion, frequency error, phase noise, amplitude that changes with frequency— add to those of the circuit under test. Knowing how signals are generated makes it possible to tell when the generator limits the measurement."},{"t":"Analog function generators","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#funciones","x":"02 Analog function generators The classic function generator starts from a triangle wave: an integrator charges a capacitor with a constant current and a comparator with hysteresis reverses the direction on reaching each threshold. The comparator output is the square wave, and the sine wave is obtained by rounding the triangle with a diode network that approximates the curvature piecewise, with a typical distortion of 0.5 to 1%. The frequency is changed with the charging current, which makes it possible to sweep it with a control voltage. Low-distortion sine oscillators, such as the Wien bridge with amplitude control, are covered in the technician program, in oscillators."},{"t":"Direct digital synthesis","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#dds","x":"03 Direct digital synthesis Almost all current generators use direct digital synthesis (DDS). An \\( N \\)-bit register, the phase accumulator, adds a number \\( M \\), the tuning word, on every clock cycle. Its value sweeps the phase from 0 to \\( 2\\pi \\) and overflows to start over. The upper bits of the accumulator index a table with the sine values, and a DAC converts them into voltage: \\[ f_{out} = \\frac{M \\cdot f_{clk}}{2^N} \\] \\[ \\Delta f = \\frac{f_{clk}}{2^N} \\] With a 100 MHz clock and a 32-bit accumulator, the resolution is 0.023 Hz, and the frequency changes from one value to another without a transient, in one clock cycle. The frequency accuracy is that of the clock: a temperature-compensated crystal oscillator gives a few parts per million. The DAC output is a staircase: each sample is held for one clock period. This has two effects. The amplitude falls with frequency according to \\( \\sin(\\pi f/f_{clk}) / (\\pi f/f_{clk}) \\), and images of the spectrum appear at \\( f_{clk} \\pm f_{out} \\), \\( 2 f_{clk} \\pm f_{out} \\)… A reconstruction filter removes them, which is why a DDS is used up to about 40% of its clock frequency: higher up, the image is too close to be filtered. Lab · a DDS synthesizer 100 MHz clock, 32-bit phase accumulator and 14-bit DAC. Top, the samples coming out of the DAC (staircase) over the ideal sine wave, for two periods; bottom, the spectrum before the filter, with the fundamental and the first two images. Requested frequency ="},{"t":"Arbitrary, pulse and RF generators","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#otros","x":"04 Arbitrary, pulse and RF generators Generator How it works Key specification Arbitrary waveform (AWG) A memory with samples of any shape, read at a fixed rate Sampling rate, memory length and DAC bits Pulse Pulses with adjustable width, period, delay and edges Rise time, jitter RF A voltage-controlled oscillator locked with a PLL to a reference Phase noise, calibrated output power, modulations The phase noise of an RF generator is the noise power at a given offset from the carrier, in dBc/Hz: for example, −110 dBc/Hz at 10 kHz. It limits the measurement of receivers and oscillators, because the generator’s noise is mixed up with that of the circuit."},{"t":"The output impedance","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#salida","x":"05 The output impedance Almost all generators have 50 Ω of output impedance and display the amplitude assuming a 50 Ω load. If the load is high, such as the input of an oscilloscope or of an operational amplifier, the actual voltage is double the indicated one: the divider between the internal 50 Ω and the load no longer divides by two. Generators allow the load to be configured so that the indication matches. With intermediate loads the divider has to be calculated."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · The 50 Ω output Program 1 V peak-to-peak on a generator and measure it with the oscilloscope at high impedance and with a 50 Ω termination. Explain the difference. Activity 2 · The DDS images With a spectrum analyzer, observe the output of an unfiltered DDS module at several frequencies and measure the level of the first image. Compare it with the lab. Activity 3 · Generator distortion With a spectrum analyzer, measure the harmonic distortion of the sine wave of an analog function generator and of a DDS one."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting the 50 Ω load. With an oscilloscope at high impedance, double the programmed value is measured. Using a DDS near half of its clock. The image is almost at the same level as the signal. Blaming the circuit for the generator’s distortion. The generator must be measured alone first. Trusting the frequency without checking the reference. The accuracy is that of the crystal, and an external 10 MHz clock improves the whole bench."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What tuning word gives 1 MHz in a 32-bit DDS with a 100 MHz clock? \\( M = 10^6 \\cdot 2^{32} / 10^8 \\approx 42\\,949\\,673 \\). How much does the amplitude of a DDS fall at a quarter of its clock frequency? \\( \\sin(\\pi/4)/(\\pi/4) = 0.90 \\): 0.91 dB. A generator indicates 2 V into a 50 Ω load. What voltage does it give into a 1 MΩ load? 4 V. Where does the first image of a 30 MHz output appear with a 100 MHz clock? At 70 MHz."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Signal sources","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Analog Devices. A Technical Tutorial on Digital Signal Synthesis. Analog Devices, 1999. The reference tutorial on DDS: phase accumulator, truncation, spurs and images. Albert D. Helfrick and William D. Cooper. Modern Electronic Instrumentation and Measurement Techniques (Spanish edition: “Instrumentación electrónica moderna y técnicas de medición”). Prentice Hall, 1990. Signal and function generators, with their circuits. Ulrich L. Rohde and Enrico Rubiola. Microwave and Wireless Synthesizers: Theory and Design. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2021. Frequency synthesis with PLL and DDS, and phase noise in depth."},{"t":"A function that cannot be graphed","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#que","x":"01 A function that cannot be graphed A real function \\( y = f(x) \\) is drawn as a curve: two axes are enough. A complex function \\( w = f(z) \\) takes a point of the plane and returns another point of the plane: it would take four dimensions to graph it. The way out is to draw two planes, the starting one and the arrival one, and show what each figure turns into. That is called a mapping, and it is the natural way to understand what a complex function does. \\[ w = f(z) = u(x,y) + j\\,v(x,y), \\qquad z = x + jy \\] Every complex function is really two real functions of two variables: the real part and the imaginary part. Why it matters to an electronics engineer The transfer function of a circuit is a complex function: to each value of \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\) it assigns a complex number \\( H(s) \\). When you run along the imaginary axis you get the frequency response; when you look for the points where \\( H \\) becomes infinite, the poles appear. Everything that follows in the course is reading a complex function somewhere in the plane."},{"t":"The functions you need to know","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#elementales","x":"02 The functions you need to know Function What it does to the plane Where it appears \\( w = z + c \\) Translates DC level shift \\( w = az \\) Scales and rotates Gain and phase shift of a stage \\( w = z^2 \\) Doubles the angle, squares the modulus Mixer, second harmonic \\( w = 1/z \\) Inverts with respect to the unit circle Impedance to admittance \\( w = e^z \\) Takes horizontal lines to rays and vertical lines to circles Complex exponential, phasors \\( w = \\dfrac{z-1}{z+1} \\) Takes the right half-plane to the unit disk The Smith chart The last row deserves attention: that transformation, called the bilinear or Möbius transformation, is the one that turns the half-plane of impedances —infinite and impossible to draw— into a circle of radius one that fits on a sheet of paper. That, and nothing else, is the Smith chart. Lab · the mapping, plane to plane On the left, a grid in the z-plane; on the right, what it turns into when the function is applied. Click or tap the left plane to follow a point. w = z² w = 1/z w = e^z w = (z−1)/(z+1) · the Smith chart w = ½(z + 1/z) · Joukowski w = (1+j)·z + 0.5 Show the transformed grid"},{"t":"The complex exponential","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#exponencial","x":"03 The complex exponential It is the most important function of the whole course. Separating modulus and phase: \\[ e^{z} = e^{x + jy} = \\underbrace{e^{x}}_{\\text{modulus}} \\cdot \\underbrace{e^{jy}}_{\\text{rotation}} = e^{x}\\left(\\cos y + j\\,\\operatorname{sin} y\\right) \\] The real part of \\( z \\) controls how much it grows or dies out. The imaginary part controls how much it rotates. It is periodic with period \\( 2\\pi j \\): \\( e^{z + 2\\pi j} = e^{z} \\). A complex function that is periodic in the imaginary direction, something with no real analog. When \\( e^{st} \\) with \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\) shows up in the course, everything has already been said: the signal rotates at \\( \\omega \\) radians per second while its amplitude is multiplied by \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\). With \\( \\sigma \\lt 0 \\) it dies out, with \\( \\sigma \\gt 0 \\) it grows, and with \\( \\sigma = 0 \\) what remains is a pure sinusoid. That single formula contains the whole natural response of a circuit. The logarithm has infinitely many values Since the exponential is periodic, its inverse cannot be an ordinary function: \\( \\ln z = \\ln|z| + j(\\arg z + 2\\pi k) \\) for any integer \\( k \\). A branch is chosen —normally the one with \\( -\\pi \\lt \\arg z \\le \\pi \\)— and you have to state it. That same phenomenon is what makes the phase measured by an analyzer appear “wrapped” between −180° and 180°, so that it has to be unwrapped."},{"t":"Conformal mapping","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#conforme","x":"04 Conformal mapping An analytic function —differentiable in a neighborhood, which is studied in the next topic— has a remarkable geometric property: wherever its derivative does not vanish, it preserves angles. Two curves that crossed at 90° in the z-plane still cross at 90° after being transformed, even though the curves have been completely deformed. In the lab you can see it for yourself: the starting grid has all its crossings at right angles, and the transformed one, however twisted it ends up, keeps them. Where that breaks down —at \\( z = 0 \\) for \\( w = z^2 \\), for example— is exactly where the derivative vanishes, and those points are called critical. Used for How The Smith chart Taking the half-plane of impedances to a circle Fields and capacitances Transforming a difficult geometry into a simple one, solving there and coming back Transmission lines Moving between reflection coefficient and impedance Stability Mapping the Nyquist contour and counting encirclements of −1"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Checking the Smith chart Take five normalized impedances —0, 1, ∞, j and −j— and compute \\( \\Gamma = (z-1)/(z+1) \\) for each one. Locate the results on the chart and check that they fall where they should: center, right edge, left edge and the two extremes of the circle. Activity 2 · Angles are preserved Draw on paper a 1×1 grid in the first quadrant and transform it with \\( w = z^2 \\) point by point. Verify that the crossings are still at right angles except at the origin. Activity 3 · The exponential, measured On an RC circuit with a step input, capture the response and fit \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\). Check that \\( \\sigma = -1/RC \\) matches the pole of the circuit, which is the point of the plane where the transfer function becomes infinite."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Trying to graph \\( w = f(z) \\) with a curve. Two planes are needed. Forgetting that the logarithm and the root are multivalued and keeping one branch without stating it. Believing that \\( e^z \\) never takes the same value twice. It is periodic in the imaginary direction. Assuming that every complex function preserves angles. Only analytic ones do, and only where the derivative does not vanish. Confusing \\( |e^z| \\) with \\( e^{|z|} \\). The modulus of \\( e^z \\) is \\( e^{x} \\), and it depends only on the real part."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is \\( |e^{2+j5}| \\)? \\( e^2 \\approx 7.39 \\): the modulus depends only on the real part; the imaginary part only rotates. What does the imaginary axis turn into under \\( w = (z-1)/(z+1) \\)? The unit circle. That is why purely reactive impedances fall on the edge of the Smith chart: they reflect all the power. What does \\( w = z^2 \\) do to the first quadrant? It stretches it until it covers the entire upper half-plane: it doubles all the angles. Why does \\( \\ln z \\) have infinitely many values? Because \\( e^z \\) is periodic with period \\( 2\\pi j \\), so infinitely many values of \\( z \\) give the same exponential. If \\( s = -100 + j2000 \\), what does the signal \\( e^{st} \\) look like? An oscillation of 2000 rad/s —about 318 Hz— whose amplitude decays with a time constant \\( 1/100 = 10 \\) ms. It is the typical response of an underdamped RLC circuit. What does it mean for a mapping to be conformal? That it preserves the angles between curves. This is the case for analytic functions wherever the derivative does not vanish."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Functions of a complex variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. The chapters on elementary functions and transformations, with many worked mappings. Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The classic treatment of conformal mapping and its applications to fields. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. The bilinear transformation put to work: the Smith chart, derived step by step."},{"t":"Three conditions, not one","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#definicion","x":"01 Three conditions, not one A function is continuous at a point a when all three of these hold at once: \\[ 1) \\; \\exists f (a) \\quad 2) \\; \\exists \\lim_{x \\to a} f (x) \\quad 3) \\; \\lim_{x \\to a} f (x) = f (a) \\] The value exists, the limit exists and they coincide. If any one of the three fails, there is a discontinuity. The intuitive picture —“it can be drawn without lifting the pencil”— is fine for getting started, but it is imprecise: there are continuous functions that are impossible to draw. The definition above is the one that is used."},{"t":"The three ways to fail","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#tipos","x":"02 The three ways to fail Removable The limit exists, but f(a) does not exist or has some other value. It is fixed by redefining the function at that single point. Jump Both one-sided limits exist and are finite, but differ. The difference is the size of the jump. It cannot be fixed in any way. Essential or infinite One of the one-sided limits is infinite or does not exist, as with 1/x at zero or with sin(1/x). It is the worst one. Lab · classifying a discontinuity The function is the same on both sides; what changes is what happens at the point and how it is approached. Removable: hole at x = 1 Removable: f(1) “wrongly” defined Jump Infinite Continuous f(1) = 0.5"},{"t":"Which functions are continuous","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#algebra","x":"03 Which functions are continuous The sum, difference, product and quotient of continuous functions are continuous —the quotient, where the denominator does not vanish—, and the composition of continuous functions is continuous. That is enough for almost everything, because the elementary functions are continuous on their domains: Function Continuous on Where it fails Polynomial All of R Nowhere Rational Its domain Where the denominator vanishes Exponential and sine/cosine All of R Nowhere Logarithm (0, ∞) Not defined for x ≤ 0 Tangent Its domain At π/2 + kπ: infinite discontinuity Piecewise-defined Inside each piece The joining points have to be studied That is why, in practice, studying the continuity of a function given by a formula comes down to looking at the “suspicious” points: zero denominators, changes of branch and boundaries of the domain."},{"t":"The three theorems that give power","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#teoremas","x":"04 The three theorems that give power A function that is continuous on a closed interval [a, b] has properties that are used all the time, often without being named: Bolzano If f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, then there is at least one c between a and b with f(c) = 0. In other words: to go from negative to positive without jumping, you have to cross zero. Intermediate value The generalization: f takes all the values between f(a) and f(b). If a thermometer read 18 °C in the morning and 27 °C in the afternoon, at some moment it read exactly 22.5 °C. Weierstrass A function that is continuous on a closed and bounded interval attains an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum. It does not merely get close: it reaches them at some point. It is the guarantee that an optimization problem has a solution before you go out looking for it. All three hypotheses matter. On (0, 1], which is not closed, f(x) = 1/x is continuous and has no maximum."},{"t":"Bolzano in action: the bisection method","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#biseccion","x":"05 Bolzano in action: the bisection method Bolzano does not just assure you that the root exists: it tells you how to find it. If f changes sign on the interval, you split it in half and keep the half where the sign change persists. Each step halves the error. Lab · finding a root by halving f(x) = x³ − x − 2 on [1; 2] f(x) = cos x − x on [0; 1] Diode: 5 V − 1 kΩ·i − 0.026·ln(i/10⁻¹²), with i in mA Halve Ten steps Reset It is slow compared with other methods, but it never fails if there is a sign change, and the error is bounded in advance: after n steps it is less than (b − a)/2 n. Ten steps divide the initial interval by more than a thousand."},{"t":"Continuity in circuits","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#electronica","x":"06 Continuity in circuits Two continuities that physics imposes The voltage across a capacitor and the current through an inductor are continuous functions of time: they cannot jump. If the capacitor voltage jumped, the current i = C·dv/dt would be infinite; if the inductor current jumped, the voltage v = L·di/dt would be. That is the physical reason for the initial conditions used to solve any transient. What does jump. The voltage across a resistor, the current through a capacitor and the voltage across an inductor can change abruptly: they are the “non-continuous” variables of the circuit. Response speed. A real op amp cannot follow a vertical edge: its slew rate limits the slope. The output signal is still continuous, but with a ramp instead of the ideal jump. Schmitt trigger. Its transfer characteristic is deliberately discontinuous: it jumps between two levels, and with hysteresis, so that it does not oscillate in the presence of noise. Quantization. The output of an ADC is a staircase: a piecewise continuous function with jumps of one least significant bit. That jump is, precisely, the quantization error studied in A/D and D/A converters."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · Finding the value that makes it continuous Given f(x) = (x² − 4)/(x − 2) for x ≠ 2 and f(2) = k, find the k that makes it continuous. Repeat with a piecewise function where the parameter is inside one of the branches. Exercise 2 · The operating point of a diode, by bisection Program the bisection in a spreadsheet or in C to solve the equation of the diode circuit from section 5 —a 5 V source, 1 kΩ in series and the diode with its exponential equation—, with a tolerance of 1 µA. Compare with the value given by a simulation and with the simplified 0.7 V model. Exercise 3 · Seeing continuity on the oscilloscope In an RC circuit, trigger a step and observe on two channels the capacitor voltage and the current. Check that the voltage does not jump and that the current does. Repeat with an inductor, swapping the roles."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Checking only the limit and forgetting that f(a) has to exist and coincide with it. Calling a jump removable. If the one-sided limits differ, no redefinition can fix it. Applying Bolzano without a sign change, or on an interval where the function is not continuous. Using Weierstrass on an open interval. The hypotheses of the theorem are not decorative. Assuming an inductor’s current can be cut off abruptly: that is where the arc across a relay contact comes from, and why the flyback diode is fitted."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What kind of discontinuity does f(x) = (x² − 1)/(x − 1) have at x = 1? Removable: the limit exists and equals 2; it is enough to define f(1) = 2. And f(x) = 1/(x − 3) at x = 3? Infinite: the one-sided limits tend to −∞ and +∞. For what k is f(x) = 3x + k if x ≤ 2, and x² if x > 2, continuous? Equating the one-sided limits at 2: 6 + k = 4, so k = −2. How many bisection steps are needed to shrink an interval of length 1 to less than 0.001? 2 n > 1000 → n = 10, because 2 10 = 1024. Why can’t the voltage across a capacitor jump? Because i = C·dv/dt: a jump would imply an infinite derivative and therefore infinite current, which no real source can deliver. A signal is −2 V at t = 0 and +3 V at t = 1 ms, and the circuit is continuous. Did it pass through zero? Yes: by Bolzano’s theorem, at some instant between the two it was exactly 0 V."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Continuous functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Continuity, types of discontinuity and the intermediate value theorem with applied examples. Michael Spivak. Calculus. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Calculus”), Reverté, 2012. Proves Bolzano, the intermediate value theorem and Weierstrass from the completeness of the reals. Richard L. Burden and J. Douglas Faires. Numerical Analysis. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Análisis numérico”), Cengage Learning, 2011. Chapter 2 compares bisection, fixed-point iteration and Newton–Raphson, with their error bounds."},{"t":"Network functions","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#funciones","x":"01 Network functions A network function is the ratio of two transformed quantities of a circuit with zero initial conditions. If both are taken at the same pair of terminals, it is a driving-point function: an impedance \\( Z(s) \\) or an admittance \\( Y(s) \\). If they are taken at different points, it is a transfer function: a voltage gain, a transimpedance. For circuits of lumped components, any of them is a ratio of polynomials in \\( s \\) with real coefficients, and is determined, up to a constant, by its poles and zeros. Analysis starts from the circuit and obtains the function; synthesis takes the reverse path: given the desired function, it finds a circuit that realizes it. It is the basis of filter design."},{"t":"What functions a passive circuit can have","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#propiedades","x":"02 What functions a passive circuit can have Not every ratio of polynomials is the impedance of a circuit of resistors, capacitors and inductors. It has to be a positive real function: real for real \\( s \\), and with a nonnegative real part throughout the right half-plane. That has consequences that can be checked at a glance: The poles and zeros are in the left half-plane or on the imaginary axis. Those on the imaginary axis are simple and have real, positive residues. The degrees of the numerator and the denominator differ by at most one. On the \\( j\\omega \\) axis, the real part (the resistance) is never negative: a passive circuit does not deliver energy. For a transfer function the requirement is milder: it is enough for it to be stable, with the poles in the left half-plane; the zeros can be anywhere."},{"t":"LC networks: Foster’s reactance theorem","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#lc","x":"03 LC networks: Foster’s reactance theorem A network of only inductors and capacitors does not dissipate energy, so its impedance is purely imaginary on the \\( j\\omega \\) axis: \\( Z(j\\omega) = jX(\\omega) \\). Foster proved that the reactance of any LC network always increases with frequency, and that therefore its poles and zeros alternate along the imaginary axis, starting and ending at zero or at infinity: \\[ Z(s) = H\\,\\frac{(s^2 + \\omega_{z1}^2)(s^2 + \\omega_{z2}^2)\\cdots}{s\\,(s^2 + \\omega_{p1}^2)(s^2 + \\omega_{p2}^2)\\cdots} \\] With \\( 0 < \\omega_{z1} < \\omega_{p1} < \\omega_{z2} < \\cdots \\). It is an odd function of \\( s \\): the ratio of an even polynomial and an odd one. Any function of this form with alternating poles and zeros can be realized with inductors and capacitors."},{"t":"Four canonical forms","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#sintesis","x":"04 Four canonical forms The same LC impedance can be realized in four ways with the minimum number of elements: Form Method Circuit Foster I Partial fractions of \\( Z(s) \\) Series connection of an inductor, a capacitor and parallel LC tank circuits, one for each finite pole Foster II Partial fractions of \\( Y(s) \\) Parallel connection of an inductor, a capacitor and series LC branches Cauer I Continued fraction removing poles at infinity Ladder of series inductors and shunt capacitors Cauer II Continued fraction removing poles at the origin Ladder of series capacitors and shunt inductors The Cauer continued fraction is obtained by dividing: the quotient is a series inductor; the remainder is inverted, divided again, and the new quotient is a shunt capacitor; and so on until no remainder is left: \\[ Z(s) = sL_1 + \\cfrac{1}{sC_2 + \\cfrac{1}{sL_3 + \\cfrac{1}{sC_4 + \\cdots}}} \\] The Cauer ladder is the structure of almost all LC lowpass filters: series inductors and capacitors to ground. Lab · synthesizing an LC impedance You choose an LC impedance (with normalized values: henry, farad and rad/s). The chart shows its reactance on the \\( j\\omega \\) axis, with the zeros (dots) and the poles (dashed lines); below, the elements of the Foster I and Cauer I forms, which give the same impedance. Z(s) = (s³ + 3s) / (s² + 2) Z(s) = (s⁴ + 4s² + 3) / (s³ + 2s) Z(s) = (s⁶ + 9s⁴ + 23s² + 15) / (s⁵ + 6s³ + 8s)"},{"t":"Beyond LC networks","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#otras","x":"05 Beyond LC networks RC networks have analogous properties, with poles and zeros alternating along the negative real axis, and are synthesized with the same methods. Transfer functions are realized with doubly terminated ladders, between the generator resistance and the load resistance, which is exactly what the passive filters of analog filters do. Today synthesis is done by design software, but its results can only be understood with these methods."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying Foster With an impedance analyzer, measure the reactance of a four-element LC network between 1 kHz and 100 kHz and locate its poles and zeros. Verify that they alternate. Activity 2 · Two networks, one impedance Build the Foster I and Cauer I forms of the same impedance, denormalized to 10 kHz and 1 kΩ, and compare their measurements. Activity 3 · Synthesis by hand Synthesize in Cauer II form the impedance \\( (s^2 + 1)(s^2 + 3)/(s(s^2 + 2)) \\) and verify the result."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Trying to synthesize a function that is not positive real. The passive circuit does not exist. Confusing Cauer I with Cauer II. The first removes poles at infinity; the second, at the origin. Forgetting to denormalize. The synthesis values are in henries and farads for 1 rad/s and 1 Ω. Expecting the reactance of an LC network to decrease somewhere. By Foster, it always increases."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Can the function (s² + 2)/(s(s² + 1)) be the impedance of an LC network? No: the zero is at √2 and the pole at 1, and with the pole at the origin the sequence would be pole, pole, zero. They do not alternate. What are the Foster I elements of Z(s) = s + 1/s? A 1 H inductor in series with a 1 F capacitor. How is a 1 H inductor denormalized for 1 kΩ and 10 kHz? \\( L = 1 \\cdot 1000 / (2\\pi \\cdot 10^4) \\approx 15.9 \\) mH. How many elements does the minimum realization of a degree-4 LC impedance have? Four, equal to the higher degree of the numerator and denominator."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Mac E. Van Valkenburg. Introduction to Modern Network Synthesis. Wiley, 1960. Positive real functions and Foster and Cauer synthesis, clearly explained. Gabor C. Temes and Jack W. LaPatra. Introduction to Circuit Synthesis and Design. McGraw-Hill, 1977. Synthesis applied to the design of passive and active filters. Ernst A. Guillemin. Synthesis of Passive Networks. Wiley, 1957. The classic treatise on network synthesis."},{"t":"One input, exactly one output","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#que-es","x":"01 One input, exactly one output A function of one real variable is a rule that assigns to each number in a starting set one and only one number in a target set. That uniqueness is the whole definition, and it is what makes functions useful in engineering: if a circuit is a function of its input, then for each input there is a well-determined output. Name What it is Example with \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 1} \\) Domain The admitted input values [1, +∞): there is no real root of a negative number Codomain The set where the outputs land, by convention R R Range The values the function actually reaches [0, +∞) Graph The pairs (x, f(x)) drawn in the plane Half a parabola lying on its side, with its vertex at (1, 0) The vertical line test A curve in the plane is the graph of a function if and only if no vertical line crosses it more than once. That is why the circle x² + y² = 1 is not a function: for x = 0 there would be two values, 1 and −1. It is split into two functions, the upper semicircle and the lower one."},{"t":"Finding the domain","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#dominio","x":"02 Finding the domain When a function is given by its formula, the domain is the largest set where that formula makes sense. Three prohibitions cover almost every case: Do not divide by zero: the denominator cannot vanish. Do not take an even root of a negative number. Do not take the logarithm of zero or of a negative number. Worked example For f(x) = ln(x + 2) / √(4 − x²): we need x + 2 > 0, that is x > −2; and 4 − x² > 0 —strict, because it is also in the denominator—, that is −2 < x < 2. Intersecting: Dom f = (−2, 2). In an engineering problem the domain is usually narrowed even further for physical reasons: a resistance cannot be negative, nor can a frequency, and time is measured from the moment the switch is closed."},{"t":"The families you have to recognize by heart","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#familias","x":"03 The families you have to recognize by heart Family Form Feature Where it appears in electronics Polynomial a n x n + … + a₀ Continuous on all of R Approximations, calibration-curve fits Rational Quotient of polynomials Asymptotes where the denominator vanishes Transfer functions: zeros and poles Exponential a x, e x Grows faster than any power Charging a capacitor, diode current Logarithmic log a x Inverse of the exponential The decibel, Bode scales Trigonometric sin x, cos x, tan x Periodic All of alternating current Piecewise-defined A different formula in each region Can have jumps Amplifier saturation, the step, rectification"},{"t":"Properties you can read off the graph","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#propiedades","x":"04 Properties you can read off the graph Parity Even if f(−x) = f(x): symmetric about the y-axis, like x² or the cosine. Odd if f(−x) = −f(x): symmetric about the origin, like x³ or the sine. It saves half the work, and in Fourier analysis it decides which harmonics appear. Monotonicity Increasing if a larger input gives a larger output. A strictly monotonic function is automatically one-to-one, and therefore invertible on its range. Boundedness It is bounded if its graph fits between two horizontal lines. A real amplifier is always bounded: this is called saturation. Periodicity f(x + T) = f(x) for all x. The smallest positive T is the period, and its reciprocal is the frequency. A function is one-to-one (injective) if different inputs give different outputs —no horizontal line crosses the graph twice—, onto (surjective) if it reaches the whole codomain, and bijective if it is both. Only bijective functions have an inverse."},{"t":"Combining functions","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#operaciones","x":"05 Combining functions Functions are added, subtracted, multiplied and divided pointwise. And they can also be composed, which is the operation that is specific to them: applying one to the result of the other. \\[ (g \\circ f) (x) = g (f (x)) \\quad (f^{-1} \\circ f) (x) = x \\] Composition is not commutative: the inner function is applied first. The inverse is the one that undoes, and its graph is that of f mirrored about the line y = x. Two circuit stages in cascade are a composition of functions; that is why order matters. And the inverse appears whenever you need to solve for something: from the decibel back to the power ratio, from a sensor reading to the actual temperature."},{"t":"Moving and deforming a graph","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#transformaciones","x":"06 Moving and deforming a graph Expression Effect on the graph In a signal f(x) + k Vertical shift DC component, offset f(x − a) Horizontal shift to the right Delay \\( A \\cdot f(x) \\) Vertical stretch Gain \\( f(k \\cdot x) \\) Horizontal compression if k > 1 Frequency change \\( -f(x) \\) and \\( f(-x) \\) Reflection about the x-axis · about the y-axis Phase inversion · time reversal A general sinusoid, v(t) = V₀ + A·sin(ωt + φ), is the sine function with all four transformations applied at once. Recognizing them saves you from studying each signal from scratch. Lab · function explorer Choose a function and move the four parameters of y = A · f(k(x − a)) + c. The dotted curve is the inverse, mirrored about y = x. f(x) = x² f(x) = x³ f(x) = √x f(x) = 1/x f(x) = eˣ f(x) = ln x f(x) = sin x f(x) = |x| f(x) = saturation (real amplifier) Show the inverse A 1 k 1 a 0 c 0"},{"t":"The static transfer function","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#electronica","x":"07 The static transfer function The most direct way a function appears in electronics is the transfer characteristic: the output as a function of the input, measured point by point at DC. Ideal amplifier: v o = A·v i, a straight line through the origin. It is linear in the strict sense of linear algebra. Real amplifier: the same line, but bounded by the supply voltages. It is a piecewise-defined function, and the flat region is saturation. Outside the straight segment superposition no longer holds. Diode: i = I S (e v/nV T − 1), a pure exponential. Its being so abrupt is what makes it usable as a switch. NTC thermistor: a resistance that decays exponentially with temperature; you have to invert the function to read the temperature from the measured resistance. Decibel: a logarithmic function of the power ratio, chosen precisely because it turns products into sums."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Exercise 1 · Measuring a transfer characteristic With a variable power supply and a multimeter, measure the output of an op amp in a non-inverting configuration for inputs from −3 to 3 V, in steps of 0.25 V. Plot the points, identify the linear segment, measure its slope (the gain) and locate where saturation begins. You can try it out beforehand in the operational amplifiers course. Exercise 2 · Domains with physical meaning Write the function that gives the current of a resistive divider as a function of R₂, state its mathematical domain and then its actual physical domain. Explain the difference. Exercise 3 · Inverting a sensor curve Using the table for an NTC thermistor, fit R(T) and solve for T(R). Check that the composition of the two returns the original value, and discuss over what range the inversion is reliable."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Confusing range with codomain. The range is what is actually reached. Forgetting that an even root requires a radicand ≥ 0, and > 0 if it is also in a denominator. Writing f −1 (x) for 1/f(x). They are different things: one is the inverse, the other the reciprocal. Composing in the wrong order: in g∘f, f is applied first. Inverting a function that is not one-to-one without first restricting the domain. Using an amplifier’s gain outside its linear region."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Domain of f(x) = √(x² − 9). x² − 9 ≥ 0 holds for |x| ≥ 3: (−∞, −3] ∪ [3, +∞). Is f(x) = x³ − x even, odd or neither? f(−x) = −x³ + x = −f(x): it is odd. If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², what are (g∘f)(2) and (f∘g)(2)? (g∘f)(2) = g(5) = 25 and (f∘g)(2) = f(4) = 9. The order changes the result. What is the inverse of f(x) = 3x − 6? y = 3x − 6 → x = (y + 6)/3, so f −1 (x) = (x + 6)/3. Why does a saturated amplifier stop being a linear function? Because in the flat region the output does not follow the input: doubling the input no longer doubles the output and superposition stops holding. What graph transformation represents a 2 ms delay in a signal? A horizontal shift: v(t − 0.002)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Hebe Rabuffetti. Introducción al análisis matemático (Cálculo 1) (in Spanish). El Ateneo. An Argentine classic, written for the first year of engineering: functions, domains and graphs in great detail. James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. The most widely used today: an excellent treatment of functions, transformations and models. Manuel Sadosky and Rebeca Ch. de Guber. Elementos de cálculo diferencial e integral (in Spanish). Alsina. Another Argentine classic, rigorous and sparing with words."},{"t":"One output, several inputs","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#que-es","x":"01 One output, several inputs The temperature of a plate depends on where it is measured: T(x, y). The power dissipated by a resistor depends on the voltage and the resistance: P(V, R). The gain of a stage depends on two components. All of these are scalar functions of several variables: two or more numbers go in, one comes out. \\[ f: D \\subseteq R^n \\to R \\quad z = f (x, y) \\] With two variables the graph is a surface in space: the height z above each point (x, y) of the plane. The domain is no longer an interval but a region of the plane: the inside of a circle, a half-plane, the plane minus a line. The restrictions are the same as always —no dividing by zero, no even roots of negative numbers, no logarithms of non-positive numbers—, only now they define a zone. Example For f(x, y) = √(9 − x² − y²), we need 9 − x² − y² ≥ 0, that is, x² + y² ≤ 9: the domain is the closed disk of radius 3. The surface is the upper hemisphere of radius 3."},{"t":"Level curves: the map of the surface","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#nivel","x":"02 Level curves: the map of the surface Drawing surfaces on paper is hard; that is why they are sliced. A level curve is the set of points where f takes a constant value: f(x, y) = k. It is exactly what a topographic map does with the height of the terrain. On the map On the surface In physics Curves very close together Steep slope Strong field: the gradient is large Curves far apart Nearly flat area Weak field Closed curves that shrink A peak or a pit Local maximum or minimum X-shaped curves A saddle point Unstable equilibrium The level curves of an electric potential are the equipotentials, and the field lines cross them perpendicularly. That perpendicularity is no coincidence: it is proved in the differential calculus of several variables, when the gradient appears. Lab · level map The color is the value of the function and the lines are the level curves. Tap the map to read the value at a point. f = x² + y² · paraboloid f = x² − y² · saddle f = 3·e^(−x²−y²) · bell f = sin x · cos y Potential of two opposite charges Dissipated power P = V²/R Level curves"},{"t":"The surfaces worth recognizing","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#superficies","x":"03 The surfaces worth recognizing Equation Surface Level curves \\( z = ax + by + c \\) Plane Parallel lines \\( z = x^{2} + y^{2} \\) Paraboloid (a bowl) Concentric circles \\( z = x^{2} - y^{2} \\) Hyperbolic paraboloid: the saddle Hyperbolas \\( x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = R^{2} \\) Sphere Circles \\( x^{2} + y^{2} = R^{2} \\) Cylinder (z free) One circle repeated These are the same quadrics that appeared in quadratic forms, and their classification again depends on the signs of the eigenvalues. With three variables a graph is no longer possible: we work with level surfaces, f(x, y, z) = k, which for an electric potential are the equipotential surfaces."},{"t":"Limits: approaching from every side","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#limites","x":"04 Limits: approaching from every side Here is the most important difference from Calculus I. In one variable, a point is approached from the left or from the right: two paths. In two variables there are infinitely many paths, and the limit exists only if all of them give the same result. The example to keep at hand For f(x, y) = xy/(x² + y²) at the origin: approaching along the x axis (y = 0) the value is always 0; approaching along the line y = x it becomes x²/(2x²) = 1/2. Two paths, two results: the limit does not exist, even though both iterated limits are 0. Finding two paths with different results is the standard way to prove that a limit does not exist; to prove that it does exist you have to bound the function, for example by switching to polar coordinates. In polar coordinates, with x = ρcos θ and y = ρsin θ, the limit as (x, y) → (0, 0) becomes a limit as ρ → 0. If the result depends on θ, there is no limit; if it does not and the bound is uniform, there is. Continuity is defined as before: f is continuous at a point if the limit exists and equals the value. And the theorems that matter still hold: sums, products, quotients and compositions of continuous functions are continuous, and a continuous function on a closed, bounded region attains a maximum and a minimum."},{"t":"Where they appear in electronics","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#aplicaciones","x":"05 Where they appear in electronics Electric potential. V(x, y, z) is a scalar function of three variables; its level surfaces are the equipotentials and its gradient, with the sign changed, is the electric field. Thermal maps. The temperature of a circuit board is T(x, y); the level curves show where the hot spot is, which is what a thermal imaging camera sees. Response surfaces. The gain of a stage as a function of two resistors, or the efficiency of a power supply as a function of output voltage and current: optimizing means looking for the maximum of a surface. Characteristic curves. The collector current of a transistor depends on two variables, I C (V CE, I B ): the family of curves in the datasheet is really a slice of a surface. See bipolar transistors."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Domains Determine and sketch the domain of f(x,y) = ln(x + y), of g(x,y) = 1/√(4 − x² − y²) and of h(x,y) = arcsin(x + y). In each case, state whether it is open, closed and bounded. Exercise 2 · A real thermal map Using an infrared thermometer, measure the temperature of a board with one component dissipating power, on a 5 × 5 grid of points, and draw the level curves by hand. Compare with what the lab shows for the Gaussian bell. Exercise 3 · A limit that does not exist For f(x,y) = (x²y)/(x⁴ + y²), compute the limit at the origin along the lines y = mx and then along the parabola y = x². Check that along every line it gives 0 and along the parabola it gives 1/2: lines are not enough."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Concluding that the limit exists because it agrees along several lines. All paths are needed, and lines are only a few of them. Confusing iterated limits with the double limit: both iterated limits can exist, be equal, and the limit can still fail to exist. Describing the domain with an equation instead of a region. Drawing level curves without indicating which value each one corresponds to. Forgetting that level curves never cross: if they did, a point would have two values."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Domain of f(x, y) = ln(9 − x² − y²). 9 − x² − y² > 0, that is, x² + y² < 9: the inside of the circle of radius 3, without the boundary. What are the level curves of z = x² + y²? Concentric circles centered at the origin: for z = k, of radius √k, with k ≥ 0. Does the limit of xy/(x² + y²) exist at the origin? No: along the x axis it gives 0 and along the line y = x it gives 1/2. What does a level curve of the electric potential represent physically? An equipotential: moving a charge along it costs no work, because the field is perpendicular to the curve. If the level curves are very close together, what happens to the function? It varies quickly in that zone: the slope —and the magnitude of the gradient— is large."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Scalar functions of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading James Stewart. Multivariable Calculus. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de varias variables. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 14 opens with domains, level curves and limits, with many surfaces drawn out. Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. More rigorous on limits in several variables and on the topology of the domain. Claudio Pita Ruiz. Cálculo vectorial. Prentice Hall (in Spanish). Written in Spanish and very detailed in its examples of limits that do not exist."},{"t":"A function that returns a vector","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#que-es","x":"01 A function that returns a vector So far, functions have taken a number and returned a number. A vector-valued function of one real variable takes a number —typically time— and returns a vector: the position of something that is moving. \\[ \\vec{r} (t) = (x (t), y (t), z (t)) = x (t) \\hat{i} + y (t) \\hat{j} + z (t) \\hat{k} \\] Each component is an ordinary function. As t varies, the tip of the vector traces a curve, and t is its parameter. Curve Vector equation Where it appears Line \\( r(t) = r_{0} + t\\cdot d \\) The one from line and plane, now read as a motion Circle (R cos t, R sin t) A rotating phasor: the representation of alternating current Helix \\( (R \\cos t, R \\operatorname{sin} t, c\\cdot t) \\) Path of an electron in a magnetic field; a winding Lissajous \\( (A \\operatorname{sin} (at), B \\operatorname{sin} (bt + \\varphi )) \\) What an oscilloscope draws in XY mode"},{"t":"Differentiating: everything component by component","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#derivada","x":"02 Differentiating: everything component by component The limit, the continuity and the derivative of a vector-valued function are computed in each component separately. That means everything learned in Calculus I still holds: \\[ {\\vec{r}}' (t) = (x' (t), y' (t), z' (t)) \\quad \\vec{v} = {\\vec{r}}' \\quad \\vec{a} = {\\vec{r}}^{''} \\] The derivative is a vector tangent to the curve and points in the direction of travel. Its magnitude, |r′(t)|, is the speed. The differentiation rules carry over with one important caveat: there are two products. For the scalar one, (u · v)′ = u′ · v + u · v′; for the vector one, (u × v)′ = u′ × v + u × v′, and here the order cannot be changed, because the cross product is not commutative. A result that is used all the time If |r(t)| is constant —the moving point travels on a sphere or a circle—, then r · r′ = 0: the velocity is perpendicular to the position. This is what explains why, in uniform circular motion, the acceleration points to the center, and why an electron in a magnetic field turns without changing its speed: the force is always perpendicular to the velocity and does no work."},{"t":"Arc length","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#longitud","x":"03 Arc length \\[ L = \\int_a^b \\left| {\\vec{r}}' (t) \\right| d t = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{{\\left(x' \\right)}^2 + {\\left(y' \\right)}^2 + {\\left(z' \\right)}^2} d t \\] It is “speed times time,” summed up: exactly the same idea used to compute the distance covered on a trip. When the curve is parametrized by the distance traveled along it, the arc-length parameter s, the speed equals 1 and the formulas simplify. This is the “natural” parametrization: traveling along the curve at unit constant speed."},{"t":"How a path bends","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#frenet","x":"04 How a path bends At each point of the curve, three mutually perpendicular vectors are defined, the Frenet frame: Unit tangent T = r′/|r′|: where it is heading. Principal normal N = T′/|T′|: where it bends toward, always pointing to the concave side. Binormal B = T × N: perpendicular to the plane in which the curve is bending. \\[ \\kappa = \\frac{\\left| {\\vec{r}}' \\times {\\vec{r}}^{''} \\right|}{{\\left| {\\vec{r}}' \\right|}^3} \\quad \\rho = \\frac{1}{\\kappa} \\quad \\vec{a} = \\frac{d | \\vec{v} |}{d t} \\hat{T} + \\kappa {\\left| \\vec{v} \\right|}^2 \\hat{N} \\] κ is the curvature and ρ = 1/κ the radius of the circle that best fits the curve at that point. The acceleration splits in two: the part that changes the speed and the part that changes the direction. Lab · travel along a curve You move the parameter and see the tangent vector, the normal vector, the speed and the circle that best fits the curve at that point. Circle: (3cos t, 3sin t) Ellipse: (3cos t, 1.5sin t) Lissajous 3:2 Cycloid Spiral Helix seen from above t 0.8 ▶ Travel Osculating circle"},{"t":"Where they appear in electronics and physics","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#aplicaciones","x":"05 Where they appear in electronics and physics The phasor. A sinusoidal voltage is represented by r(t) = V(cos ωt, sin ωt): a vector-valued function whose magnitude is constant and whose derivative, perpendicular to it, leads by 90°. That perpendicularity is the phase shift between voltage and current in a capacitor. It is seen in the vector study of alternating current. Charge in a magnetic field. With a velocity not parallel to the field, the path is a helix: a circle in the plane perpendicular to B and uniform advance along B. This is the principle of the cyclotron and of cathode-ray tubes. Lissajous figures. Two signals on the X and Y axes of the oscilloscope form a parametric curve; the frequency ratio determines the figure and the phase shift determines its tilt. Robotics and CNC. The path of a tool is r(t), and the controller needs bounded velocity and acceleration: that is why corners are rounded, since an infinite curvature would require infinite acceleration."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Lissajous on the oscilloscope With two generators in XY mode, obtain the figures for 1:1, 1:2 and 3:2 ratios, and measure the phase shift from the tilt of the ellipse. Write the vector-valued function of each figure. You can try it first in the instrument simulator. Exercise 2 · Length of a path Compute the length of one arc of a helix of radius 5 cm and pitch 2 cm, for one complete turn, and check it by measuring the wire needed to wind it. Exercise 3 · Acceleration in two components For a moving point that travels along a curve with increasing speed, compute the tangential and normal components of the acceleration at three points and verify that their vector sum equals r″."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing the curve with the function: the same curve admits infinitely many parametrizations, which traverse it at different speeds. Differentiating the magnitude instead of the vector: |r|′ is not |r′|. Changing the order in (u × v)′. Forgetting to normalize when computing T and N. Believing that zero acceleration implies constant velocity in direction: in uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the acceleration is not zero."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment For r(t) = (cos t, sin t, t), find v and its magnitude. v = (−sin t, cos t, 1) and |v| = √(sin² + cos² + 1) = √2, constant: the helix is traversed at uniform speed. What is the length of one turn of that helix? L = ∫₀ 2π √2 dt = 2π√2 ≈ 8.89. Why is r · v = 0 in uniform circular motion? Because |r| is constant: differentiating r · r = const gives 2 r · r′ = 0. What does curvature measure? How much the curve bends per unit of length. Its reciprocal is the radius of the osculating circle: line → κ = 0; circle of radius R → κ = 1/R. A moving point speeds up while turning. How is its acceleration divided? Into a tangential component, d|v|/dt, which changes the speed, and a normal one, κ|v|², which changes the direction."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading James Stewart. Multivariable Calculus. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de varias variables. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 13 develops vector functions, arc length and curvature with good figures. Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. The reference text for the course, more demanding and with excellent physical applications. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. The motion of charges in magnetic fields, which is the central physical example of this topic."},{"t":"From the DDS to the phase-locked loop","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#sintesis","x":"01 From the DDS to the phase-locked loop Direct digital synthesis in the signal sources of Measurements I gives very fine resolution, but its output cannot exceed a fraction of the clock. To generate signals from hundreds of megahertz to several gigahertz with the accuracy of a crystal, RF generators use a phase-locked loop (PLL): a voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) whose frequency, divided by \\( N \\), is compared with a stable reference. The loop corrects the VCO until the two coincide: \\[ f_{out} = N \\cdot f_{PFD} \\] \\[ f_{PFD} = \\frac{f_{ref}}{R} \\] \\( f_{PFD} \\) is the frequency at which the phase-frequency detector operates. In an integer-N synthesizer, the output frequency step is \\( f_{PFD} \\): for 25 kHz channels, the comparison has to be at 25 kHz."},{"t":"The building blocks of the synthesizer","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#bloques","x":"02 The building blocks of the synthesizer Block Function Reference A temperature-compensated (TCXO) or oven-controlled (OCXO) crystal oscillator, sometimes locked to GPS R divider Lowers the reference to the comparison frequency Phase-frequency detector with charge pump Delivers current pulses proportional to the phase error Loop filter Integrates the pulses and sets the loop bandwidth and its stability VCO Generates the output; its frequency depends on the control voltage N divider Brings the output down to the comparison frequency The PLL is a feedback system, and its bandwidth and phase margin are designed like those of a feedback amplifier. For stability, the loop bandwidth is kept below one tenth of \\( f_{PFD} \\): comparing at a low frequency forces a slow loop, which takes a long time to lock when the channel changes."},{"t":"Phase noise","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#ruido","x":"03 Phase noise A real generator does not produce a perfect spectral line: its phase fluctuates, and the spectrum has \"skirts\" on both sides of the carrier. Phase noise is specified in dBc/Hz at an offset from the carrier. Within the loop bandwidth, the noise of the reference and of the detector is multiplied by \\( N \\), that is, it rises \\( 20 \\log N \\); outside it, the VCO noise dominates. The in-band noise is estimated with the detector’s figure of merit, about −220 dBc/Hz in current circuits: \\[ \\mathcal{L}_{band} \\approx FOM + 10\\log f_{PFD} + 20 \\log N \\] For the same output frequency, \\( N f_{PFD} \\) is fixed: doubling \\( f_{PFD} \\) adds 3 dB through the second term and subtracts 6 dB through the third. It pays to compare at the highest possible frequency. That is the reason for fractional-N synthesizers: the divider alternates between \\( N \\) and \\( N+1 \\) with a sigma–delta modulator, so that the average is fractional. The comparison is made at tens of megahertz and a step of a few hertz is obtained. The price is the fractional spurs, lines close to the carrier that the modulator and the loop filter have to keep low. Lab · integer-N or fractional-N synthesizer A synthesizer for the 2.4 GHz band with a figure of merit of −220 dBc/Hz. In integer-N, the comparison frequency is the channel step; in fractional-N, 50 MHz. The bars compare the in-band phase noise of the two architectures. Output frequency = 25 kHz channel step 200 kHz step 1 MHz step"},{"t":"How to read the datasheet of a synthesized generator","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#especificaciones","x":"04 How to read the datasheet of a synthesized generator Frequency range and resolution, and the accuracy, which is that of the internal or external reference. Phase noise at 10 kHz or 20 kHz from the carrier, the figure that separates a laboratory generator from a high-purity one. Spurs and harmonics, in dBc. Switching time between frequencies, which depends on the loop bandwidth. Output level and its accuracy, and the available modulations: AM, FM, pulse, I/Q."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Phase noise Use a spectrum analyzer to measure the phase noise of a synthesized generator at 1 kHz, 10 kHz and 100 kHz from the carrier, correcting for the resolution bandwidth. Activity 2 · An integrated PLL Program an integrated PLL synthesizer with a microcontroller, in integer-N and in fractional-N, and compare the output spectrum and the spurs. Activity 3 · External reference Lock the laboratory’s generator and counter to the same 10 MHz reference and observe how the reading difference disappears."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Comparing at the channel step frequency without need. It raises the phase noise and makes the loop slow. Forgetting that the reference noise is multiplied. A noisy reference ruins a GHz synthesizer. Ignoring the fractional-N spurs. They can fall inside the adjacent channel. Reading the analyzer’s phase noise without correcting for its bandwidth. It is expressed per hertz."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What N is needed to synthesize 915 MHz comparing at 200 kHz? 4575. How much does the in-band phase noise rise when multiplying by 1000? \\( 20 \\log 1000 = 60 \\) dB. Why can a fractional synthesizer have a faster loop? Because it compares at a much higher frequency, and the loop bandwidth can be proportionally greater. What is the disadvantage of fractional-N? The fractional spurs close to the carrier."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Synthesized signal generators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Dean Banerjee. PLL Performance, Simulation, and Design. 5th ed., Dog Ear Publishing, 2017. The design of charge-pump synthesizers, with phase noise and spurs. Ulrich L. Rohde. Microwave and Wireless Synthesizers: Theory and Design. Wiley, 1997. The classic reference on radio-frequency synthesizers. Roland E. Best. Phase-Locked Loops: Design, Simulation, and Applications. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2007. Analog and digital PLLs from the standpoint of control theory."},{"t":"Measuring in order to manage","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#instrumentos","x":"01 Measuring in order to manage Environmental management needs tools to answer concrete questions: what impact a project will have before it is built, whether a plant meets what it promised, how much a product pollutes over its life. Each question has its instrument: Instrument Question it answers When it is used Environmental impact assessment What effects will this project have and how are they mitigated? Before approving a work or activity Environmental audit Are the law and the commitments being met? Periodically, during operation Management system (ISO 14001) How are environmental aspects controlled and improved on a continual basis? Permanently Life cycle assessment What is the impact of a product from extraction to waste? In product design and comparison Carbon footprint How many greenhouse gases does an organization or a product emit? To set targets and communicate"},{"t":"Environmental impact assessment","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#eia","x":"02 Environmental impact assessment The General Environmental Law, 25,675, requires a prior environmental impact assessment (EIA) for any work or activity that could significantly degrade the environment; the provinces set the procedure. The study describes the project and the environment, identifies the impacts, assesses them, proposes mitigation measures and a management plan, and goes through a stage of public participation before the authority decides. A classic tool for identifying impacts is the Leopold matrix, proposed in 1971 by the United States Geological Survey: it crosses the project’s actions with the factors of the environment and, in each affected cell, records the magnitude and the importance of the impact. Its value lies in forcing us to look at all the combinations, not only the obvious ones."},{"t":"Management systems and audits","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#iso14001","x":"03 Management systems and audits The ISO 14001 standard, in its 2015 version, organizes environmental management with the same cycle of continual improvement as ISO 45001: the organization identifies its environmental aspects (emissions, waste, consumption) and their impacts, considers the life cycle perspective, meets the legal requirements, sets objectives and controls them. Audits verify the system with evidence; third-party audits make it possible to certify it."},{"t":"Life cycle and carbon footprint","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#acv","x":"04 Life cycle and carbon footprint Life cycle assessment, standardized in ISO 14040 and 14044, adds up the impacts of all the stages: extraction of materials, manufacturing, transport, use and end of life. In electronic equipment the result depends strongly on use: in devices that are always on, the energy consumed dominates, and in those used briefly, manufacturing does, which includes that of chips and displays, very intensive in energy and materials. \\[ E_{\\mathrm{total}} = E_{\\mathrm{manuf}} + P\\, t_{\\mathrm{use}}\\, f_e + E_{\\mathrm{EoL}} \\] \\( P \\) is the average power, \\( t_{\\mathrm{use}} \\) the hours of use over the whole service life and \\( f_e \\) the emission factor of the electric grid, in kg of CO₂ equivalent per kWh. The factor for each country depends on its electricity mix: use the one published by the energy authority. The carbon footprint of an organization is calculated with the GHG Protocol or ISO 14064, in three scopes: direct emissions (scope 1), those from purchased electricity (scope 2) and the rest of the value chain, such as the manufacture of what is purchased and the use of what is sold (scope 3), which are usually the largest. Lab · the carbon footprint of a device and when to replace it The footprint of an electronic device over its service life, split between manufacturing and use, and the years it takes a new, more efficient device to offset the emissions of its own manufacturing. The initial values are an example; the manufacturing footprint is "},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Leopold matrix Build a reduced Leopold matrix for the installation of a board assembly plant in an industrial park, with ten actions and ten factors. Activity 2 · Real consumption Using a wattmeter, measure the consumption of the laboratory equipment when on, on standby and off, and estimate the annual footprint of the energy it consumes. Activity 3 · Environmental aspects List the environmental aspects of an equipment repair workshop, with their impacts, and propose a measurable objective for the most significant one."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Looking only at consumption in use. In many electronic devices manufacturing weighs more. Replacing working equipment \"for efficiency\". If use is low or the grid is clean, the manufacturing of the new one is never offset. Confusing the EIA with a bureaucratic formality. Its value lies in changing the project before it is built. Forgetting scope 3. It is usually the largest part of an electronics company’s footprint."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment A 30 W device switched on 8 hours per day: how much energy does it consume per year? 87.6 kWh. With a factor of 0.35 kg CO₂e/kWh, how much is that in emissions? About 30.7 kg per year. In which scope are the emissions from the electricity a company buys counted? In scope 2. What does the Leopold matrix cross? The project’s actions with the factors of the environment."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Vicente Conesa Fernández-Vítora. Guía metodológica para la evaluación del impacto ambiental (Methodological guide for environmental impact assessment) (in Spanish). 4th ed., Mundi-Prensa, 2010. The most widely used impact assessment method in Spanish, with matrices and valuation. Walter Klöpffer and Birgit Grahl. Life Cycle Assessment (LCA): A Guide to Best Practice. Wiley-VCH, 2014. Life cycle assessment according to ISO 14040 and 14044, step by step. World Resources Institute and WBCSD. The Greenhouse Gas Protocol: Corporate Standard. Revised edition, 2004. The reference standard for the carbon footprint of organizations and its scopes."},{"t":"Hazard and risk","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#peligro","x":"01 Hazard and risk A hazard is a source of possible harm: a 220 V voltage, a flammable solvent, a ladder. Risk combines the likelihood that the harm occurs with the severity of its consequences. The same hazard can give very different risks: 220 V in a closed panel with an RCD is not the same as 220 V in an open power supply on the bench. \\[ R = P \\times C \\] \\( P \\) is the probability or expected frequency and \\( C \\) the consequence. In practice, both are estimated on scales from 1 to 5 and the risk is placed in a matrix; with enough data, as in probability theory, they are calculated quantitatively."},{"t":"Identifying hazards","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#identificar","x":"02 Identifying hazards What has not been identified cannot be managed. The techniques range from the simplest to the most systematic: Technique How it works When to use it Checklists Predefined questions by type of hazard Routine inspections Job safety analysis Steps of the task with their hazards Manual and maintenance tasks What if...? A group proposes deviations and analyzes their effects New processes, changes HAZOP Guide words (more, less, no, reverse) applied to each process variable Continuous process plants FMEA Failure modes of each component, their effects and their detection Design of equipment and products The participation of workers is part of the method: the person who does the task knows deviations and shortcuts that do not appear in any drawing."},{"t":"Evaluating: the risk matrix","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#evaluar","x":"03 Evaluating: the risk matrix Each hazard is placed in a likelihood-by-consequence matrix, and the product is classified into levels that indicate what to do. A common classification for scales from 1 to 5: Value of R Level Action 1 to 4 Low Maintain the existing controls 5 to 9 Moderate Reduce when reasonable; plan 10 to 16 High Reduce as a priority before continuing, or with immediate measures 20 and 25 Intolerable Do not start or continue the work until it has been reduced The underlying criterion is the ALARP principle (as low as reasonably practicable): a risk is reduced as long as the cost of reducing it is not disproportionate to the benefit. Measures are chosen using the hierarchy of controls, and the risk that remains after applying them, the residual risk, is evaluated again. Lab · the risk matrix of an electronics laboratory Four hazards of a laboratory, with their estimated likelihood and consequence. For each one you choose the control; the hollow dot is the initial risk and the filled one the residual risk. The estimates are a reasoned example, not table values."},{"t":"A management system","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#gestion","x":"04 A management system Evaluating once is not enough: processes, people and equipment change. A management system turns prevention into a cycle of continual improvement: plan, do, check and act. The ISO 45001 standard organizes it with the common structure of ISO management standards: Context and leadership: management takes responsibility, and workers participate and are consulted. Planning: hazard identification, risk assessment, legal requirements and objectives. Support and operation: resources, competence, communication, operational controls and emergency preparedness. Evaluation and improvement: monitoring, internal audits, management review, incident investigation and corrective actions. The legal requirements of the legal framework enter the system as one more requirement: the standard does not replace them."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Matrix of the classroom-workshop Identify the hazards of the university laboratory with a checklist, estimate them in the matrix and propose controls for those that remain at a high level. Activity 2 · What if...? In a group, apply the technique to the charging of lithium batteries in the laboratory: what happens if one is short-circuited, if it is overcharged, if it is punctured, if no one is present. Activity 3 · FMEA of a device Do a simplified FMEA of a bench power supply, from the point of view of the safety of the person who uses it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing hazard with risk. The hazard is the source; the risk is how likely and how serious its harm is. Believing that the matrix gives an exact number. It orders and prioritizes; its values are estimates. Lowering the risk with PPE alone. It reduces the likelihood a little and does nothing to the potential severity of the hazard. Evaluating once and filing it away. Every change of process, equipment or staff requires evaluating again."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment A hazard has likelihood 3 and consequence 4. At what level does it fall? R = 12: high. What does ALARP mean? Reducing the risk as far as is reasonably practicable, until the cost is disproportionate to the benefit. Which technique would you use to analyze the hazards of a continuous chemical plant? HAZOP. Why does an engineering control usually lower the risk more than an administrative one? Because it acts on the hazard without depending on each person following a procedure every time."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Occupational risk management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading ISO. ISO 45001:2018. Occupational health and safety management systems. International Organization for Standardization; IRAM version in Spanish. The requirements of the management system and risk assessment. ISO. ISO 31000:2018. Risk management — Guidelines. International Organization for Standardization. The general principles of risk management, applicable to any organization. Trevor Kletz. HAZOP and HAZAN: Identifying and Assessing Process Industry Hazards. 4th ed., Institution of Chemical Engineers, 1999. The classic text on identifying and assessing process hazards."},{"t":"A pipe that carries waves","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#por-que","x":"01 A pipe that carries waves Above a few gigahertz, a coaxial cable starts to lose a lot: the center conductor is thin, the skin effect concentrates the current on its surface and the dielectric absorbs. To carry microwave power with low losses, a hollow metal tube is used, the waveguide. It has no center conductor: the wave travels through the air inside, bouncing between the walls. Unlike a cable, a waveguide does not let every frequency through. It only propagates the waves whose field configuration satisfies the boundary conditions —zero tangential electric field on the metal— and that is only possible above a minimum frequency."}]);
